{"case_id": "q002-batch", "row_id": "q002", "mode": "batch", "objective": "C. difficile Infection Episode: Receipt of SOC Antibacterial Therapy (Fidaxomicin, Vancomycin, Metronidazole) With Planned Duration 10–25 Days at Time of IMP Administration competing agents in the same mechanistic class as AZD5148, including Monoclonal Antibody targeting Clostridioides Difficile Toxin B TcdB", "query": ["AZD5148 competing anti-TcdB monoclonal antibodies Phase 2 2026", "AZD5148 TcdB antibody competitors licensing", "AZD5148 RePreve comparator anti-toxin antibody", "AZD5148 TcdB epitope binding mechanism", "AZD5148 clinical pipeline TcdB antibody 2026"], "anchors": ["AZD5148"], "search_latency_ms": 632.5, "target": "drugs competing with AZD5148: other agents in the same mechanistic class or for the same indication, as described in `objective`", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 581.0, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 11261, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000473, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 8, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.6624, "results": [{"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Monoclonal Antibodies Targeting Bacterial Infections: A Broad ...", "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s40259-026-00787-9?error=cookies_not_supported&code=d6acfad0-c39d-44cd-90d2-d4edff505376", "domain": "link.springer.com", "snippet": "Despite these advancements—and the approval of numerous mAbs for other indications—few anti-bacterial mAbs have been approved for clinical use, which is limited to obiltoxaximab (Anthim) and raxibacumab (Abthrax) for treatment of *Bacillus anthracis* toxin exposure, and bezlotoxumab (Zinplava^®^) for *Clostridioides difficile* infection of the gastrointestinal tract [11,12,13].\n...\nMedarex was the first to report anti-toxin mAbs, which were later licensed by Merck [23].\nTwo fully human IgG1κ mAbs, anti-TcdA (CDA1/actoxumab) and TcdB (CDB1/bezlotoxumab) were selected for in vitro potency, and efficacy in a lethal intoxication hamster model [24].\n...\nBezlotoxumab (Zinplava^®^) was approved as a single intravenous infusion of 10 mg/kg in the USA and European Union for prevention of rCDI in individuals at high risk for CDI and receiving antibacterial treatment [23].\n...\nAstraZeneca is developing another anti-TcdB mAb, having licensed two anti-toxin mAbs from Progenics (both IgG1κ) [31].\nThe anti-TcdB mAb, AZD5148, was engineered with a YTE substitution, a triple-mutation M252Y/S254T/T256E in the Fc region that extends the mAb’s half-life in human sera [32, 33].\nAZD5148 (formerly PA41) binds the GTD of TcdB and prevents the delivery of TcdB enzymatic cargo into the host cell cytosol [34].\n...\nIn contrast, the strategy employed by AZD5148, focused on binding the GTD, is independent of the receptor expressed on the target cell surface and may offer broader neutralizing activity.\nA recent study on 9134 global isolates showed that the AZD5148 epitope was highly conserved (99.58%) when compared to Zinplava^®^ (82.47%).\nAZD5148 also exhibited broad neutralization in vitro against eight recent circulating TcdB ribotypes [33].\n...\nAZD5148 is currently being evaluated for efficacy and safety for the prevention of recurrence of CDI in individuals 18 years of age and above in the PRISM study (NCT07285213) [40, 41].", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-15", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.96, "evidence": 0.69}, "jev_score": 0.6624, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Immune aspects of Clostridioides difficile infection and vaccine ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12767691/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "This is supported by clinical evidence that: (1) hospital outbreaks have been caused by TcdA-negative and TcdB-positive *C. difficile* strains^7,8^; (2) higher antibody titers against TcdB (but not TcdA) correlate with a lower risk of recurrent infection^7,9^; and (3) neutralizing TcdB with bezlotoxumab reduces the risk of recurrent infections and adverse outcomes, while treatment with an anti-TcdA neutralizing mAb does not^10^.\n...\n|GSK|GSK2904545A|TcdA, TcdB DD and CROPS (VPI 10463)|Recombinant fusion protein (F2 antigen)|AS01|N/A|2024, Phase 1|Ongoing|\n...\nBezlotoxumab binds to two epitopes in the TcdB CROPS, located near the ‘hinge’ region (Figure 2B).\n...\nAnother well-characterized neutralizing mAb, PA41 (now called AZD5148), binds to a single site on the GTD (Figure 2D), which allows TcdB uptake, but prevents the formation of the toxin pore and GTD translocation into the cytosol^89^.\n...\n- The neutralizing mAb Zinplava^™^ (bezlotoxumab) was discontinued in January 2025; however, a promising antibody candidate, AZD5148 (formerly PA41), is currently in early clinical trials (NCT06469151).", "date": "2025-08-12", "last_updated": "2026-06-09", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.94, "evidence": 0.7}, "jev_score": 0.658, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Epitope Conservation of AZD5148, a Broadly Neutralizing Anti-Toxin B Monoclonal Antibody, Among Diverse and Global Contemporary Clostridioides difficile Isolates", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12614991/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "AZD5148 is a toxin B–neutralizing human monoclonal antibody that binds to an epitope on the toxin B glucosyltransferase domain.\n...\nHerein, we showed that the AZD5148 epitope is highly conserved across all geographic regions and sequence types and has a higher average conservation frequency in its binding site (99.58%) when compared with bezlotoxumab (82.47%).\nAZD5148 also exhibited broad neutralization in vitro against 8 recently circulating ribotypes.\n...\nAn anti-TcdB monoclonal antibody (mAb), Zinplava (bezlotoxumab), was approved in 2016 and recently withdrawn from the market by the sponsor [5].\n...\nWe recently identified AZD5148: a TcdB–neutralizing human mAb that binds to an epitope on the GTD between residues 290 to 360 and prevents GTD export from the endosomes to the cytosol [16].\n...\nAZD5148 targets the GTD region of TcdB (Figure 1*A*).", "date": "2025-08-14", "last_updated": "2026-08-09", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.93, "evidence": 0.7033}, "jev_score": 0.6541, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "The monoclonal antibody AZD5148 confers broad protection against ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12594360/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "AZD5148 is a humanized anti-TcdB mAb that neutralizes toxin activity by blocking the delivery of the enzymatic glucosyltransferase domain (GTD) into host cells.\n...\nAZD5148 potently neutralized TcdB1 and TcdB2 *in vitro*, with EC~50~ values 1,000- to 14,000-fold lower than those of bezlotoxumab.\nIn a mouse CDI model induced by TcdB1- or TcdB2-expressing strains, AZD5148 provided robust protection against weight loss and mortality at significantly lower doses than bezlotoxumab.\nFurthermore, the addition of the anti-TcdA mAb, PA50, provided no additional protective benefit.\n...\nBezlotoxumab (Zinplava), an FDA-approved mAb targeting TcdB, was effective in reducing recurrence but has recently been discontinued.\n...\nBezlotoxumab (Zinplava), a TcdB-neutralizing monoclonal antibody (mAb), was FDA-approved to prevent recurrent CDI in high-risk patients, but its production was discontinued in January 2025 [16,17].\n...\nWe previously described PA41 (now called AZD5148), a humanized mAb that binds a highly conserved TcdB GTD epitope and prevents the delivery of the GTD enzymatic cargo into the host cytoplasm [25,26].\n...\nThese results suggest that the prophylactic administration of AZD5148 alone is effective in preventing severe CDI in mice.\n...\nIn conclusion, our data show that AZD5148 provides robust protection in a mouse model of CDI caused by diverse *C. difficile* strains.\nBy blocking the delivery of toxic cargo into the cell, AZD5148 efficacy is independent of the receptor tropism differences observed across TcdB subtypes.\n...\nAZD5148 is currently being evaluated in a Phase I clinical trial for safety, tolerability, and pharmacokinetics in healthy adults (NCT06469151).", "date": "2025-11-03", "last_updated": "2025-11-12", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.92, "evidence": 0.69}, "jev_score": 0.6348, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Monoclonal Antibodies Targeting Bacterial Infections: A Broad ...", "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s40259-026-00787-9?error=cookies_not_supported&code=2487fb5c-a174-4489-a3aa-8931585c2cf7", "domain": "link.springer.com", "snippet": "AstraZeneca is developing another anti-TcdB mAb, having licensed two anti-toxin mAbs from Progenics (both IgG1κ) [31].\nThe anti-TcdB mAb, AZD5148, was engineered with a YTE substitution, a triple-mutation M252Y/S254T/T256E in the Fc region that extends the mAb’s half-life in human sera [32, 33].\nAZD5148 (formerly PA41) binds the GTD of TcdB and prevents the delivery of TcdB enzymatic cargo into the host cell cytosol [34].\nTcdB binds at least three classes of receptors including CSPG4, Nectin-3, and PVRL3 [35,36,37].\nThe two most recently discovered receptors are known to bind TcdB outside of the CROPs domain.\nTherefore, it is possible that TcdB could escape neutralization by Zinplava^®^ [14].\nIn contrast, the strategy employed by AZD5148, focused on binding the GTD, is independent of the receptor expressed on the target cell surface and may offer broader neutralizing activity.\nA recent study on 9134 global isolates showed that the AZD5148 epitope was highly conserved (99.58%) when compared to Zinplava^®^ (82.47%).\nAZD5148 also exhibited broad neutralization in vitro against eight recent circulating TcdB ribotypes [33].\nIn addition, AZD5148 conferred protection in a CDI gnotobiotic piglet model, and in a mouse CDI model against strains with different TcdB ribotypes [38, 39].\nAZD5148 is currently being evaluated for efficacy and safety for the prevention of recurrence of CDI in individuals 18 years of age and above in the PRISM study (NCT07285213) [40, 41].\n...\nTcdB, as previously discussed, is a well-characterized protein that has been extensively validated as a therapeutic target for mAb development, making it a good choice for testing novel mAb development methodologies.", "date": "2026-06-15", "last_updated": "2026-09-04", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.91, "evidence": 0.66}, "jev_score": 0.6006, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Ozmosi | AZD-5148 Drug Profile", "url": "https://pryzm.ozmosi.com/product/33039", "domain": "pryzm.ozmosi.com", "snippet": "Anti-toxin B neutralizing mAb AZD5148 provides protection in a Clostridioides difficile gnotobiotic piglet model.\n...\nMechanisms of Action: TcdB Inhibitor\n...\nFDA Designation: *\nApproval Status: Not Approved\nApproved Countries: None\n...\nCompany: AstraZeneca\n...\n###### Active Clinical Trial Count: 3\n...\nPhase 1: Clostridium Infections|Healthy Volunteers\n|Trial|Phase|Trial Status|Disease|Primary Completion Date|Probability of Success|Latest Trial Update Date|Data Updated|\n...\n|D8820C00005|P1|Active, not recruiting|Clostridium Infections|2025-12-29|69%|2025-01-23|Primary Endpoints|Study Completion Date|Treatments|Trial Status|\n|jRCT2071240071|P1|Not yet recruiting|Healthy Volunteers|2026-01-12|\n|D8820C00002|P1|Active, not recruiting|Healthy Volunteers|2025-11-07|69%|2025-01-22|", "date": "2025-01-23", "last_updated": "2025-06-13", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.75, "evidence": 0.62}, "jev_score": 0.465, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "H1 and Q2 2026 Results Clinical Trials Appendix", "url": "https://www.