{"case_id": "q015-batch", "row_id": "q015", "mode": "batch", "objective": "GFH375/VS-7375 in vivo monotherapy tumour growth inhibition TGI tumour regression tumour shrinkage in KRAS G12D-Mutated/MTAP-Deleted Pancreatic Ductal Adenocarcinoma animal models", "query": ["GFH375 VS-7375 quantitative TGI KP4 PDAC monotherapy", "GFH375 VS-7375 MTAP-deleted KP4 monotherapy tumour regression", "GFH375 VS-7375 MTAP-deleted pancreatic xenograft single-agent TGI", "GFH375 VS-7375 AsPC-1 Panc 04.03 monotherapy tumour volume data"], "anchors": ["GFH375 VS-7375", "GFH375", "VS-7375"], "search_latency_ms": 542.1, "target": "GFH375 VS-7375 / GFH375 / VS-7375", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 461.9, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 10755, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000452, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 10, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.7284, "results": [{"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "VS-7375 (GFH375): An oral, selective KRAS G12D (ON/ ...", "url": "https://www.verastem.com/pdf/AACR-VS7375-Poster-Presentation.pdf", "domain": "verastem.com", "snippet": "VS-7375 (GFH375) is an oral KRAS G12D-selective small molecule inhibitor (G12Di) that binds\nKRAS G12D in both the active (ON; GTP-bound) and inactive (OFF; GDP-bound) states (Figure \n1) (2).\n...\nFurthermore, we have shown that oral administration of VS-7375 inhibits tumor \ngrowth in a dose-dependent manner in KRAS G12D-mutant human cancer models in vivo including pancreatic and colorectal cancers (Figure 2) (2).\nVS-7375 monotherapy is currently in phase 2 clinical evaluation (NCT06500676).\n...\n• VS-7375 induces strong tumor regressions (>30% reduction) in contrast to G12D ON (RMC-9805) and\npan-RAS ON (RMC-6236) inhibitors in a KRAS G12D-mutant xenograft model\n• VS-7375 monotherapy induces tumor regressions in multiple KRAS G12D-mutant cancer models and \nshows strong combinatorial anti-tumor efficacy with cetuximab\n• These results support the ongoing clinical evaluation of VS-7375 for treatment of patients with KRAS \nG12D-mutant cancers (NCT06500676)", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-14", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.98, "evidence": 0.7433}, "jev_score": 0.7284, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Selectivity over HUVEC (P value not updated) - verastem.com", "url": "https://www.verastem.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/GFH375-poster-for-2024-AACR-Final-20240329.pdf", "domain": "verastem.com", "snippet": "GFH375 potently and selectively inhibits p-ERK signaling and proliferation in KRAS G12D mutant tumor cells\nGFH375 accumulates in tumor tissue and elicits sustained inhibition of p-ERK signaling following a single oral administration\nGFH375 induces tumor regression in multiple KRAS G12D subcutaneous CDX tumor models\nvia oral administration\nPK/PD study in Panc04.03 PDAC CDX mice\n...\nThe in vitro potency and selectivity, oral bioavailability, and in vivo efficacy of monotherapy and combination therapy with avutometinib, a unique RAF/MEK clamp, were evaluated in preclinical studies.\n...\n• GFH375 (VS-7375) is a highly potent and selective inhibitor of \nKRAS G12D (ON/OFF).\n• GFH375 is orally bioavailable across preclinical species and \nholds promising therapeutic potential for treatment of KRAS \nG12D solid tumors according to its performance in multiple CDX tumor models.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-09-17", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.96, "evidence": 0.72}, "jev_score": 0.6912, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "[PDF] Verastem Oncology Announces Late Breaking and Regular ...", "url": "https://investor.verastem.com/node/15761/pdf", "domain": "investor.verastem.com", "snippet": "Preclinical research with VS-7375 in combination with PRMT5 inhibitors demonstrated strong durable tumor regressions in KRAS G12D pancreatic cancer models\nIn preclinical pancreatic, lung, and colorectal cancer models, VS-7375 (dual ON/OFF inhibitor) showed deeper and more sustained tumor regression compared to ON-only G12D or pan-RAS inhibitors\n...\nTitle: Strong durable tumor regressions with the KRAS G12D ON/OFF inhibitor VS-7375 in combination with PRMT5 inhibition in MTAP-deleted/KRAS\nG12D-mutant pancreatic cancer\n...\nTitle: VS-7375: An oral, selective KRAS G12D dual ON/OFF inhibitor with potent anti-tumor activity as a single agent and in combination with other\n...\nIn the KP4 KRAS G12D pancreatic cancer model, VS-7375 (50 mg/kg twice daily), zoldonrasib (100 mg/kg once daily) and daraxonrasib (25 mg/kg once daily), produced similar initial tumor regression through day nine.\nBy approximately day 20, however, zoldonrasib and daraxonrasib lost anti-tumor activity with tumor outgrowth, (mean tumor volume >850 mm by day 30) whereas VS-7375 maintained sustained tumor regression (mean tumor volume ~80 mm by day 30), consistent with pharmacodynamic analyses showing durable pathway inhibition only with VS-7375.\nVS-7375 also showed deeper tumor regression compared to these RAS ON-only inhibitors in KRAS G12D-mutated lung and colorectal xenograft models.\n...\nVerastem initiated VS-7375-101, an international Phase 1/2 clinical trial, in June of 2025 in the U.S., that is evaluating the safety and efficacy of VS-7375 in patients with advanced KRAS G12D mutant solid tumors.\nIn\nJuly 2025, U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) granted Fast Track Designation (FTD) to VS-7375 for the first-line treatment of patients with\nKRAS G12D-mutated locally advanced or metastatic adenocarcinoma of the pancreas (PDAC) and for the treatment of patients with KRAS\nG12D-mutated locally advanced or metastatic PDAC who have received at least one prior line of standard systemic therapy.\n...\nVerastem selected VS-7375 (also known as GFH375), an oral KRAS G12D\n(ON/OFF) inhibitor, as its lead program in December 2023 and the license for VS-7375 that was exercised in January 2025 is the first one from this", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-07-06", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.89, "evidence": 0.76}, "jev_score": 0.6764, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "[PDF] VS-7375: An oral, selective KRAS G12D dual ON/OFF inhibitor with ...", "url": "http://www.verastem.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/AACR-2025-VS-7375-poster-E-poster-4.8.26-printed.pdf", "domain": "verastem.com", "snippet": "VS-7375 (GFH375) is an oral KRAS G12D-selective small molecule inhibitor that binds KRAS G12D in both the ON and OFF states.\nBy blocking the activities of the ON and OFF states of KRAS G12D,\nVS-7375 has the potential to inhibit KRAS G12D signaling and tumor growth more completely than compounds that block KRAS G12D predominantly in the OFF state or only in the ON state.\nVS-7375 monotherapy and in combination with other agents is currently in clinical evaluation in the US\n(NCT07020221) and in China (NCT06500676; NCT07262567; NCT07259590).\n...\nMice bearing KRAS G12D-mutated tumors were treated with VS-7375 (KP4: 50 mg/kg orally twice daily; LS513 & LU0876: 30 mg/kg orally \ntwice daily), RMC-9805 (KP4: 100 mg/kg orally once daily; LS513: 60 mg/kg orally once daily) or RMC-6236 (25 mg/kg orally once daily), N = 8\nmice/group.\nAverage tumor volumes over time are shown.\nPDAC = pancreatic cancer; CRC = colorectal cancer; NSLCL = non-small cell lung cancer.\n...\n• VS-7375 is a potent KRAS G12D ON/OFF inhibitor with long residence time (18-24 hours) leading to more\npotent and durable suppression of pERK than the KRAS G12D ON-only inhibitor zoldonrasib (RMC-9805)\n• Across KRAS G12D-mutated tumor models in vivo, oral dosing of VS-7375 is more efficacious than KRAS \nG12D and pan-RAS ON-only inhibitors\n• In a KRAS G12D-mutated pancreatic cancer model, mechanisms of resistance observed with the tricomplex \ninhibitors zoldonrasib (RMC-9805) and daraxonrasib (RMC-6236) do not limit the durability of VS-7375 efficacy\n• Combinations of VS-7375 with other targeted therapies (e.g. cetuximab, FAK inhibitor, avutometinib, PRMT5i)\nconfer durable tumor regressions \n• These results support the ongoing clinical evaluation of VS-7375 as monotherapy and in combinations for \ntreatment of patients with KRAS G12D-mutated cancers (VS-7375-101; NCT06500676)\n...\nMice bearing KP4 KRAS G12D-mutated pancreatic tumors were treated with VS-7375 (50 mg/kg twice daily), RMC-9805 (100 mg/kg once \n...\nCombination of a PRMT5 inhibitor with VS-7375 induces durable tumor regressions            \nin a KRAS G12D-mutated;MTAP-deleted pancreatic cancer model\n...\nFigure 8.\nMice bearing KP4 KRAS G12D-mutated/MTAP-deleted pancreatic tumors were treated with VS-7375 (50 mg/kg orally, twice daily), a PRMT5 inhibitor (BMS-986504 & AMG193: 100 mg/kg orally, once daily; TNG462: 30 mg/kg orally, twice daily) or the combination.\nN = 8 mice/group.\n...\nVS-7375 suppresses pERK, pAKT, pS6 and MYC levels more potently, rapidly, and \ndurably than the KRAS G12D ON-only inhibitor zoldonrasib (RMC-9805)", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-08", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.98, "evidence": 0.69}, "jev_score": 0.6762, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Selectivity over HUVEC （P value not updated)", "url": "https://www.verastem.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/04/GFH375-poster-for-2024-AACR-Final-20240329.pdf", "domain": "verastem.com", "snippet": "GFH375 potently and selectively inhibits p-ERK signaling and proliferation in KRAS G12D mutant tumor cells\nGFH375 accumulates in tumor tissue and elicits sustained inhibition of p-ERK signaling following a single oral administration\nGFH375 induces tumor regression in multiple KRAS G12D subcutaneous CDX tumor models\nvia oral administration\nPK/PD study in Panc04.03 PDAC CDX mice\n...\nThe in vitro potency and selectivity, oral bioavailability, and in vivo efficacy of monotherapy and combination therapy with avutometinib, a unique RAF/MEK clamp, were evaluated in preclinical studies.\n...\n• GFH375 (VS-7375) is a highly potent and selective inhibitor of \nKRAS G12D (ON/OFF).\n• GFH375 is orally bioavailable across preclinical species and \nholds promising therapeutic potential for treatment of KRAS \nG12D solid tumors according to its performance in multiple CDX tumor models.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-11-04", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.94, "evidence": 0.6967}, "jev_score": 0.6549, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Resources | Verastem, Inc.", "url": "https://www.verastem.com/our-science/resources/", "domain": "verastem.com", "snippet": "### VS-7375: An oral, selective KRAS G12D dual ON/OFF inhibitor with potent anti-tumor activity as a single agent and in combination with other agents\n...\n### Strong durable tumor regressions with the KRASG12D ON/OFF inhibitor VS-7375 in combination with PRMT5inhibition in MTAP-deleted/KRASG12D-mutant pancreatic cancer", "date": "2026-05-05", "last_updated": "2026-10-03", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.93, "evidence": 0.67}, "jev_score": 0.6231, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Pipeline | Verastem, Inc.", "url": "https://www.verastem.com/our-science/pipeline/", "domain": "verastem.com", "snippet": "- **VS-7375, an oral KRAS G12D (ON/OFF) inhibitor**: we are evaluating VS-7375 as monotherapy and in combinations for KRAS G12D-mutated PDAC, non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC), colorectal cancer (CRC) and other solid tumors.\n...\n|## KRAS G12D-mutated solid tumors (PDAC, NSCLC, CRC) **Regimen**: dose escalation and expansion trial of VS-7375 monotherapy + various combinations**Study Name**: TARGET-D 101|TARGET-D 101|Discovery|Preclinical|Phase 1|Phase 2|Phase 3|FDA Approved|\n|## KRAS G12D-mutated 2L metastatic PDAC **Regimen**: registration-directed trial of VS-7375 monotherapy + EGFR combination**Study Name**: TARGET-D 201|TARGET-D 201|Discovery|Preclinical|Phase 1|Phase 2|Phase 3|FDA Approved|\n...\nVS-7375 (known as GFH375 in China), a potential best-in-class, potent and selective oral KRAS G12D (ON/OFF) inhibitor, has been identified as the lead discovery program.\nGenFleet’s IND for GFH375 (VS-7375) was approved in June 2024, and they are currently conducting Phase 1/2 and Phase 3 clinical trials in China.", "date": "2026-05-06", "last_updated": "2026-10-03", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.97, "evidence": 0.62}, "jev_score": 0.6014, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Untitled", "url": "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1526119/000110465925100537/tm2529024d1_ex99-1.htm", "domain": "sec.gov", "snippet": "*GFH375 resulted in a 41% ORR for patients with\nheavily pre-treated pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (68% had received 2 or more prior lines of therapy) at the 600 mg daily dose*\n...\n*91.5% of patients experienced a reduction in\ntumors and a disease control rate of 96.7%; nearly half the patients evaluated remain on treatment*\n...\nannounced positive, updated efficacy and safety data from partner GenFleet Therapeutics’ Phase 1/2 monotherapy study in China\nof GFH375, an oral KRAS G12D (ON/OFF) inhibitor (VS-7375 outside of China) for patients with KRAS G12D mutant advanced pancreatic ductal\nadenocarcinoma (PDAC).\nAmong 59 heavily pre-treated patients with advanced disease, who received two or more prior lines of therapy, an\noverall response rate (ORR) of 41% was achieved at the monotherapy recommended Phase 2 dose (RP2D) of 600 mg daily (QD).\n...\nGenFleet reported that 66 patients with advanced KRAS G12D mutant PDAC\nwere treated with 600 mg QD of GFH375 monotherapy.\n...\nAs of the data cutoff of September 27, 2025,\n59 efficacy-evaluable patients had at least one post-treatment tumor assessment and achieved an ORR of 40.7% (24/59) (confirmed and unconfirmed)\nand a disease control rate (DCR) of 96.7% (57/59) with the majority of patients (91.5%) experiencing a reduction in target lesions.\nOverall\nSurvival (OS) observed at month four was 92.2%.\nThe median OS was not reached as of the data cutoff, with a median follow-up time of 5.65\nmonths.\nThe median progression-free survival (PFS) was 5.52 months with a median follow-up time of 5.65 months and a 4-month PFS rate\nof 78.2%.\nAt evaluation, 31 (47%) of patients were still on treatment with the longest duration of treatment eclipsing one year (367 days).\n...\nOf the 66 patients in the safety population, four patients (6.1%) had a dose reduction and two patients (3%) discontinued due to TRAEs.\nNo TRAE-related deaths were reported.\n...\nThe Phase 1/2a study will be conducted in the U.S., with the potential\nto expand globally, and will evaluate the safety and efficacy of VS-7375 in patients with advanced KRAS G12D mutant solid tumors.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-06-18", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.96, "evidence": 0.6033}, "jev_score": 0.5792, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "[PDF] GenFleet Therapeutics (Shanghai) Inc. 勁方醫藥科技(上海)股份有限 ...", "url": "https://www1.hkexnews.hk/listedco/listconews/sehk/2026/0820/2026082000047.pdf", "domain": "www1.hkexnews.hk", "snippet": "The board of directors of the Company (the “Board”) is pleased to announce the initiation of a three-arm, parallel-controlled phase II study evaluating two novel combination regimens versus monotherapy of GFH375, an oral KRAS G12D (ON/OFF) inhibitor, among pretreated KRAS\nG12D-mutant patients with locally advanced unresectable or metastatic pancreatic cancer.\n...\nGFH375/VS-7375 received China’s first Breakthrough Therapy Designation for monotherapy in\nKRAS G12D-mutant metastatic pancreatic cancer in February 2026, following Fast Track designation from the U.S. FDA for KRAS G12D-mutant metastatic pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (PDAC) granted in 2025.\n...\nGFH375 is the world’s first oral KRAS G12D inhibitor that advanced into a registrational phase III monotherapy trial for metastatic pancreatic cancer.\n...\nGFH375 is an orally active, potent, highly selective small-molecule KRAS G12D (ON/OFF) inhibitor designed to bind to the protein of KRAS G12D non-covalently, thereby disrupting the activation of downstream pathways and effectively inhibiting tumor cell proliferation.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-21", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.89, "evidence": 0.6033}, "jev_score": 0.5369, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "GFH375/VS-7375 Program Update", "url": "https://www.genfleet.com/Public/Uploads/uploadfile/files/20251207/Oct.pdf", "domain": "genfleet.com", "snippet": "ORR [90%CI]\n40.7% [30%, 52%]\n...\n• ORR was 40.7% (24/59), 90%CI was [30%, 52%].\n• DCR was 96.7% (57/59), 90%CI was [90%, 99%].\nMajority (91.5%) had reduction in target lesions.\n...\n• Median PFS was 5.52 months (90%CI: 4.27, 7.20), with a median follow-up time 5.65 months (90%CI: 4.96, 6.08).\n• 4-month PFS rate was 78.2% (90%CI: 69.8%, 87.5%).\n...\n• The safety profile of GFH375 in  KRAS G12D mutant PDAC patients is consistent with previous report.1,2\n– Common TRAEs were gastrointestinal and hematological AEs; most were grade 1 or 2 and manageable with supportive treatment.\n– Most frequent TRAEs (≥20%) were diarrhea (56.1%), neutrophil count decreased (48.5%), vomiting (47.0%), nausea (47.0%),\n...\n(N=59)\n2L\n...\n(N=47)\nORR\n[90%CI]\n40.68%\n[29.87%, 52.22%]\n58.33%\n...\nPopulation\n2L PDAC with KRAS G12Dm\n1\n3L+ PDAC with KRAS G12Dm\n1\n2L PDAC with KRAS G12Xm\n3\n3L+ PDAC with KRAS G12Xm\n4\nSample size\n12\n47\n26\n63\nORR\n58.3%\n36.2%\n35%\n22%\nDCR\n100%\n95.7%\n92%\n89%\n...\n• No new safety signal has been found\n...\nThe overall safety/tolerability of GFH375 is better than that of daraxonrasib.\n...\nThe incidence of grade ≥3 TEAEs/TRAEs, TEAEs/TRAEs leading to treatment interruption, and \nSAEs/TRSAEs were higher in participants with prior ICI than in the ICI naïve participants (ESMO2025 data)\n...\nBest-in-class as G12D monotherapy for NSCLC\n•\nSupporting single-arm accelerated approval for 2L+ NSCLC\n...\nVS-7375-101 Study Schema, Efficiently Testing KRAS G12D+ Indications with Monotherapy and SOC Combinations\n...\n• Efficacious starting dose:\n• 400 mg starting dose supported by monotherapy \nefficacy/safety in FIH study\n• Evaluating GI side effect mitigations:\n• Use prophylactic anti-emetics\n• Evaluate dosing with and without food\n...\nFDA Engagement Plans\n• Granted Fast Track Designation for: \n• First-line in patients with KRAS G12D locally \nadvanced or metastatic PDAC and\n• Patients with KRAS G12D locally advanced or \nmPDAC who received at least one prior line of standard systemic therapy in July 2025\n• Plan to pursue Breakthrough Therapy \nDesignation\n• Accelerated clinical development with FDA \n...\n• First two monotherapy dose levels (400 mg QD and 600 mg QD) cleared, \nwith no dose-limiting toxicities (DLT’s) reported\n• Promising anti-tumor activity observed in patients with various solid \ntumors, including advanced pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma\n• No nausea, vomiting, or diarrhea greater than Grade 1 was observed \n• Enrollment initiated for VS-7375 in combination with cetuximab in \npatients with advanced KRAS G12D mutant solid tumors, including colorectal \ncancer\n...\nmonotherapy dose ", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-21", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.94, "evidence": 0.5233}, "jev_score": 0.4919, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}], "total_ms": 1004.0, "ts": "2026-10-06T12:18:07+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q012-batch", "row_id": "q012", "mode": "batch", "objective": "Lenvatinib + Nofazinlimab pivotal, registrational, or Phase 3 trial in Unresectable or Metastatic Hepatocellular Carcinoma: planned or initiated status and expected start date", "query": ["Lenvatinib + Nofazinlimab NCT04194775 first patient enrollment date", "Lenvatinib + Nofazinlimab NCT04194775 current trial status"], "anchors": ["Lenvatinib + Nofazinlimab NCT04194775", "Lenvatinib", "Nofazinlimab", "NCT04194775"], "search_latency_ms": 1057.7, "target": "Lenvatinib + Nofazinlimab NCT04194775 / Lenvatinib / Nofazinlimab / NCT04194775", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 633.0, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 10018, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000421, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 10, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.9734, "results": [{"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "A Study of Nofazinlimab (CS1003) in… · NCT04194775 · OnCo", "url": "https://onco.cc/trials/nct04194775/", "domain": "onco.cc", "snippet": "A phase 3 trial of Lenvatinib in hepatocellular carcinoma, run by CStone Pharmaceuticals, active and no longer recruiting.\n...\nA Study of Nofazinlimab (CS1003) in Subjects With Advanced Hepatocellular Carcinoma is a phase 3 interventional study registered as NCT04194775 by CStone Pharmaceuticals, with 534 participants enrolled, started 2019-12-13 and due to reach its primary completion in 2025-06-18.\nInterventions recorded: Nofazinlimab (CS1003)+Lenvatinib, Nofazinlimab (CS1003) Placebo+Lenvatinib.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-29", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.98, "evidence": 0.9933}, "jev_score": 0.9734, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "A Study of Nofazinlimab (CS1003) in Subjects With ...", "url": "https://www.guidelinecentral.com/clinical-trial/?id=NCT04194775&v=tabular", "domain": "guidelinecentral.com", "snippet": "# A Study of Nofazinlimab (CS1003) i