{"case_id": "q017-batch", "row_id": "q017", "mode": "batch", "objective": "SW-682 in vivo single-agent monotherapy tumour growth inhibition TGI tumour regression or shrinkage in Head and Neck Squamous Cell Carcinoma animal models", "query": ["SW-682 CAL-33 SCC-25 xenograft tumour growth inhibition monotherapy", "SW-682 HNSCC xenograft tumour regression monotherapy", "SW-682 CAL-33 SCC-25 tumour volume TGI"], "anchors": ["SW-682"], "search_latency_ms": 553.6, "target": "SW-682", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 596.1, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 9431, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000396, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 8, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.8396, "results": [{"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Targeting YAP/TAZ-TEAD signaling as a therapeutic approach in ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12044704/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Here, we provide the pre-clinical evidence for the antitumor activity of novel smTEADi, SW-682 in HPV-negative HNSCC.\nBy the use of multiple complementary experimental approaches, including siRNA knockdown, expression of a genetically encoded TEAD inhibitor peptide (pTEADi), and SW-682, we revealed that disruption of YAP/TAZ-TEAD interaction suppresses YAP/TAZ-TEAD-dependent target gene transcription and growth of HNSCC tumors.\nHNSCC cells with genetic alterations in *FAT1* were more sensitive to TEADi compared to *FAT1-* wild type cells.\nMechanistically, TEADi suppressed cell cycle progression and promoted the expression of terminal differentiation gene programs, resulting in tumor growth inhibition.\n...\nFinally, we provide pre-clinical evidence for the efficacy of novel smTEADi, SW-682, in HNSCC.\n...\nStrikingly, pTEADi significantly suppressed tumor growth of Cal33 and Cal27, whereas failed to exhibit growth inhibition in HN12 that carries wild type *FAT1* (Figs. 2I–2L; Supplementary Figs. S2E and S2F), suggesting that these *FAT1*-mutant HNSCC cells are more sensitive to TEAD inhibition.\n...\nWe then examined the antitumor activities of SW-682, the investigational smTEADi being tested clinically, in Cal33, Cal27 and HN12 HNSCC xenograft models.\nTumor-bearing NSG mice were treated once daily with SW-682 (30mg/kg) or vehicle.\nNotably, we observed significant growth inhibition in Cal33 and Cal27 tumors (Figs. 3F and 3G), but more moderate effects in HN12, similar to the observations in the pTEADi-expressing HN12 xenograft models (Figs. 3H and 3I).\nWe did not observe a significant difference in body weight between vehicle- and SW-682-treated groups, suggesting minimal toxicity *in vivo* (Supplementary Figs. S3C and S3D).\n...\nOf interest, the novel pan-TEAD inhibitor SW-682 showed a higher antitumor effect in *FAT1-* mutant HNSCC xenograft models than *FAT1*-wild type counterparts, which was supported by the results with pTEADi that closely phenocopied the TEAD pharmacological blockade.\n...\nThe emerging picture from our studies is that both a smTEADi, SW-682, and pTEADi promote the transcriptional reprogramming of HNSCC cells, promoting concomitantly cell cycle arrest and terminal differentiation, thereby providing a novel mechanistic framework for the potential antitumor activity of TEADi.", "date": "2025-01-16", "last_updated": "2026-03-04", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.98, "evidence": 0.8567}, "jev_score": 0.8396, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Targeting TEAD in cancer - PMC - NIH", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12672301/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "In head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (HNSCC), use of SW-682 to block YAP/TAZ-TEAD binding led to an antitumor response in FAT1-mutant HNSCC xenograft models (85).\n...\nSW-682 (NCT06251310) is a pan-TEAD small molecule inhibitor which blocks palmitoylation of TEAD to inhibit all TEAD-dependent transcription (161).\nIt was reported that SW-682 inhibited proliferation of Hippo-mutant mesothelioma cells *in vitro* and tumor regression in murine mesothelioma models.\nLike other TEAD inhibitors, SW-682 synergized with EGFR and KRAS^G12C^ inhibitors for NSCLC PDX models *in vivo (* 161).\nSW-682 was also shown to decrease cellular proliferation and downregulated YAP/TAZ-TEAD target genes in HNSCC cell lines Cal27 and Cal33 and in tumor xenograft models (85).\nIt was also reported that SW-682 has good oral bioavailability in mice and did not lead to weight loss during treatment.\nHowever, there were no reports on the potential effects of SW-682 on renal function and urine biomarkers which have been reported with use of other TEAD inhibitors which could indicate a better side effect profile clinically.", "date": "2025-11-19", "last_updated": "2026-10-02", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.98, "evidence": 0.7833}, "jev_score": 0.7676, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "SW-682 - Drug Targets, Indications, Patents", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/drug/375454a460c05815e86c5f64e4da29bb", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "Here, we provide the pre-clinical evidence for the antitumor activity of novel smTEADi, SW-682 in HPV-negative HNSCC.\nBy the use of multiple complementary experimental approaches, including siRNA knockdown, expression of a genetically encoded TEAD inhibitor peptide (pTEADi), and SW-682, we revealed that disruption of YAP/TAZ-TEAD interaction suppresses YAP/TAZ-TEAD-dependent target gene transcription and growth of HNSCC tumors.\nHNSCC cells with genetic alterations in FAT1 were more sensitive to TEADi compared to FAT1-wild type cells.\nMechanistically, TEADi suppressed cell cycle progression and promoted the expression of terminal differentiation gene programs, resulting in tumor growth inhibition.\n...\n|Advanced Malignant Solid Neoplasm|Phase 1|United States|Springworks Therapeutics, Inc.|31 Jul 2024|\n...\n|Solid tumor|IND Application|United States|SpringWorks Therapeutics LLC|09 Jan 2024|\n...\n|Squamous Cell Carcinoma of Head and Neck|Preclinical|United States|Springworks Therapeutics, Inc.|29 Apr 2025|", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-30", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.98, "evidence": 0.6933}, "jev_score": 0.6794, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Springworks Therapeutics, Inc. - Drug pipelines, Patents ...", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/organization/130c14f677be7b74c390c1511f90bc6e", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "Here, we provide the pre-clinical evidence for the antitumor activity of novel smTEADi, SW-682 in HPV-negative HNSCC.\nBy the use of multiple complementary experimental approaches, including siRNA knockdown, expression of a genetically encoded TEAD inhibitor peptide (pTEADi), and SW-682, we revealed that disruption of YAP/TAZ-TEAD interaction suppresses YAP/TAZ-TEAD-dependent target gene transcription and growth of HNSCC tumors.\nHNSCC cells with genetic alterations in FAT1 were more sensitive to TEADi compared to FAT1-wild type cells.\nMechanistically, TEADi suppressed cell cycle progression and promoted the expression of terminal differentiation gene programs, resulting in tumor growth inhibition.\n...\nSTRIKE-001 (NCT07103018) is a multi-center, open-label dose escalation evaluating KTX-2001 as a monotherapy (Part A) and in combination with darolutamide (Part B).\nPart A is designed to evaluate the safety, tolerability, maximum tolerated dose, and recommended Phase 2 dose(s) of KTX-2001 monotherapy.\n...\n|SW-682 ( TEAD )|Advanced Malignant Solid Neoplasm More|Phase 1|", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-29", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.96, "evidence": 0.6933}, "jev_score": 0.6656, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "SW-682 / EMD Serono", "url": "https://delta.larvol.com/Products/?ProductId=d78df0cf-33d8-4282-974c-3719ae487f81", "domain": "delta.larvol.com", "snippet": "## Targeting YAP/TAZ-TEAD signaling as a therapeutic approach in head and neck squamous cell carcinoma. (PubMed, Cancer Lett) - \"Here, we provide the pre-clinical evidence for the antitumor activity of novel smTEADi, SW-682 in HPV-negative HNSCC. A HNSCC-specific TEADi target gene set was defined from RNA-seq data, which is highly expressed in HNSCC tissues and predicts poor prognosis of HPV-negative HNSCC patients. Our results underscore that YAP/TAZ-TEAD-mediated growth-promoting programs represent a vulnerability in HPV-negative HNSCC, thus providing a pre-clinical rationale for the future evaluation of YAP/TAZ-TEAD targeting strategies as a therapeutic approach for HPV-negative HNSCC patients.\"\n...\n## Rational combination of pan-TEAD inhibitor SW-682 and MEK inhibitor mirdametinib in head and neck squamous cell carcinomas leads to synergistic response (EORTC-NCI-AACR 2024) - \"The pan-TEAD inhibitor SW-682 modulates tumor growth in HNSCC models harboring aberrant Hippo pathway signaling both in vitro and in vivo. The combination of SW-682 with mirdametinib resulted in enhanced anti-tumor responses in HNSCC models. These observations may provide a rational combination approach for clinical studies.\"\n...\n## SW-682: A novel TEAD inhibitor for the treatment of cancers bearing mutations in the Hippo signaling pathway (AACR 2023) - \"Moreover, to identify new indications that may benefit from TEAD inhibition, we screened patient-derived 3D organoid tumor cells and matching patient-derived xenograft models that have been molecularly profiled. In summary, SW-682 is a potent and selective investigational TEAD inhibitor which demonstrated anti-tumor effects in models harboring aberrant expression of the Hippo pathway, suggesting therapeutic potential in multiple Hippo-mutant solid tumors.\"", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-10-02", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.98, "evidence": 0.6733}, "jev_score": 0.6598, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "[PDF] SW-682: a novel TEAD inhibitor for the treatment of cancers bearing ...", "url": "https://springworkstx.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/TEAD-AACR-2023-Poster_FINAL.pdf", "domain": "springworkstx.com", "snippet": "In vivo, daily oral administration of \nSW-682 in tumor-bearing mice resulted in tumor regression in Hippo-mutant mesothelioma models and caused decreased expression of the TEAD- dependent genes CCN1 and CCN2 and a YAP gene signature4, as measured by RNA-seq analysis.\n...\nSW-682 is efficacious in vivo in two mesothelioma models and caused dose-\ndependent tumor regression in xenograft model\n...\nopportunities guided by a biomarker-driven development approach \n...\nTumor regressions observed with SW-682 at well-tolerated daily\noral doses with no effects on body weight ", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-07-10", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.97, "evidence": 0.66}, "jev_score": 0.6402, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Genigraphics Research Poster Template 42x72", "url": "https://springworkstx.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/Schirmer_TEAD_Head-Neck-SCC_ENA-2024.pdf", "domain": "springworkstx.com", "snippet": "Rational combination of pan-TEAD inhibitor SW-682 and MEK inhibitor mirdametinib in head and neck squamous cell carcinomas leads to synergistic response\n...\n▪The pan-TEAD inhibitor SW-682 modulates tumor growth in \nHNSCC models harboring aberrant Hippo pathway signaling both in vitro and in vivo.\n▪Addition of SW-682 synergized with the anti-proliferative effects of\nmirdametinib in vitro in HNSCC models\n▪Combination with SW-682 enhanced efficacy and response \ndurability of MEK targeted therapies in preclinical HNSCC models, supporting investigation of SW-682 combinations in clinical studies.\n...\nSW-682 decreases colony formation in HNSCC FAT1 loss models\n• Treatment with SW-682 results in a dose dependent decrease in \nCAL-33 and SCC-25 colony formation after 14 days.\nCombination of mirdametinib and SW-682 synergized to reduce proliferation of HNSCC in vitro\n...\nCombination of mirdametinib and SW-682 further reduced colony formation when combined in HNSCC\n• Given the evidence of cooperative dependence between MAPK \nand Hippo pathways and the variable monotherapy response, in vitro combination effects of SW-682 with mirdametinib were\nevaluated.\n• Higher synergy was observed in the SCC-25 model, with an \naverage Bliss score of 24.4.\nCAL-33 showed a reduced Bliss score of 10.7.\n• CAL-33 and SCC-25 cell lines harboring biallelic loss of function \n...\nwith 100 nM SW-682\n• Addition of SW-682 to mirdametinib reduced clonogenic capacity \nof SCC-25 and CAL-33 cells.\nCombination of SW-682 and mirdametinib \ndemonstrated increased anti-tumor activity in CAL-33 xenograft model\nA. Combination of SW-682 and mirdametinib significantly \nincreased tumor growth inhibition (%TGI) over monotherapy in the CAL-33 model.\nB. On day 47 post-treatment, SW-682 plus mirdametinib resulted \nin significantly smaller tumor volumes compared to mirdametinib alone.\nThis study is ongoing.\n...\nGr.\n2: 44%\nGr.\n4: 80%\nGr.\n6: 93%*", "date": null, "last_updated": "2024-12-20", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.95, "evidence": 0.6633}, "jev_score": 0.6301, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "GEO Accession viewer", "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/query/acc.cgi?acc=GSE277859", "domain": "ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Targeting YAP/TAZ-TEAD as a precision therapy approach in head and neck squamous cell carcinoma\n...\nIn this regard, HPV-negative head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (HNSCC) harbor multiple genetic alterations that promote YAP/TAZ hyperactivation, raising the possibility that HNSCC cells might be dependent on YAP/TAZ-TEAD driven oncogenic transcriptional programs.\nTo test this hypothesis, we examined the antitumor activity of the novel smTEADi, SW-682 and genetically encoded TEAD inhibitor peptide (pTEADi) in Cal33 HPV-negative HNSCC cell line-derived xenograft model.\nTo elucidate the transcriptomic changes upon YAP/TAZ-TEAD inhibition, RNA extracted from xenograft tumors treated with SW-682 or pTEADi, as well as control, was subjected to RNA sequencing.", "date": "2024-10-01", "last_updated": "2025-10-04", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.97, "evidence": 0.6467}, "jev_score": 0.6273, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Patient-Derived Xenograft and Organoid Models for Precision Medicine Targeting of the Tumour Microenvironment in Head and Neck Cancer", "url": "https://www.mdpi.com/2072-6694/12/12/3743", "domain": "mdpi.com", "snippet": "A matched PDX model and cell line developed from the resistant tumour remained sensitive to gefitinib, so the patient was switched to gefitinib monotherapy and demonstrated remarkable regression within six weeks [88].\nA second patient that progressed on cytotoxic chemotherapy was switched to erlotinib after the patient’s PDX showed a robust response, leading to tumour regression in the patient [88].", "date": "2020-12-12", "last_updated": "2026-08-13", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.04, "evidence": 0.11}, "jev_score": 0.0044, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "CCDI Hub", "url": "https://moleculartargets.ccdi.cancer.gov/studies/phs003215", "domain": "moleculartargets.ccdi.cancer.gov", "snippet": "This system is provided for Government-authorized use only.\n...\nAt any time, and for any lawful Government purpose, the government may monitor, intercept, and search and seize any communication or data transiting or stored on this system.\n- Any communication or data transiting or stored on this system may be disclosed or used for any lawful Government purpose.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-19", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.06, "evidence": 0.0033}, "jev_score": 0.0002, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}], "total_ms": 1149.7, "ts": "2026-10-06T12:18:08+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q019-batch", "row_id": "q019", "mode": "batch", "objective": "Innovent Biologics Inc. descriptions of IBI-3001's advantage over existing treatments for Head and Neck Squamous Cell Carcinoma in investor materials, earnings calls, investor day presentations, and press releases", "query": ["IBI-3001 HNSCC investor presentation treatment advantage", "IBI3001 recurrent metastatic HNSCC Innovent press release", "IBI3001 HNSCC first-line standard of care comparison"], "anchors": ["IBI-3001"], "search_latency_ms": 530.8, "target": "IBI-3001", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 411.3, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 10186, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000428, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 5, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.5852, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Takeda Announces Oncology Partnership with Innovent", "url": "https://www.takeda.com/newsroom/newsreleases/2025/innovent/", "domain": "takeda.com", "snippet": "**OSAKA, Japan and CAMBRIDGE, Massachusetts, October 21, 2025** – Takeda (TSE:4502/NYSE:TAK) today announced that it has entered into a license and collaboration agreement with Innovent Biologics (HKEX: 01801) for the development, manufacturing and commercialization of two late-stage oncology medicines, IBI363 and IBI343, worldwide outside of Greater China.\n...\nTakeda will also receive an exclusive option to license global rights outside of Greater China for IBI3001, an early-stage investigational medicine.\n...\nIBI3001 is a potential first-in-class bispecific ADC designed to target both EGFR and B7H3.\nIt is being studied in an ongoing Phase 1 clinical trial in patients with locally advanced or metastatic solid tumors in the U.S., China and Australia.\nAs part of the agreement, Innovent will be solely responsible for clinical development of IBI3001 prior to potential exercise of the option to license.\nShould Takeda exercise the option, Takeda will develop, manufacture and commercialize IBI3001 worldwide, outside of Greater China.\n...\nIBI3001 is a potential first-in-class bispecific antibody-drug conjugate (ADC) that comprises a bispecific antibody targeting EGFR and B7H3 antigens and an exatecan payload.\n...\nTakeda will also receive an exclusive option to license global rights outside of Greater China for IBI3001, an early-stage investigational medicine.\n...\nIt is being studied in an ongoing Phase 1 clinical trial in patients with locally advanced or metastatic solid tumors in the U.S., China and Australia.", "date": "2025-10-21", "last_updated": "2026-10-02", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.97, "evidence": 0.6033}, "jev_score": 0.5852, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "IBI3001 / Innovent Biologics, Takeda, Fortvita ...", "url": "https://delta.larvol.com/Products/?ProductId=9b437310-e3ab-4b3f-93e2-3f9bb486584c", "domain": "delta.larvol.com", "snippet": "## Safety and efficacy of IBI3001, an anti-EGFR/B7H3 bispecific ADC, in patients (pts) with advanced solid tumors: Results of a multi-regional phase 1 study (ESMO 2026) - No abstract available\n...\n## Innovent Biologics Announces Closing of Global Strategic Partnership with Takeda for Next-Generation IO and ADC Therapies (PRNewswire) - \"Takeda will pay Innovent an upfront payment of US$1.2 billion, including a US$100 million equity investment in Innovent through new share issuance at premium, i.e., HK$112.56 per share. Furthermore, Innovent is eligible for development and sales milestone payments for IBI363, IBI343, and IBI3001 (if option exercised) totaling up to approximately $10.2 billion, for a total deal value of up to $11.4 billion. Innovent is also eligible to receive potential royalty payments for each molecule outside Greater China, except with respect to IBI363 in the U.S....\"\n...\n## IBI3001 (EGFR/B7H3 ADC): Option to Global License for Development and Commercialization (PRNewswire) - \"Innovent will grant Takeda an exclusive option to license global development, manufacturing, and commercialization rights for IBI3001 outside of Greater China. If exercised, Takeda will pay Innovent an exercise fee, potential milestone payments, and tiered royalties on net sales up to mid-teens.\"\n...\n## IBI3001 in Participants With Unresectable, Locally Advanced or Metastatic Solid Tumors (clinicaltrials.gov) - P1 | N=250 | Recruiting | Sponsor: Innovent Biologics (Suzhou) Co. Ltd. | Not yet recruiting ➔ Recruiting | Phase classification: P1/2 ➔ P1 | N=180 ➔ 250 | Trial completion date: May 2027 ➔ Dec 2027 | Initiation date: May 2024 ➔ Dec 2024 | Trial primary completion date: May 2026 ➔ Dec 2026\n...\n## Innovent Biologics’ new dual-antibody ADC cancer drug approved for clinical trials in China [Google translation] (vbdata.cn) - \"Innovent Biologics' new bispecific ADC drug IBI3001 has been approved for clinical trials in China. It targets B7-H3 and EGFR, has multiple anti-tumor mechanisms, shows good efficacy and safety, and has been conducting a Phase 1/2 study in Australia.\"\n...\n## Innovent to Present Preclinical Data of Multiple Novel Molecules at the 2024 AACR Annual Meeting (PRNewswire) - \"IBI3001 has multiple anti-tumor mechanisms of action: (1) enhanced EGFR signaling blockade; (2) EGFR- and B7-H3-aided payload internalization and cytotoxicity; and (3) potent bystander killing effects of ADC. The optimized B7-H3 arm not only enhances the EGFR signaling blockade but also reduces EGFR on-target toxicities. IBI3001 showed strong anti-tumor efficacy in vitro and in vivo across multiple solid tumors and is well tolerated with the therapeutic index at 40.\"", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-10-02", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.97, "evidence": 0.6033}, "jev_score": 0.5852, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Press Release", "url": "https://en.innoventbio.com/InvestorsAndMedia/PressReleaseDetail?key=557", "domain": "en.innoventbio.com", "snippet": "Press Release\nInnovent Biologics Announces Global Strategic Partnership with Takeda to Bring Innovent’s Next Gen IO Backbone Therapy and ADC Molecules to the Global Market\n...\n- Innovent will grant Takeda an exclusive option for the rights for IBI3001 (EGFR/B7H3 ADC) outside Greater China.\n...\n- Innovent to host conference calls and webcasts at 9:00 a.m.\nHKT (Chinese session) and 9:00 p.m.\nHKT (English session) on Wednesday, October 22, 2025.\nSAN FRANCISCO, U.S. and SUZHOU, China, October 22, 2025 — Innovent Biologics (HKEX: 01801) announced a strategic global collaboration with Takeda (TSE:4502, NYSE:TAK) to advance next-generation IO and ADC cancer therapies, with the goal of developing potentially transformative cancer treatments to benefit patients worldwide.\n...\nIBI343, a potentially best-in-class CLDN18.2 ADC currently in Phase 3 clinical stage; and IBI3001, a first-in-class EGFR/B7H3 bispecific ADC currently in Phase 1 clinical stage.\n...\nIBI363 is now in registrational clinical development, including a global Phase 3 study in second line sqNSCLC that is expected to begin in the coming months; the China NMPA has granted Breakthrough Designation (BTD) and U.S. FDA has granted Fast Track Designation (FTD) for this indication.*\n...\nBuilding on its already robust clinical data of over 1,200 treated patients, IBI363 will be initially developed globally in non-small cell lung cancer (“NSCLC”) and colorectal cancer (“CRC”), including in the first-line settings.\n...\nIt has also received Fast Track Designation from the U.S. FDA for the treatment of advanced unresectable or metastatic pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (PDAC) that has relapsed and/or is refractory to one prior line of therapy.*\n...\n*IBI3001, currently in a Phase 1 clinical trial, is a first-in-class bispecific ADC targeting B7-H3 and EGFR.\nIt combines multiple anti-tumor mechanisms, including enhanced EGFR blockade, receptor-mediated internalization, and strong ADC-mediated cytotoxicity, with a high safety margin demonstrated in preclinical models.*\nInnovent will grant Takeda an exclusive option to license global development, manufacturing, and commercialization rights for IBI3001 outside of Greater China.\nIf exercised, Takeda will pay Innovent an exercise fee, potential milestone payments, and tiered royalties on net sales up to mid-teens.\n...\n**Innovent will host conference calls and webcasts at 9:00 a.m.\nHKT (Chinese session) and 9:00 p.m.\nHKT (English session) on Wednesday, October 22, 2025.** *Details of the conference call dial-in and the webcast link will be provided on the company website at * https://investor.innoventbio.com/en/investors/webcasts-and-presentations/*.\nA replay will also be available on the website shortly after the event.*", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-16", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.95, "evidence": 0.6033}, "jev_score": 0.5731, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Press Release", "url": "https://en.innoventbio.com/InvestorsAndMedia/PressReleaseDetail?key=566", "domain": "en.innoventbio.com", "snippet": "Press Release\nInnovent Biologics Announces Closing of Global Strategic Partnership with Takeda for Next-Generation IO and ADC Therapies\n...\nSAN FRANCISCO and SUZHOU, China, Dec. 5, 2025 -- Innovent Biologics (HKEX: 01801) today announced that the global strategic collaboration with Takeda (TSE: 4502, NYSE: TAK) has closed and become effective following the satisfaction of all closing conditions.\nThe collaboration, initially announced on October 22, 2025, aims to accelerate the global development and commercialization of Innovent's next-generation immuno-oncology (IO) and antibody-drug conjugate (ADC) therapies, including the global partnership on IBI363 (PD-1/IL-2^α-bias^) and IBI343 (CLDN18.2 ADC), and an option for an early-stage program IBI3001 (EGFR/B7H3 ADC).\n...\n- Additionally, Takeda receives an exclusive option to license global rights for IBI3001, a first-in-class EGFR/B7H3 bispecific ADC in Phase 1 stage, outside Greater China.\nTakeda will pay Innovent an upfront payment of US$1.2 billion, including a US$100 million equity investment in Innovent through new share issuance at premium, i.e., HK$112.56 per share.\nFurthermore, Innovent is eligible for development and sales milestone payments for IBI363, IBI343, and IBI3001 (if option exercised) totaling up to approximately $10.2 billion, for a total deal value of up to $11.4 billion.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-29", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.95, "evidence": 0.5933}, "jev_score": 0.5636, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Innovent to Present New Clinical Data of Next-gen IO and ...", "url": "https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/innovent-to-present-new-clinical-data-of-next-gen-io-and-adc-pipeline-at-the-2026-esmo-annual-meeting-302887455.html", "domain": "prnewswire.com", "snippet": "Sep 23, 2026, 04:48 ET\nSAN FRANCISCO and SUZHOU, China, Sept. 23, 2026 /PRNewswire/ -- Innovent Biologics, Inc.\n(\"Innovent\") (HKEX: 01801), a world-class biopharmaceutical company that develops, manufactures and commercializes high quality medicines for the treatment of oncology, cardiovascular and metabolic, autoimmune, ophthalmology and other major diseases, announces that clinical data for its IBI363/TAK-928 (PD\n...\nIn addition, we will unveil global multicenter Phase 1 data for the first-in-class IBI3001 (EGFR/B7H3 ADC) for the first time, as well as Phase 2 data for the potentially best-in-class IBI354 (HER2 ADC) in first-line breast cancer.\n...\nPresentation Title: IBI363 (TAK-928) plus chemotherapy as first line (1L) treatment for advanced non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC): Update of a phase 1b study**\n...\n**4.** **Presentation Title: IBI363 (TAK-928) plus bevacizumab (Bev) vs docetaxel (DTX) in patients (pts) with locally advanced or metastatic immunotherapy (IO)-resistant lung adenocarcinoma (LUAC) without actionable genomic alterations: a randomized phase Ib study**\n...\n**5.** **Presentation Title: Safety and efficacy of IBI3001, an anti-EGFR/B7H3 bispecific ADC, in patients (pts) with advanced solid tumors: Results of a multi-regional phase 1 study**\nFinal Publication Number: 1001O\nSession Type: Proffered Paper\nSession Title: Developmental Therapeutics\nSession Date & Time: October 24, 2026 14:45 PM - 16:15 PM CET\nRoom: Cordoba Auditorium - Hall 4", "date": "2026-09-23", "last_updated": "2026-10-02", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.95, "evidence": 0.5833}, "jev_score": 0.5541, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "信達生物製藥 INNOVENT BIOLOGICS, INC. - hkexnews.hk", "url": "https://www1.hkexnews.hk/listedco/listconews/sehk/2025/1022/2025102200031.pdf", "domain": "www1.hkexnews.hk", "snippet": "The Board is pleased to announce that on October 22, 2025 (Hong Kong time), Innovent and \nTakeda (through its wholly-owned subsidiary, Takeda Pharmaceuticals International AG) have established a global strategic collaboration to accelerate the development of Innovent’s next- generation IO and ADC cancer therapies to the global market.\nThe collaboration includes two late-stage investigational medicines IBI363 (PD-1/IL-2α-bias) and IBI343 (CLDN18.2 ADC), as well as an option for an early-stage program IBI3001 (EGFR/B7H3 ADC).\n...\nAdditionally, Takeda will receive an exclusive option for the rights outside of Greater China for IBI3001 (EGFR/\nB7H3 ADC).\n...\nThis collaboration aims to explore and maximize IBI363’s potential as a new IO backbone therapy through aligned co-development plans.\nBuilding on its already robust clinical data of over 1,200 treated patients, IBI363 will be initially developed globally in non-small cell lung cancer (“NSCLC”) and colorectal cancer (“CRC”), including in the first-line settings.\n...\nThe Company has shown that IBI363, with an IL-2 receptor alpha focused approach, selectively expands tumor-specific CD8+ T cells that increase tumor cell killing efficiency without activating or expanding the toxicity related peripheral T cells, which results in a better safety profile than what is seen with traditional IL-2s.\nPhase 1b/2 results presented at ASCO 2025 have demonstrated outstanding tumor responses and preliminary survival benefits of IBI363 across immunotherapy-resistant lung cancer, “cold tumors” such as acral and mucosal melanoma, and microsatellite stable (“MSS”) colorectal cancer.\nIBI363 is now in registrational clinical development, including a global Phase 3 study in second line sqNSCLC that is expected to begin in the coming months; the China NMPA has granted\nBreakthrough Designation (“BTD”) and the U.S. FDA has granted Fast Track Designation \n(“FTD”) for this indication.\n...\nInnovent will grant Takeda an exclusive option to license global development, manufacturing and commercialization rights for IBI3001 outside of Greater China.\nIf exercised, Takeda will pay Innovent an exercise fee, potential milestone payments, and tiered royalties on net sales up to the mid-teens.\n...\nIBI3001, currently in a Phase 1 clinical trial, is the Company’s first-in-class bispecific ADC targeting B7-H3 and EGFR.\nIt combines multiple anti-tumor mechanisms, including enhanced \nEGFR blockade, receptor-mediated internalization, and strong ADC-mediated cytotoxicity, with a high safety margin demonstrated in preclinical models.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-07-13", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.83, "evidence": 0.4967}, "jev_score": 0.4123, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Takeda's Strategic Partnership with Innovent Closes", "url": "https://www.takeda.com/newsroom/newsreleases/2025/closing-partnership-innovent/", "domain": "takeda.com", "snippet": "As part of the agreement, Takeda also has an exclusive option to license global rights to IBI3001, an early-stage investigational medicine, outside of Greater China.\n...\nAs part of the agreement, Takeda also has an exclusive option to license global rights to IBI3001, an early-stage investigational medicine, outside of Greater China.\nTakeda will pay Innovent US$1.2 billion upfront per the terms of the agreement before the end of Takeda’s fiscal year, ending March 31, 2026.", "date": "2025-12-04", "last_updated": "2026-10-02", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.96, "evidence": 0.38}, "jev_score": 0.3648, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Head and Neck Squamous Cell Carcinoma", "url": "https://pro.boehringer-ingelheim.com/us/inoncology/tumor-types/hnscc", "domain": "pro.boehringer-ingelheim.com", "snippet": "For localized HNSCC, the standard of care primarily consists of a surgical approach followed by radiation and adjuvant chemotherapy.^1,4 ^This is often the procedure recommended for tumors of the oral cavity and advanced tumors of the larynx and hypopharynx when functional preservation is not feasible.^1 ^In patients with localized disease with no clinical node involvement, or with only a single node involved, cure rates of over 80% have been achieved by surgery alone.^4^\nDespite the high cure rate for standard of care surgery, about 17-31% of these patients eventually undergo further radiation therapy and/or adjuvant chemotherapy due to disease recurrence.^1^ For high-risk patients with post-surgical extra-nodal extension, close surgical margins, perineural invasion, or poor candidates for surgical management, chemoradiotherapy with single-agent cisplatin is recommended.\n^1,4 ^For recurrent, unresectable, or metastatic disease with no surgical or radiotherapy options, the preferred first-line regimens include pembrolizumab for tumors that express PD-L1 with combined positive score ≥1, and pembrolizumab with platinum (cisplatin or carboplatin) and 5-fluorouracil.", "date": "2022-04-22", "last_updated": "2026-09-30", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.04, "evidence": 0.3033}, "jev_score": 0.0121, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Innovent Bio (1801.HK): Innovent Bio's 6 innovative drugs have positive TCE data for new medical insurance", "url": "https://news.futunn.com/en/post/65944053/innovent-bio-1801-hk-six-innovative-drugs-from-innovent-bio", "domain": "news.futunn.com", "snippet": "Aoyixin (liotinib tablets, EGFR TKI).\nThis time, it has been added to the new national medical insurance drug catalogue: 1.\nFirst-line treatment for adult patients with locally advanced or metastatic non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) with epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR) exon 19 deficiency or exon 21 (L858R) replacement mutation; 2.\nAdult patients with locally advanced or metastatic NSCLC who have previously been treated with EGFR tyrosine kinase inhibitors (TKI) and have been tested to have positive EGFR T790M mutations.\nLiotinib introduces a unique naphthylamine group structure, which is more lipophilic, which ensures that the drug can effectively pass through the blood-brain barrier, thereby significantly reducing the risk of disease progression in patients with brain metastases and the risk of progression of intracranial disease, bringing a more effective choice for patients with EGFR mutations.\n...\n5. Dabrol (tarretinib adipate capsules, ROS1 TKI).\nThis time, it was added to the new national medical insurance drug catalogue: adult patients with locally advanced or metastatic non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) positive for ROS1.\n...\nOn December 8, Innovent Bio first announced preliminary data from the first human trial of its self-developed anti-GPRC5D/BCMA/CD3 tri-specific antibody IBI3003 for patients with recurrent or refractory multiple myeloma (R/R MM) in the form of an oral report at the 2025 American Society of Hematology Annual Meeting (ASH).\nIBI3003 has shown good tolerability and controllable safety characteristics.\nDespite the current short follow-up time, IBI3003 has shown encouraging initial efficacy signals, particularly in high-risk patients with extramedullary lesions or previous single or dual targeted treatments against BCMA and GPRC5D.\n...\nIBI3003 has shown encouraging efficacy and depth of relief at doses of ≥120ug/kg.\n① Encouraging outcomes were observed during median follow-up of 3.25 months: patients treated at least 120 μg/kg (n=24) had an ORR of 83.3%, including 4 CRs, 7 VGPR, and 9 PR cases.\n...\nIBI3003 has controlled safety in R/R MM patients.\nHematologic toxicity is the most common grade ≥3 TEA Es.\nIt mainly occurs during the dose escalation phase, and the extent is controllable and recoverable.\nThe incidence of CRS and immune-effector cell-related neurotoxicity syndrome (ICA NS) was 64.1% and 6.1%, respectively, both grade 1-2, and recovered after treatment; prophylactic use of tocilizumab can reduce the risk of CRS.", "date": "2025-12-09", "last_updated": "2025-12-09", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.04, "evidence": 0.04}, "jev_score": 0.0016, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Innovent", "url": "https://en.innoventbio.com/", "domain": "en.innoventbio.com", "snippet": "Innovent Announces Expansion of Global Phase 3 MarsLight-11 Study of IBI363/TAK-928 (Alpha-biased IL-2/PD-1 Bispecific Fusion Protein) in Immunotherapy-Resistant NSCLC to Include Patients with Non-Squamous NSCLC", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-30", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.02, "evidence": 0.01}, "jev_score": 0.0002, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}], "total_ms": 942.1, "ts": "2026-10-06T12:18:08+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q018-batch", "row_id": "q018", "mode": "batch", "objective": "SPR2015 in vivo animal models and results: cell-line xenograft CDX, patient-derived xenograft PDX, syngeneic, orthotopic, or genetically engineered models", "query": ["SPR2015 HNSCC in vivo xenograft", "SPR2015 PDAC patient-derived xenograft", "SPR2015 PDAC syngeneic model", "SPR2015 PDAC genetically engineered mouse model", "SPR2015 HPAC AsPC-1 tumour volume percentage inhibition"], "anchors": ["SPR2015"], "search_latency_ms": 605.7, "target": "SPR2015", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 524.7, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 9259, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000389, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 5, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.931, "results": [{"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Merck, SciBrunch Launch Up-to-$2.13B Collaboration ...", "url": "https://www.genengnews.com/topics/cancer/merck-scibrunch-launch-up-to-2-13b-collaboration-to-develop-preclinical-kras-inhibiting-molecular-glue/", "domain": "genengnews.com", "snippet": "SPR2015 is a preclinical molecular glue, more specifically an oral KRAS G12D (ON) inhibitor that SciBrunch is developing for forms of cancer that include colorectal cancer (CRC), non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC), and pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (PDAC)—the most common and aggressive type of pancreatic cancer.\n...\nAt the 2026 American Association for Cancer Research (AACR) Annual Meeting, a team of SciBrunch researchers that included founder, chairman, and CEO Tao Hu, PhD, presented preclinical data showing SPR2015 to have demonstrated what it termed compelling antitumor efficacy as a monotherapy across multiple *in vivo* cell-derived and patient-derived xenograft (CDX and PDX) models.\n“SPR2015 is a highly potent and selective KRAS G12D (on) inhibitor with nanomolar proliferation inhibitory activities in various KRAS G12D cell lines while with good selectivity over KRAS wild type,” Hu and SciBrunch researcher colleagues reported in a poster presentation during AACR 2026.\n...\nIn a head-to-head mouse study comparing a single oral once-daily dose at 100 mg/kg of SPR2015 to another unspecified G12D (ON) inhibitor in over 15 CRC CDX or PDX models, SPR2015 showed significantly superior efficacy in the *in vivo* CRC xenograft models, achieving a 64.7% objective response rate (ORR) and 94.1% disease control rate (DCR) as monotherapy.\n...\nIn their announcement, Merck and SciBrunch did not disclose a current timeframe for taking SPR2015 into Phase I.", "date": "2026-09-29", "last_updated": "2026-10-03", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.98, "evidence": 0.95}, "jev_score": 0.931, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "KRAS G12D Inhibitor's $2.13B Deal Tests Cross-Border Tech Transfer", "url": "https://www.pharmtech.com/view/kras-g12d-inhibitor-s-2-13b-deal-tests-cross-border-tech-transfer", "domain": "pharmtech.com", "snippet": "- SPR2015 functions as an ON-state molecular glue, showing nanomolar antiproliferative activity in KRAS G12D cell lines with wild-type selectivity and tumor regression in xenograft models.\n...\nSPR2015 is described as a molecular glue inhibitor that engages the ON form of the mutant protein.^1^ It showed nanomolar antiproliferative activity across multiple KRAS G12D-mutant cell lines while maintaining selectivity over wild-type cells, and it demonstrated monotherapy antitumor efficacy in cell-derived and patient-derived xenograft models.", "date": "2026-09-28", "last_updated": "2026-10-03", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.97, "evidence": 0.8533}, "jev_score": 0.8277, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Merck Licenses Preclinical KRAS G12D Inhibitor from SciBrunch", "url": "https://www.pharmexec.com/view/merck-licenses-preclinical-kras-g12d-inhibitor-scibrunch", "domain": "pharmexec.com", "snippet": "- Preclinical monotherapy produced compelling tumor regressions across CDX and PDX models, with AACR 2026 data emphasizing activity in colorectal cancer models where prior G12D agents have struggled.\n...\nKRAS G12D is the most common oncogenic RAS mutation found in human tumors, arising from a substitution of glycine with aspartate at position 12, a change that leaves KRAS active and continuously signaling for cell proliferation and survival.^1^ SPR2015 has also shown compelling antitumor efficacy as a monotherapy across multiple in vivo cell-derived and patient-derived xenograft models, according to preclinical data presented at the 2026 AACR Annual Meeting.^2^", "date": "2026-09-28", "last_updated": "2026-10-02", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.97, "evidence": 0.7867}, "jev_score": 0.7631, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "MSD takes a $400m bite of SciBrunch's KRAS drug", "url": "https://pharmaphorum.com/news/msd-takes-400m-bite-scibrunchs-kras-drug", "domain": "pharmaphorum.com", "snippet": "The company reported studies at this year's American Association for Cancer Research (AACR) congress which showed antitumour efficacy for SPR2015 as a monotherapy across multiple *in vivo* cell-derived and patient-derived xenograft models.\n...\nKRAS G12D mutations are found in 37% of pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (PDAC) cancers, 13% of colorectal cancer cases, and 4% of non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) cases.", "date": "2026-10-02", "last_updated": "2026-10-02", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.98, "evidence": 0.7567}, "jev_score": 0.7416, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Merck Enters into Exclusive Global License Agreement ...", "url": "https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20260928125137/en/Merck-Enters-into-Exclusive-Global-License-Agreement-with-SciBrunch-Therapeutics-for-SPR2015-an-Investigational-Oral-KRAS-G12D-ON-Inhibitor", "domain": "businesswire.com", "snippet": "SPR2015 is a potent and selective novel investigational preclinical molecular glue KRAS G12D (ON) inhibitor that has demonstrated nanomolar antiproliferative activities in various KRAS G12D-mutant cell lines while maintaining good selectivity over KRAS wildtype cells.\n...\nIn addition, SPR2015 has demonstrated compelling antitumor efficacy as a monotherapy across multiple in vivo cell-derived and patient-derived xenograft (CDX/PDX) models, as reported in preclinical data presented at the 2026 AACR Annual Meeting.", "date": "2026-09-28", "last_updated": "2026-10-02", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.98, "evidence": 0.73}, "jev_score": 0.7154, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Genetically Engineered Mouse Models of Pancreatic Cancer", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3594661/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "While different genetic and chemical approaches to generate pancreatic cancer in mice and other rodents date back to the early 1980s^6-8^, it was the generation of the K-ras^LSL.G12D^ mouse^9, 10^ in 2001 that allowed tissue-specific expression of mutant K-ras under physiological control from the endogenous mouse locus.\nThe backbone of this mouse is a targeting construct containing a transcriptional and translational silencing cassette (STOP) flanked by two loxP sites, residing upstream of exon 1 in the K-ras gene.\nThis “LSL cassette” prevents expression of the allele, which also contains an additional G-A transition that yields a glycine to aspartic acid substitution in codon 12, the most commonly mutated residue K-ras in human PDAC.\nThis mutation alters the intrinsic GTPase activity of K-ras,^11-13^ as well as its affinity for GAPs, locking the protein into the inactive, GTP-bound configuration.^14^ Expression of the bacterial recombinase *cre* results in removal of the LSL cassette, expression of mutant K-ras^G12D^ from its endogenous locus, and thereby activation of the Ras pathway.\n...\n^17^ By crossing PDX-Cre^18, 19^ or p48-Cre^20^mice to K-ras^LSL.G12D^ mice, the authors targeted expression of K-ras^G12D^specifically to the pancreas.\nSurprisingly, the mice were born with normal pancreatic histology and architecture.\n...\nAguirre et al. found that homozygous deletion of cdkn2a (which encodes the p16^Ink4a^ and p19^Arf^ tumor suppressors) in the context of K-ras mutation in the pancreas led to the rapid development of highly aggressive and metastatic ductal adenocarcinoma, causing 100% lethality at eleven weeks.\n...\nSimilarly, the conditional expression of the tumor-associated p53^R172H^ mutation also accelerated K-ras^G12D^ pancreatic tumorigenesis^21^.\nWhile, born with histologically normal pancreata, K-ras^LSL.G12D/+^; p53^R172H/+^; PdxCre mice (now referred to as KPC mice) developed PanIN lesions on an accelerated schedule, and died of PDAC with a median survival of 5.5 months.\n...\nIjichi and colleagues crossed the K-ras^LSL.G12D^ model with a knockout of the TGF-β2-receptor (Tgfbr2), resulting in rapid onset of PanIn formation and progression to PDAC with a median survival of eight weeks.\n...\nA list of genetic combinations that have been reported to develop PDAC (or premalignancies)\n...\n|^78^Elastase-CreERT;K-ras^G12D^|mPanIN|No|<18 months|Acinar derived mPanINs|\n...\n|^80^Elastase-tTA TRE-Cre;K-ras^G12V^|mPanINs, PDAC|No|18 months|PDAC development in adult mice through pancreatitis, inducible|\n...\n|^18^Pdx1-Cre;K-ras^G12D^|mPanIN to PDAC progression|Yes|>12 months|Slow progression|", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-23", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.13, "evidence": 0.5333}, "jev_score": 0.0693, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Mouse Models of Pancreatic Ductal Adenocarcinoma", "url": "https://2024.sci-hub.se/4365/612c5cc4af0de1f0fbfa4f70d2e69969/ponz-sarvise2015.pdf", "domain": "2024.sci-hub.se", "snippet": "�New genetically engineered mouse models are needed to address the genetic heteroge-\nneity seen in human patients.\n...\nTransplantation of human cancer cell lines or primary human tumors into a host mouse to create a xenograft was one of the first models developed to study PDAC.\n...\nXenografts can be derived either from established cell lines or directly from human tumors.\nCells or tumor tissue can be implanted subcutaneously (heterotopically) or into the mouse pancreas (orthotopically).\nEach approach has certain advantages and disadvantages.\n...\nPatient derived xenografts (PDXs) are generated by transplantation of PDAC tumor tissue directly into mice, either subcutaneously or in the subrenal capsule.\nPDX tumors may reflect more of the genetic heterogeneity and stromal abundance present in the original tumor.11 Despite serial passaging in mice, PDX tumors have been shown to maintain some of these features, as well as responses to cytotoxic chemotherapeutic agents.12,13 Like standard xenografts, the PDX approach can be used to generate large numbers of mice, serving as a platform upon which therapeutic candidates can be screened.\n...\nGENETICALLY ENGINEERED MOUSE MODELS\nThe genetically engineered mouse model (GEMM) was developed to address many of the key weaknesses of the xenograft approach.\nThe GEMM attempts to recapitulate the PDAC phenotype by introducing into the mouse pancreas the specific genetic mutations present in human PDAC.\nThe most common of these are activating mutations in KRAS, followed by inactivating mutations in CDKN2A (P16/INK4A),\nTP53,\nand\nSMAD4.8\nSuccessful\nengineering\nof\nthe\nLSL-KrasG12D\nand\nthe\nLSL-KrasG12V-bGeo mouse was a key development that launched the field.16–18\nLSL-KrasG12D Model\nThe original GEMM, the LSL-KrasG12D mouse, was generated by targeting expression of the endogenous oncogenic LSL-KrasG12D allele to the developing pancreas through the use of pancreas-specific Cre recombinase alleles.17 Such PDX1-Cre,\nLSL-KrasG12D, or P481/�Cre, LSL-KrasG12D (KC) GEMMs develop a spectrum of pre- invasive ductal lesions that mirror human pancreatic intraepithelial neoplasia (PanIN), and upon aging, KC mice stochastically develop primary and metastatic PDAC.\nThe\nKC GEMM develops PanIN with 100% penetrance.\nThe median survival of this model is around 1 year, and not all KC mice develop PDAC.\nThe KC GEMM represents a suitable platform for the study of PanIN biology and the development of diagnostic and therapeutic approaches.\n...\nTrp53R172H/- (KPC) GEMM incorporates inactivation of the TP53 suppressor gene, and consequently, rapidly develops PDA.\nThe median survival is approximately\n5 months, and most mice (96%) develop PDAC at the time of death.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-07-31", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.1, "evidence": 0.4367}, "jev_score": 0.0437, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Genetically engineered mouse models of pancreatic ... - PMC", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5528418/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Over the last decade, several models, primarily based on the genetic activation of resident KRas oncogenes knocked‐in within the endogenous KRas locus have been generated.\nThese models faithfully reproduce the histological lesions that characterize human pancreatic tumors.\nDecoration of these models with additional mutations, primarily involving tumor suppressor loci known to be also mutated in human PDAC tumors, results in accelerated tumor progression and in the induction of invasive and metastatic malignancies.\n...\nGenetically engineered mouse models of cancer faithfully reproduce the histological changes characteristic of human PDAC tumors.\n...\nThese mice do not develop any lesions unless they undergo pancreatitis.\n...\n## 2. Genetically engineered mouse (GEM) models of PDAC: prenatal models\n...\nThe first model to faithfully reproduce the natural history of human PDAC in mice involved expression of a resident (knocked‐in) KRasG12D oncogene in all pancreatic lineages during early embryonic development (Hingorani et al., 2003) (Table 1).\n...\nThe resulting strains, designated as Pdx1‐Cre; LSL‐KRasG12D (from now on KC, according to the nomenclature given in the original publication) and Ptf1a‐Cre; LSL‐KRasG12D, express the KRasG12D oncogene in all pancreatic lineages from early embryonic development (E8.5 in KC mice and E9.5 in Ptf1a‐Cre; LSL‐KRasG12D animals).\n...\nIn addition, these mice display ADM, a benign lesion that precedes the appearance and possibly be a precursor of PanIN lesions (Zhu et al., 2007).\nPanIN lesions progress with long latencies to PDAC in a percentage of mice (Table 1).\nAddition of mutations in loci encoding tumor suppressors known to be mutated or inactivated in human PDAC such as INK4A, TP53, LKB1 or SMAD4 accelerate the progression of these PanIN lesions leading to the generation of invasive PDAC with complete penetrance (Table 2).\n...\nList of current GEM models of PDAC.\n|GEM model|Lesions|Metastasis|Survival|References|\n|--|--|--|--|--|\n|Pdx1‐Cre; K‐Ras+/LSLG12D; Ink4a/Arflox/lox|PanIN and PDAC|Yes|2 m|Aguirre et al., 2003|\n|Pdx1‐Cre; K‐Ras+/LSLG12D; p53R172H/+(KPC)|PanIN and PDAC|Yes|5 m|Hingorani et al., 2005|\n...\n|Pdx1‐Cre; K‐Ras+/LSLG12D; Brca2Tr/Δ11|PanIN and PDAC|NA|NA|Skoulidis et al., 2010|\n|Pdx1‐Cre; K‐Ras+/LSLG12D; p53R270H/+; Brca2Tr/+|PanIN and PDAC|NA|<5 m|Skoulidis et al., 2010|\n...\nAs indicated above, addition of mutations in Ink4a and TP53 tumor suppressors increase the penetrance of PDAC to 100% of the animals and significantly reduce tumor latency, inducing death of all animals by 6–8 month of age (Guerra et al., 2011) (Table 2).\n...\nThese GEM models illustrate that the alteration in the TGF‐α and TGF‐β pathways result in generation of cystic neoplasms and PanINs, which progress to PDAC.\n...\nIn GEM models of PDAC, pancreatitis appears to be an essential component for tumor development in adult mice.\n...\nGEM models available to date faithfully reproduce the histological changes that lead to the formation of PDAC tumors, specially those combining expression of resident KRas oncogenes with mutations in tumor suppressors known to be mutated in the corresponding human tumors, mainly P16INK4a, TP53 and SMAD4.\nIndeed, some of these GEM models also result in the generation of metastatic tumors whose behavior closely resembles that of their human counterparts.", "date": "2013-02-11", "last_updated": "2026-09-25", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.07, "evidence": 0.35}, "jev_score": 0.0245, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Current methods in mouse models of pancreatic cancer", "url": "https://mdanderson.elsevierpure.com/en/publications/current-methods-in-mouse-models-of-pancreatic-cancer-3/", "domain": "mdanderson.elsevierpure.com", "snippet": "Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (PDAC) is the fourth leading cause of cancer death in the Western world.\n...\nIn the past several years, there has been an explosion of genetically engineered mouse models (GEMM) and patient-derived xenografts (PDX) that recapitulate both genetic and morphological alterations that lead to the development of PDAC.\nBoth models are increasingly used for characterization and validation of diagnostic and therapeutic strategies.\nIn this chapter we will discuss state-of-the-art models to consider when selecting an appropriate in vivo system to study disease etiology, cell signaling, and drug development.\n...\n|Pages (from-to)|185-211|\n|Number of pages|27|\n|Journal|Methods in Molecular Biology|\n|Volume|1267|\n|DOIs|- https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4939-2297-0_9|\n|State|Published - 2015|\n...\nMazur, P. K., Herner, A., Neff, F., & Siveke, J. T. (2015).\nCurrent methods in mouse models of pancreatic cancer.\n*Methods in Molecular Biology*, *1267*, 185-211.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-10-01", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.06, "evidence": 0.3667}, "jev_score": 0.022, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "CCDI Hub", "url": "https://moleculartargets.ccdi.cancer.gov/studies/phs003215", "domain": "moleculartargets.ccdi.cancer.gov", "snippet": "## Warning\n...\nThis system is provided for Government-authorized use only.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-19", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.11, "evidence": 0.0133}, "jev_score": 0.0015, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}], "total_ms": 1130.4, "ts": "2026-10-06T12:18:08+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q020-batch", "row_id": "q020", "mode": "batch", "objective": "Inflammatory Bowel Disease competing agents in the same mechanistic class as JTO2, including Monoclonal Antibody and CXCL10 therapies", "query": ["JTO2 CXCL10 inflammatory bowel disease competitors", "JTO2 anti-CXCL10 monoclonal antibody IBD pipeline", "JTO2 CXCL10 clinical trial inflammatory bowel disease"], "anchors": ["JTO2"], "search_latency_ms": 811.8, "target": "drugs competing with JTO2: other agents in the same mechanistic class or for the same indication, as described in `objective`", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 369.1, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 9688, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000407, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 5, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.7055, "results": [{"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Advancing therapeutic frontiers: a pipeline of novel drugs ...", "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/gastroenterology/articles/10.3389/fgstr.2026.1747118/full", "domain": "frontiersin.org", "snippet": "|IP-10 antagonist|Eldelumab (BMS-936557)|Fully human monoclonal IgG1 antibody targeting IP-10/CXCL10|NCT00656890, NCT01294410|Phase 2, Phase 2b|Completed; failed to meet primary endpoints|\n...\n|CCL11 antagonist|Bertilimumab|Recombinant human IgG4 monoclonal antibody neutralizing eotaxin-1|NCT01671956|Phase 2|Status unknown as of Jan 2018; remained as recruiting|\n...\nAbrilumab, a human monoclonal IgG2 antibody targeting α4β7 integrin, was evaluated in two Phase 2 studies for moderate-to-severe UC.\n...\nLeiolizumab is a tetravalent agonist IgG1 antibody targeting PSGL-1 being evaluated in a Phase 2a, multicenter, single-arm, open-label study (NCT06109441) for moderately to severely active UC refractory to biologic therapy.\n...\nBertilimumab is a recombinant human IgG4 monoclonal antibody that neutralizes human eotaxin-1 (CCL11), a chemokine involved in eosinophil recruitment and activation.\n...\nCU104 is a new oral investigational drug that specifically inhibits IL1β, IL-6, and TNFα (69).\nIt is being studied in a double-blind, placebo-controlled, multi-center Phase 2 RCT (NCT05907330) designed to evaluate its efficacy and safety in patients with moderate to severe UC.", "date": "2026-01-30", "last_updated": "2026-09-29", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.85, "evidence": 0.83}, "jev_score": 0.7055, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Cell Trafficking Interference in Inflammatory Bowel Disease", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6327230/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "We discuss the targets that have been explored in clinical trials: chemokines and its receptors (eg, IP10, CCR9), integrins (eg, natalizumab, AJM300, vedolizumab, and etrolizumab), and its endothelial ligands (MAdCAM-1, ICAM-1).\n...\n|CXCL10|CXCR3|Leukocyte recruitment to inflamed intestine.|\n...\nEldelumab is a fully human monoclonal antibody against Interferon-γ-inducible protein-10 (IP-10).\nIP-10 or CXCL10 is a chemokine that mediates trafficking of activated T cells, dendritic cells and monocytes to the inflamed colon.\nIts efficacy was evaluated in both UC (n = 252) and CD (n = 121).\nIn both, eldelumab did not demonstrate efficacy over placebo.^19, 20, 21^\n...\nThese early results are indicative of the efficacy of etrolizumab in treating CD.^47^ Currently, phase 3 clinical trials are ongoing in CD and UC, evaluating etrolizumab safety and efficacy.\nAn ongoing clinical trial in UC will compare the effectiveness of etrolizumab with anti-TNF.^48, 49, 50, 51^\n...\nPTG-100 (Protagonist) is a novel oral α4β7 antagonist peptide with minimal systemic absorption and distribution largely restricted to the gut.\nPTG-100 was evaluated in patients with moderate to severe ulcerative colitis in the phase 2b PROPEL study and was discontinued because it did not meet its primary endpoint of clinical remission.^53, 54^\n...\nMAdCAM-1 Blockade (PF-00547659/SHP647) is a humanized monoclonal antibody against MAdCAM-1 (the endothelial ligand for integrin α4β7).\nRecently, the results of the TURANDOT—a phase 2, randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled trial in patients with UC—have been reported.\n...\n^65^ The Opera II was a 72-week, phase 2 extension study designed to assess the long safety and efficacy of SHP647 (Shire designation).\nA total of 268 patients were enrolled, and 149 completed the study.\n...\nClinicalTrial.gov.\nNCT00457171 GI-270384 Study In Patients With Mild To Moderate Ulcerative Colitis.\nhttps://clinicaltrials.gov/ct2/show/NCT00457171?\nterm=selectin&cond=ulcerative+colitis&rank=1.", "date": "2018-08-24", "last_updated": "2025-10-19", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.91, "evidence": 0.7633}, "jev_score": 0.6946, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Targeting Immune Cell Trafficking – Insights From Research Models ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8129496/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Treatment with anti-CXCL10 antibodies attenuated colitis in IL10-deficient mice and in DSS colitis and reduced cell infiltration to the lamina propria (73–76).\nIn clinical studies, treatment with a monoclonal antibody against CXCL10 was efficient for the treatment of rheumatoid arthritis (MDX-1100) (77).\nHowever, blocking CXCL10 with the antibody eldelumab failed to induce remission in patients with moderate-to-severe UC or CD (78–80).\nStill, in subgroups of anti-TNFα naïve patients, CXCL10 blockade ameliorated mucosal response, suggesting that this treatment could be effective in selected patients (66).\n...\nHowever, to our knowledge, no CCL20 antagonist has been used in clinical trials of IBD so far.\n...\nAiming to expand the clinically successful anti-α4β7 strategy, the humanized monoclonal antibody etrolizumab was developed to target the β7-subunit of α4β7 as well as αE (CD103) β7 integrin hetereodimers (110).\n...\n|CXCL10|Epithelium|Eldelumab|Monoclonal antibody|IL-10^-/-^ and piroxicam colitis mouse model (74)|Yes|UC (Phase II) (78)|IV|No|Improvement of IBDQ score|\n|CXCL10|Epithelium|Eldelumab|Monoclonal antibody|DSS colitis mouse model (75)|Yes|UC (Phase II) (80)|IV|No|N/A|\n|CXCL10|Epithelium|Eldelumab|Monoclonal antibody|IL-10^-/-^ colitis mouse model (76)|Yes|CD (Phase II) (79)|IV|No|Numerically higher remission and response rates|", "date": "2021-05-04", "last_updated": "2026-08-30", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.86, "evidence": 0.7533}, "jev_score": 0.6478, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Product Pipeline Summary", "url": "https://jyanttech.com/index.php?/pipeline", "domain": "jyanttech.com", "snippet": "|Product (Target)|Indication|Pre-Clin.|Phase 1|Phase 2|Phase 3|\n|--|--|--|--|--|--|\n|JT02 (CXCL10)|IBD|\n|JT03 (CXCL13)|RA, Solid Tumors|\n|JT04 (CCL25)|Solid Tumors|\n|JT05 (CXCL16)|Solid Tumors|\n|JT06 (CXCL11)|MS|\n|JT07 (CXCR3 CXCR7)|IBD, Solid Tumors|\n|JT08 (CXCR5)|Solid Tumors|\n|Nanocisplatin|Solid Tumors|\n|Nanoflavopiridol|HRPC|\n|Nanocurcumin|RA, Solid Tumors|\n|Nanoresveratrol|Solid Tumors|\nDiagnostic Product Pipeline (coming soon)", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-02-09", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.83, "evidence": 0.5767}, "jev_score": 0.4787, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Optimising Monoclonal Antibody Therapies for Inflammatory Bowel ...", "url": "https://www.dovepress.com/optimising-monoclonal-antibody-drug-development-for-inflammatory-bowel-peer-reviewed-fulltext-article-CEG", "domain": "dovepress.com", "snippet": "**Abstract:** Despite the success of monoclonal antibody (mAb) therapies in treating inflammatory bowel disease (IBD), a persistent therapeutic ceiling remains.\nThis comprehensive review explores emerging strategies to enhance the efficacy of mAb-based treatments.\nKey among these is the development of bispecific antibodies designed to simultaneously engage two cytokine targets, offering dual blockade of inflammatory pathways and the potential for synergistic effects.\nCo-formulation approaches, comprising two or more mAbs within a single therapeutic product, are also examined as a means of broadening immunologic coverage and streamlining delivery.\nFinally, advances in pharmacokinetic optimisation are discussed, including Fc region modifications, polyethylene glycol conjugation, albumin fusion, and glycoengineering, all aimed at reducing immunogenicity and extending half-life.\n...\n### Modifying the Fc Region\n...\n^22,23^ There are no current afucosylated mAbs licenced for IBD or immune mediated inflammatory disorders (IMIDs) but given the feasibility of the technology and the demonstrated impact upon NK cells via enhanced ADCC activity, it remains an area of interest.\n...\nAll three of vedolizumab, mirikizumab and risankizumab are licenced for IBD and contain such Fc modifications and demonstrate the plausibility of this technology for real-world practice.^65^ There are several other types of mutations or modifications, such as the STR mutation in the CH2 domain of the Fc, that offer promise in Fc engineering of mAbs to reduce immunogenic potential.^65^\n...\nSeveral bsAbs targeting various cytokines are currently in development (Table 2).\n...\nTwo novel bsAbs targeting TNFα and IL-23 are currently in development, with Phase 2 trials anticipated.^102,103^\n...\nFurther bsAbs targeting TL1A are in the pipeline.\nHXN1002 targets both TL1A and α4β7 integrin, which mediates the homing of lymphocytes to gut-associated lymphoid tissue via binding to MAdCAM-1 on endothelial cells.^110^ Preclinical studies presented by SPYRE Therapeutics in 2025 showed enhanced efficacy of dual inhibition in murine colitis models, and a Phase 2 study evaluating the extended half-life antibodies as single agents and pairwise combinations that started in 2025.^111^", "date": "2025-09-30", "last_updated": "2026-10-01", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.76, "evidence": 0.5033}, "jev_score": 0.3825, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "UCSF Inflammatory Bowel Disease Clinical Trials for 2026 ...", "url": "https://clinicaltrials.ucsf.edu/inflammatory-bowel-disease", "domain": "clinicaltrials.ucsf.edu", "snippet": "# Inflammatory Bowel Disease clinical trials at UCSF\n27 in progress, 11 open to eligible people\n...\nAll ages Under 18 Over 18\n- ## Adverse Events, Change in Disease Activity, and How Intravenous and Subcutaneous Risankizumab Moves Through the Body of Pediatric Participants With Moderately to Severely Active Crohn's Disease\nopen to eligible people ages 2-17\n...\nThis study will assess the pharmacokinetics, efficacy, and safety of risankizumab in pediatric participants with moderately to severely active CD aged 2 to < 18 years old who have had intolerance or inadequate response to other therapies.\n...\nThe CAMEO study has been started to help understand what factors are important in determining whether a child with CD completely heals the inflammation after anti-TNF therapy.\nThe investigators will do that by measuring certain markers of inflammation in the blood and stool and by looking at a person's genes (DNA) and how inflammation is controlled in the intestine.\n...\nThis study will assess how safe and effective oral Upadacitinib is in treating moderately to severely active Crohn's Disease in pediatric participants aged 2 to 18 years old who have had inadequate response, loss of response, intolerance, or medical contraindications to corticosteroids, immunosuppressants, and/or biologic therapy.\n...\nThe purpose of this study is to determine the safety, efficacy, and pharmacokinetics (PK) of etrasimod for the treatment of moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis in adolescents (≥ 12 years up to < 18 years of age).\n...\nThis research study is testing an investigational research product called TRX103 as a possible treatment for individuals suffering from Crohn's Disease (CD).\nThe primary purpose of this study is to learn how safe and effective different doses of TRX103 are when administered to individuals with CD.\n...\nA multicenter study to evaluate the efficacy and safety of maintenance and long-term treatment administration of upadacitinib, an orally administered Janus kinase 1 inhibitor, in adult participants with Crohn's Disease.", "date": "2026-09-24", "last_updated": "2026-10-01", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.64, "evidence": 0.4867}, "jev_score": 0.3115, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Delving into the Latest Updates on JT-02(Jyant Technologies) with Synapse", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/drug/b291dac8a0e24306a1bb17fa4b94e798", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "|Drug Type Monoclonal antibody|\n|--|\n|Synonyms|\n|Target CXCL10|\n|Action antagonists|\n|Mechanism CXCL10 antagonists(C-X-C motif chemokine ligand 10 antagonists)|\n|Therapeutic Areas Digestive System Disorders|\n|Active Indication Inflammatory Bowel Diseases|\n|Inactive Indication-|\n|Originator Organization JYANT Technologies, Inc.|\n...\n|Active Organization JYANT Technologies, Inc.|\n...\n|Drug Highest PhasePhase 2|\n...\n|Indication|Highest Phase|Country/Location|Organization|Date|\n|--|--|--|--|--|\n|Inflammatory Bowel Diseases|Phase 2|United States|JYANT Technologies, Inc.|30 Jan 2022|\n...\n|Study|Phase|Population|Analyzed Enrollment|Group|Results|Evaluation|Publication Date|\n|--|--|--|--|--|--|--|--|\n| | | | | | | | |\n|--|--|--|--|--|--|--|--|\n|No Data|No Data|No Data|No Data|No Data|No Data|No Data|No Data|\n...\nIdentify the latest clinical trials across global registries.", "date": "2025-03-20", "last_updated": "2026-09-28", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.62, "evidence": 0.4333}, "jev_score": 0.2686, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Landscape of new drugs and targets in inflammatory bowel disease", "url": "https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/ueg2.12305", "domain": "onlinelibrary.wiley.com", "snippet": "lthough UST (an inhibitor of p40 subunit) has been shown to be effective in IBD,^8^ the p19 antagonists (selective IL-23 inhibitors) have proven to be more effective than UST in other immune-mediated conditions, leading us to evaluate them in IBD.^15, 16^ Four IL-23 p19 inhibitors are currently being evaluated in IBD: risankizumab, guselkumab, brazikumab and mirikizumab.", "date": "2022-12-01", "last_updated": "2026-09-28", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.46, "evidence": 0.54}, "jev_score": 0.2484, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Clinical Trials | Jill Roberts Center for Inflammatory Bowel ...", "url": "https://jillrobertsibdcenter.weillcornell.org/research-and-clinical-trials/clinical-trials", "domain": "jillrobertsibdcenter.weillcornell.org", "snippet": "The Jill Roberts Center for Inflammatory Bowel Disease (JRC) has an active clinical trials and translational research program.\nSee below for a list of actively recruiting clinical trials and translational research trials.\nPatients who are interested in participating in a clinical trial should contact Fatiha Chabouni at (212) 746-5109.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-07-20", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.05, "evidence": 0.01}, "jev_score": 0.0005, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Cloudflare", "url": "https://www.thelancet.com/journals/lanrhe/article/PIIS2665-9913(23)00274-6/fulltext", "domain": "thelancet.com", "snippet": "Cloudflare is currently unable to resolve your requested domain (www.thelancet.com).\nThere are two potential causes of this:", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-11-03", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.02, "evidence": 0.0}, "jev_score": 0.0, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}], "total_ms": 1180.9, "ts": "2026-10-06T12:18:08+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q022-batch", "row_id": "q022", "mode": "batch", "objective": "competing agents in the same mechanistic class for this indication Novel UC Treatment, Cosmo Health, Distal Ulcerative Colitis", "query": ["Novel UC Treatment mechanism of action ulcerative colitis", "Novel UC Treatment target mechanistic class UC", "Novel UC Treatment Phase 2 competitor landscape ulcerative colitis"], "anchors": ["Novel UC Treatment"], "search_latency_ms": 578.8, "target": "drugs competing with Novel UC Treatment: other agents in the same mechanistic class or for the same indication, as described in `objective`", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 362.8, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 10337, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000434, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 8, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.6293, "results": [{"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Emerging Therapies for Ulcerative Colitis: Updates from Recent ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10441644/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "There are currently multiple emerging agents in the pipeline for the management of UC.\nThis includes agents with alternative routes of administration such as oral or subcutaneous tumor necrosis factor inhibitors or novel mechanisms of action such as toll-like receptor 9 (TLR9) agonist cobitolimod and phosphodiesterase 4 inhibitor apremilast.\n...\nThese small molecules include Janus Kinase (JAK) inhibitors such as tofacitinib, upadacitinib and filgotinib;\n...\n|Drug Class|Agent|Target|Mode of Delivery|Stage|\n|--|--|--|--|--|\n|**Anti-TNF**|CT-P13|TNF|SC|Phase III completed|\n|**Anti-TNF**|OPRX-106|TNF|Oral|Phase II completed|\n|**JAK inhibitors**|Ivarmacitinib|JAK1|Oral|Phase II completed/Phase III recruiting|\n|**JAK inhibitors**|Brepocitinib (PF-06700841)|JAK1/TYK2|Oral|Phase IIb completed|\n...\n|**Anti-Trafficking Agents**|PTG-100|α4β7 integrin|Oral|Phase IIa completed (primary endpoint not met)|\n|**IL-23 Inhibitors**|Mirikizumab|IL23/p19 subunit|IV, SC|Phase III completed|\n|**IL-23 Inhibitors**|Risankizumab|IL23/p19 subunit|IV, SC|Phase III completed (results pending)|\n|**IL-23 Inhibitors**|Brazikumab|IL23/p19 subunit|IV, SC|Development Halted|\n...\n|**S1P Receptor Modulators**|Amiselimod (MT-1303)|S1PR1|Oral|Phase II recruiting|\n|**PDE4 Inhibitors**|Apremilast|PDE4|Oral|Phase II completed|\n|**TLR9 Agonist**|Cobitolimod|TLR9|Topical (enema)|Phase IIb completed/Phase III recruiting|\n...\nEtrolizumab is an SC humanized monoclonal antibody which targets the β7 subunit of the α4β7 and αEβ7 integrins with high affinity.25 It blocks binding to their ligands MAdCAM-1 and E-cadherin resulting in inhibition of leukocyte trafficking to the gut and inhibition of retention of leukocytes, respectively.26 Studies on animal models indicate that the medication selectively targets lymphocyte homing on mucosal surfaces with no impact on migration to non-mucosal tissues.26\n...\nHCA2969, the active metabolite of AJM300, selectively inhibits binding of α4 integrin (α4β7/α4β1) to the cell adhesion molecules.31 It shares the same mechanism of action as natalizumab.\n...\nAbrilumab (AMG 181 or MEDI 7183) is a human monoclonal IgG2 antibody which targets α4β7 integrin on gut-tropic lymphocytes.\n...\nMirikizumab is an IgG4-variant monoclonal antibody that binds to the p19 subunit of IL23.\n...\nCobitolimod is a topical toll-like receptor 9 (TLR9) agonist.\nIt results in the activation of TLR9 on lymphocytes and antigen presenting cells which lead to induction of regulatory T-cells, eventual production of IL-10 and suppression of TH-17 cells.", "date": "2023-08-17", "last_updated": "2026-08-12", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.87, "evidence": 0.7233}, "jev_score": 0.6293, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Advancing therapeutic frontiers: a pipeline of novel drugs for ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12952356/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Emerging mechanisms include next-generation Janus kinase and tyrosine kinase 2 inhibitors with enhanced selectivity, novel cell trafficking modulators, advanced tumor necrosis factor-alpha inhibition strategies, and selective interleukin-23 pathway antagonists.\n...\nMore recently, oral small molecule therapies including Janus kinase (JAK) inhibitors (tofacitinib, upadacitinib, filgotinib) and sphingosine-1-phosphate receptor (S1PR) modulators (ozanimod, etrasimod) have expanded therapeutic options (22–26).\n...\nThis pipeline encompasses novel mechanisms of action targeting diverse inflammatory pathways, including advanced TNF-α inhibition strategies, next-generation JAK and Tyrosine Kinase 2 (TYK2) inhibitors, cell trafficking modulators, IL-23 pathway antagonists, and innovative approaches targeting costimulation, checkpoint pathways, and tissue repair mechanisms.\n...\n|JAK Inhibitor|Ivarmacitinib (SHR0302)|Selective JAK1 inhibitor blocking JAK-STAT pathway|NCT03675477, NCT05181137|Phase 2, Phase 3|Phase 2 completed Feb 2021; Phase 3 status unknown|\n...\n|TYK2 Inhibitor|Zasocitinib (TAK-279)|AI-designed highly selective allosteric TYK2 inhibitor|NCT06254950, NCT06764615|Phase 2|Recruiting; estimated completion Aug 2027|\n...\nIvarmacitinib (SHR0302) is a selective JAK1 inhibitor designed to block the JAK-signal transducer and activator of transcription pathway, thereby controlling inflammation in UC.\n...\nUpon binding to S1PR1 on lymphocyte surfaces, these agents cause persistent receptor internalization and proteasome-mediated degradation, resulting in functional downregulation (43).\n...\nThese investigational agents represent mechanistically heterogeneous approaches that can be conceptually organized into several categories: immune checkpoint modulation strategies targeting co-stimulatory and co-inhibitory pathways (PD-1 agonism with rosnilimab, CD40 antagonism with ravagalimab, CD28-CD80/86 blockade with abatacept, OX40 antagonism with KHK4083), which aim to restore immune homeostasis by modulating T cell activation thresholds;\n...\nSeveral mechanistic classes demonstrate particular promise.\nTL1A pathway inhibitors represent the most compelling novel target, with phase 2 data showing clinical remission rates exceeding 25% versus less than 2% for placebo across both treatment-naïve and treatment-experienced populations.\nNext-generation selective JAK1 and TYK2 inhibitors aim to preserve efficacy while mitigating safety concerns through enhanced selectivity.", "date": "2026-01-30", "last_updated": "2026-09-25", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.85, "evidence": 0.7367}, "jev_score": 0.6262, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Ulcerative Colitis: Today, Tomorrow, and the Future", "url": "https://www.emjreviews.com/gastroenterology/symposium/ulcerative-colitis-today-tomorrow-and-the-future/", "domain": "emjreviews.com", "snippet": "Emerging therapies for UC currently undergoing Phase III development fall under four main mechanisms of action: leukocyte retention in lymphoid organs by S1P receptor modulators (etrasimod and ozanimod), IL-23 inhibitors (mirikizumab, guselkumab, brazikumab, and risankizumab), JAK/TYK2 inhibitors (filgotinib and upadacitinib), and leukocyte adhesion inhibitors (integrin blockers and etrolizumab).^35^\n...\nThe leukocyte adhesion inhibitor etrolizumab selectively targets the β7 subunit of both α4β7 and αEβ7 integrins and blocks interactions with their respective ligands, mucosal vascular addressin cell adhesion molecule 1 and E-cadherin, to reduce gut-specific lymphocyte trafficking to the inflamed colon.", "date": "2020-12-01", "last_updated": "2026-09-22", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.89, "evidence": 0.65}, "jev_score": 0.5785, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "[PDF] Advances in the Treatment of Ulcerative Colitis—From Conventional ...", "url": "https://pdfs.semanticscholar.org/75a0/e296f6c60ceffcd0af16826ef7f1d11558b9.pdf", "domain": "pdfs.semanticscholar.org", "snippet": "Its therapeutic effect is primarily local, targeting\nthe epithelial cells of the colonic mucosa and modulating various inflammatory path- ways [21].\n...\nOverall [25,26], the mechanism of action of glucocorticoids allows for the control of the inflammatory process in the large intestine and modulation of the immune response.\nIn the context of IBD, reducing the expression of adhesion molecules plays a key role, which can limit the migration of inflammatory cells to diseased sites [27].\n...\nThe mechanism of action of IFX in the context of IBD focuses on reducing the expression of cytokines and inflammatory mediators in intestinal tissues.\n...\nVedolizumab is a humanized monoclonal antibody that aims to limit lymphocyte migration toward the intestines by specifically binding to the α4β7 heterodimer found on the surface of intestinal lymphocytes.\n...\nVedolizumab’s mechanism of action is primarily based on disrupting the transport of T cells and leukocytes, which involves the adhesion, activation, and migration of these molecules across vascular walls.\n...\nThe main target in the mechanism of action of golimumab is binding to human TNF and neutralizing the expression of adhesion molecules, including E-selectin, VCAM-1, and\nICAM-1, on the surface of vascular endothelium.\n...\nIts mechanism of action is based on binding to the p40 subunit of IL-12 and IL-23, which inhibits interaction with the IL-12 receptor, its signaling, and subsequent activation of Th1 cells.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-03-25", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.86, "evidence": 0.5767}, "jev_score": 0.496, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Emerging Therapies in Inflammatory Bowel Disease - PMC - NIH", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12428888/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Anti-TNF agents, including infliximab, adalimumab, and certolizumab pegol, represent the most extensively studied biologic class in IBD, targeting TNF-α, a key cytokine in the pathogenesis of both Crohn’s disease (CD) and ulcerative colitis (UC).\n...\nAnti-integrin therapy includes vedolizumab, a monoclonal antibody that selectively blocks α4β7 integrin, thereby preventing lymphocyte trafficking to the gut mucosa through a mechanism that specifically targets gut-homing immune cells while preserving systemic immune function.\n...\nAnti-IL-12/23 therapy is represented by ustekinumab, which targets the p40 subunit shared by IL-12 and IL-23, modulating Th1 and Th17 pathways implicated in both CD and UC through a mechanism that addresses key cytokine networks central to IBD pathogenesis.\n...\nBy inhibiting the JAK-STAT signaling pathway, tofacitinib blocks the transmission of signals from pro-inflammatory cytokines, including interleukins (IL-2, IL-4, IL-6) and interferons (IFN-γ) [49].\n...\n**Sphingosine 1-phosphate receptor (S1PR) modulators** such as ozanimod and etrasimod selectively prevent the egress of lymphocytes from lymph nodes, thereby reducing the inflammatory response in the gut.\n...\nBy selectively inhibiting IL-23, these agents reduce the inflammatory activity of Th17 cells, providing a novel mechanism of action distinct from anti-TNF agents [59].", "date": "2025-08-29", "last_updated": "2026-10-02", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.89, "evidence": 0.5533}, "jev_score": 0.4924, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Current Pharmacologic Options and Emerging Therapeutic ... - PMC", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11402463/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Now, a wide range of therapies has emerged, including alpha-4-integrin blockade agents, anti-CD3 antibodies, probiotic bacterial therapy, epidermal growth factors, etc., which offer the prospect of improved dosing and compliance with traditional molecules.\n...\n^26^ Mesalamine acts on colonic mucosa and reduces inflammation through a diverse process which includes inhibiting the mediators of lipoxygenase and cyclooxygenase, interleukin-1, interleukin-2, and tumor necrosis factor-alpha.\nAlso, it activates the Peroxisome proliferator-activated receptor (PPAR)-gamma, which helps in the transcription of a few key target genes such as nuclear factor B, signal transducers, and activators of transcription that help in the control of intestinal inflammation.^27^\n...\nThese drugs are monoclonal antibodies that act against the TNF-alpha either by neutralizing its biological activity or by interfering with the TNF-alpha attachment to its receptor site.\n...\nThis drug acts by inhibiting the action of integrin, which is a protein involved in neutrophil function and migration by interacting with cellular adhesion molecules to allow for leukocyte adhesion to that vessel wall.\n...\nTofacitibin and Upadacitinib are JAK inhibitors, which act by inhibiting the JAK kinase, thereby inhibiting the cytokine and growth factor receptor signaling.", "date": "2024-09-09", "last_updated": "2026-08-02", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.86, "evidence": 0.5633}, "jev_score": 0.4844, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Practical guidance for managing patients with moderate-to-severe ...", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/jcag/article/7/4/282/7674170", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "snippet": "The emergence of small molecule therapies offers novel treatment options that target mechanisms central to the immunopathology of UC.\nSphingosine-1-phosphate (S1P) receptor modulators and Janus-activated kinase inhibitors are 2 classes of therapies that target unique pathways to attenuate inflammation and modulate the immune response characteristic of UC.\n...\nAdvanced small molecule therapies, such as sphingosine-1-phosphate (S1P) receptor modulators and Janus-activated kinase (JAK) inhibitors, are among the emerging UC treatment options designed to provide patients with alternatives to support long-term treatment goals (Figure 1).\nThese treatment options have novel mechanisms of action for patients to manage symptoms effectively and relatively safely.\n...\nEtrasimod functions by selectively activating S1P receptor subtypes 1, 4, and 5.^26^ The blockade of S1P receptors prevents lymphocytes from being recruited into the colon, allowing the mucosa to heal.^27^", "date": "2024-08-12", "last_updated": "2026-09-26", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.83, "evidence": 0.5367}, "jev_score": 0.4455, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Advancing therapeutic frontiers: a pipeline of novel drugs ...", "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/gastroenterology/articles/10.3389/fgstr.2026.1747118/full", "domain": "frontiersin.org", "snippet": "Emerging mechanisms include next-generation Janus kinase and tyrosine kinase 2 inhibitors with enhanced selectivity, novel cell trafficking modulators, advanced tumor necrosis factor-alpha inhibition strategies, and selective interleukin-23 pathway antagonists.\n...\nThe introduction of biologic therapies targeting tumor necrosis factor-α (TNF-α) (infliximab, adalimumab, golimumab), integrin α4β7 (vedolizumab), and interleukin (IL)-12/23 pathways (ustekinumab, mirikizumab, guselkumab, risankizumab) has transformed treatment paradigms for moderate to severe UC (11, 13–21).\nMore recently, oral small molecule therapies including Janus kinase (JAK) inhibitors (tofacitinib, upadacitinib, filgotinib) and sphingosine-1-phosphate receptor (S1PR) modulators (ozanimod, etrasimod) have expanded therapeutic options (22–26).\n...\nThe therapeutic landscape for UC is rapidly evolving, with an unprecedented number of investigational agents currently in clinical development.\nThis pipeline encompasses novel mechanisms of action targeting diverse inflammatory pathways, including advanced TNF-α inhibition strategies, next-generation JAK and Tyrosine Kinase 2 (TYK2) inhibitors, cell trafficking modulators, IL-23 pathway antagonists, and innovative approaches targeting costimulation, checkpoint pathways, and tissue repair mechanisms.\n...\nIntegrin antagonists block the interaction between α4β7 and MAdCAM-1, preventing lymphocyte adhesion to and transmigration across intestinal endothelium.\n...\nThese investigational agents represent mechanistically heterogeneous approaches that can be conceptually organized into several categories: immune checkpoint modulation strategies targeting co-stimulatory and co-inhibitory pathways (PD-1 agonism with rosnilimab, CD40 antagonism with ravagalimab, CD28-CD80/86 blockade with abatacept, OX40 antagonism with KHK4083), which aim to restore immune homeostasis by modulating T cell activation thresholds;\n...\nTL1A pathway inhibitors represent the most compelling novel target, with phase 2 data showing clinical remission rates exceeding 25% versus less than 2% for placebo across both treatment-naïve and treatment-experienced populations.", "date": "2026-01-30", "last_updated": "2026-09-29", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.88, "evidence": 0.4967}, "jev_score": 0.4371, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Advances in the Treatment of Ulcerative Colitis—From Conventional ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12897911/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Mesalazine is an anti-inflammatory agent with a complex, yet not fully elucidated mechanism of action in UC [20].\nIts therapeutic effect is primarily local, targeting the epithelial cells of the colonic mucosa and modulating various inflammatory pathways [21].\nMesalazine is thought to inhibit the cyclooxygenase and lipoxygenase pathways, thereby reducing the synthesis of pro-inflammatory mediators such as prostaglandins and leukotrienes [22].\nA key component of its activity involves agonism of the peroxisome proliferator-activated receptor gamma (PPAR-), which plays a critical role in regulating intestinal inflammation.\nAdditionally, mesalazine may exert antioxidant effects and contribute to the suppression of T-cell activation and proliferation, further supporting its anti-inflammatory properties [20,22].\n...\nBy increasing the expression of anti-inflammatory genes while simultaneously reducing the expression of pro-inflammatory ones, the GR/GC complex is responsible for anti-inflammatory and immunosuppressive effects, it also reduces the functioning of pro-inflammatory factors, including nuclear factor kappa-light-chain-enhancer of activated B cells (NF-κB) and activator protein 1 (AP-1) [25,26].\n...\nOverall [25,26], the mechanism of action of glucocorticoids allows for the control of the inflammatory process in the large intestine and modulation of the immune response.\nIn the context of IBD, reducing the expression of adhesion molecules plays a key role, which can limit the migration of inflammatory cells to diseased sites [27].\n...\nThe immunosuppressive effects attributed to thiopurines occur through several mechanisms, the most common of which include inhibition of the expression of proinflammatory genes, such as *ITGA4* and tumor necrosis factors [38].\n...\nThe mechanism of action of IFX in the context of IBD focuses on reducing the expression of cytokines and inflammatory mediators in intestinal tissues.\nTNF is extensively produced by immune cells during the inflammatory process and then participates in their migration from blood vessels to diseased sites.\nBy inhibiting TNF with IFX, it is possible to control the symptoms of UC [43].\n...\nAdalimumab (ADA) is another drug belonging to the TNF inhibitor class, this fully human recombinant IgG1 antibody is involved in inhibiting neutrophil activation and leukocyte migration to sites of inflammation.\nBy interacting with TNF, adalimumab induces antibody-dependent cytotoxicity, stimulates the complement system, and induces T-cell apoptosis [47].\n...\nVedolizumab is a humanized monoclonal antibody that aims to limit lymphocyte migration toward the intestines by specifically binding to the α4β7 heterodimer found on the surface of intestinal lymphocytes.\n...\nVedolizumab’s mechanism of action is primarily based on disrupting the transport of T cells and leukocytes, which involves the adhesion, activation, and migration of these molecules across vascular walls.\n...\nThis antibody can inhibit leukocyte migration into the intestine by inhibiting α4β7 and MAdCAM-1 and retain leukocytes in the intestinal epithelium by blocking the interaction between E-cadherin and αEβ7.\n...\nIts mechanism of action is based on binding to the p40 subunit of IL-12 and IL-23, which inhibits interaction with the IL-12 receptor, its signaling, and subsequent activation of Th1 cells.", "date": "2026-02-04", "last_updated": "2026-09-26", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.8, "evidence": 0.51}, "jev_score": 0.408, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Frontiers | Harnessing nature’s pharmacy: investigating natural compounds as novel therapeutics for ulcerative colitis", "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/pharmacology/articles/10.3389/fphar.2024.1394124/full", "domain": "frontiersin.org", "snippet": "**Results:** The results showed that 279 natural compounds treat UC through four main mechanisms, including regulating gut microbiota and metabolites (Mechanism I), protecting the intestinal mucosal barrier (Mechanism II), regulating intestinal mucosal immune response (Mechanism III), as well as regulating other mechanisms (Mechanism Ⅳ) such as cellular autophagy modulation and ferroptosis inhibition.\n...\nThese compounds have been found to have an impact on the makeup of the microbiota and its metabolites, as well as the prevention of colonization by intestinal pathogens and the reduction of the risk of recurrence of ulcerative colitis, as illustrated in Figure 3.\n...\nAs shown in Supplementary Table S1, natural compounds can improve the barrier function of the UC mucosa through multiple perspectives, these include upregulation of the expression of tight junction protein, reduction in the intestinal mucosal, permeability, regulation of the intestinal mucus function, reduction of oxidative stress, and protection of the intestinal epithelial cells (Figure 4).", "date": "2024-08-14", "last_updated": "2026-05-18", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.11, "evidence": 0.3233}, "jev_score": 0.0356, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}], "total_ms": 941.6, "ts": "2026-10-06T12:18:09+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q023-batch", "row_id": "q023", "mode": "batch", "objective": "Inflammatory Bowel Diseases Mild-to-moderate ulcerative colitis Ulcerative Colitis competing agents with the same mechanistic class as Gut Microbiome Dysbiosis, including Live Biotherapeutic Product", "query": ["VE202 Phase 2 current status licensing", "MB310 Phase 2 Microbiotica licensing", "SER-287 ulcerative colitis development status licensing", "EXL01 ReMind Phase 2 licensing Crohn's disease"], "anchors": ["VE202", "MB310", "SER-287", "EXL01"], "search_latency_ms": 533.5, "target": "drugs competing with VE202 / MB310 / SER-287 / EXL01: other agents in the same mechanistic class or for the same indication, as described in `objective`", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 748.2, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 10054, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000422, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 4, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.6, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "SER-287 Drug Asset Due Diligence Report 2026", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/blog/drug-asset-due-diligence-report-ser-287-2026-mcp", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "*Decision date: 11 September 2026.\n...\n**Phase 2**\nHighest phase\n**2**\nRegistered trials\n**2**\nResult records\n**1**\nMatched deals\n## Executive recommendation: CONDITIONAL GO\n### Decision memo\nAdvance with milestone-based economics while efficacy durability, safety differentiation, patent scope, and market positioning are validated.\nThe central underwriting question is whether SER-287 can convert its Live biotherapeutic products profile and Not disclosed biology into clinically meaningful differentiation while preserving an investable safety, IP and commercial position.\n...\n|Asset|SER-287 (query alias: SER-287)|\n|--|--|\n|Modality / target|Live biotherapeutic products; Not disclosed; Bacteria replacements, Microbiome modulators|\n|Highest global status|Phase 2|\n|Originator|Seres Therapeutics, Inc.|\n|Active developers|Aimmune Therapeutics, Inc.|\nThe MCP disease footprint includes Colitis, Ulcerative.\nThe highest-phase flag is a useful orientation point, but the licensing case depends on indication-level evidence and rights, not the global label alone.\n...\n|NCT03759041|Phase 2|Terminated|203|Primary endpoint not disclosed in English source|\n|NCT02618187|Phase 1|Completed|58|Primary endpoint not disclosed in English source|\n...\n### ECO-RESET: A Phase 2B, Randomized, Double-Blind, Placebo-Controlled, Multiple Dose, Multicenter Study to Assess Efficacy and Safety of SER-287 in Adults With Active Mild-to-Moderate Ulcerative Colitis\n**Phase 2**; n=203; evaluation: Not stated in English source.\nReported fields: clinical remission = 8 Pts ; clinical remission = 7 Pts\n...\nSER-287 addresses Colitis, Ulcerative.\n...\nThe MCP screen returned 1 matched transaction record(s) under the scope “asset-specific.”\nPartner activity is a market-validation signal, but it does not establish net present value.\n...\n|2016-01-11|Seres Therapeutics Announces Strategic Collaboration with Nestlé Health Science Bowel Disease Therapies in Markets Outside of North America|Not disclosed|US$120.0M upfront; US$1,900.0M stated total|\n...\n### CONDITIONAL GO\nAdvance with milestone-based economics while efficacy durability, safety differentiation, patent scope, and market positioning are validated.\n**Required pre-signing gates:** reproduce key efficacy analyses; complete an indication-specific safety review; run a full patent-family and freedom-to-operate search; model risk-adjusted economics by territory; and reconcile all rights, sublicenses and encumbrances.", "date": "2026-09-11", "last_updated": "2026-09-11", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.9, "evidence": 0.6667}, "jev_score": 0.6, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "EX-99.1", "url": "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1609809/000119312521149022/d157894dex991.htm", "domain": "sec.gov", "snippet": "*– Topline clinical data from SER-287 Phase 2b study in patients with mild-to-moderate ulcerative *\n*colitis expected in mid-2021, microbiome biomarker data anticipated in H2 2021 – *\n...\n“Our SER-287 ECO-RESET Phase 2b study in mild-to-moderate ulcerative colitis patients is fully enrolled and we anticipate topline clinical results in mid-year.\n...\n**SER-287 Phase 2b ECO-RESET study in ulcerative colitis (UC):** SER-287, an oral microbiome therapeutic candidate consisting of a bacterial consortium, is designed to restructure the gastrointestinal microbiome and reduce inflammation in individuals with UC.\nSeres has obtained FDA Fast Track designation for SER-287 in active mild-to-moderate ulcerative colitis.\nSER-287 targets restructuring the microbiome to reduce proinflammatory activity and modulate UC-relevant inflammatory pathways, potentially providing a much-needed non-immunosuppressive treatment option to patients suffering from ulcerative colitis.\nSER-287 has the potential to be used as both a monotherapy and in combination with other approved agents.\nThe SER-287 Phase 2b ECO-RESET study is a randomized, placebo-controlled, three-arm induction trial that has enrolled 203 patients with active mild-to-moderate ulcerative colitis who have had inadequate response or loss of response on prior therapy.\nIn arm A, patients receive a short course of vancomycin preconditioning followed by ten weeks of the same daily regimen used in the arm of the Phase 1b trial that showed the highest clinical remission rate.\nIn arm B, patients receive vancomycin preconditioning followed by two weeks of the SER-287 daily regimen as in the first arm, followed by eight weeks of a lower dose of SER-287.\nIn arm C, patients receive placebo.\nThe study’s primary endpoint is the proportion of patients achieving clinical remission at ten weeks.\nThe study will assess endoscopic improvement as a secondary endpoint, as well as a range of microbiome and metabolomic measures.\nSeres has\ncompleted enrollment in the SER-287 Phase 2b ECO-RESET induction study in patients with active mild-to-moderate ulcerative colitis and expects to report topline clinical study results in mid-2021, with additional microbiome biomarker data becoming available in H2 2021.\n...\nSeres’ SER-287 program has obtained Fast Track and Orphan Drug designations from the FDA and is being evaluated in a Phase 2b study in patients with active mild-to-moderate ulcerative colitis.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-28", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.91, "evidence": 0.6333}, "jev_score": 0.5763, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "SER-287 - Drug Targets, Indications, Patents", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/drug/985aa1a5aff1423498531f9518d90223", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "|Active Indication Colitis, Ulcerative|\n...\n|License Organization Nestle Health Science SASeres Therapeutics, Inc.|\n|Drug Highest PhasePhase 2|\n...\n|--|--|--|--|--|\n|Colitis, Ulcerative|Phase 2|United States|Seres Therapeutics, Inc.|19 Dec 2018|\n|Colitis, Ulcerative|Phase 2|Canada|Seres Therapeutics, Inc.|19 Dec 2018|\n...\n|NCT03759041 (ECO-RESET, CTgov)|Phase 2|Colitis, Ulcerative|203|Placebo for SER-287 (Placebo (After Placebo Pre-treatment))|zjopgsxadt = xkriqroamm bckpknvwij (uyjdziffpg, wqvnoydebn - qjcbuwcqhx) View more|-|21 Jun 2022|\n...\n|NCT03759041 (ECO-RESET, CTgov)|Phase 2|Colitis, Ulcerative|203|SER-287+Vancomycin Pre-Treatment (SER-287 Induction Dosing (After Vancomycin Pre-treatment))|zjopgsxadt = pjvhacqflt bckpknvwij (uyjdziffpg, mjvnkqkruf - ahxdydjmhw) View more|-|21 Jun 2022|", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-16", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.83, "evidence": 0.5967}, "jev_score": 0.4953, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "VE-202 - Drug Targets, Indications, Patents", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/drug/1fb685bb89f84c16af0a3d32e6f4c385", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "|Originator Organization Janssen Research & Development LLC|\n...\n|Active Organization-|\n|Inactive Organization Vedanta Biosciences, Inc.Janssen Research & Development LLC|\n|License Organization Janssen Biotech, Inc.Vedanta Biosciences, Inc.|\n|Drug Highest PhaseDiscontinuedPhase 2|\n...\n|RegulationFast Track (United States)|\n...\nClinical Trials associated with VE-202\nNCT05370885\n/ TerminatedPhase 2\nRandomized, Double-Blind, Placebo-Controlled, Phase 2 Study of VE202 in Patients With Mild-to-Moderate Ulcerative Colitis\n...\nPureTech Founded Entity Vedanta Biosciences Announces Phase 2 Study of VE202 in Ulcerative Colitis Did Not Meet Primary Endpoint\n...\n|--|--|--|--|--|\n|Colitis, Ulcerative|Phase 2|United States|Vedanta Biosciences, Inc.|12 May 2022|", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-05-15", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.79, "evidence": 0.56}, "jev_score": 0.4424, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "MAINTAIN POP submitted to ANSM - Exeliom Biosciences", "url": "https://www.exeliombio.com/news/maintain-pop-submitted-to-ansm", "domain": "exeliombio.com", "snippet": "🔈 We are thrilled to share that the MAINTAIN POP trial was submitted, in collaboration with REMIND, to the ANSM Agence nationale de sécurité du médicament et des produits de santé.\nMAINTAIN POP is a phase II randomized placebo-controlled trial which will assess the safety and efficacy of EXL01, a single-strain live biotherapeutic, against endoscopic endpoints in patients with Crohn’s disease that have been successfully brought into remission by surgery.\nThe trial will take place across 13 sites in the REMIND group, a network of specialist gastroenterologists in IBD across France.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-25", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.79, "evidence": 0.4733}, "jev_score": 0.3739, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Partnering - Microbiotica", "url": "https://microbiotica.com/partnering/", "domain": "microbiotica.com", "snippet": "Microbiotica is collaborating with the Crohn’s & Colitis Foundation for the development of MB310, its Live Biotherapeutic Product, for the treatment of ulcerative colitis (UC).\nThe collaboration has supported the cGMP manufacture of MB310, enabling an Investigational New Drug (IND) application and supporting progression into a first-in-human clinical.", "date": "2026-05-05", "last_updated": "2026-09-23", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.85, "evidence": 0.4333}, "jev_score": 0.3683, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Delving into the Latest Updates on EXL-01 with Synapse", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/drug/9500af2e0b0346d8b3f9d8d939800b1b", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "Originator Organization\nExeliom Biosciences\nActive Organization\nExeliom Biosciences\nInactive Organization\n...\nDrug Highest Phase\nPhase 2\n...\nCTIS2024-511357-22-00\nRecruiting\nPhase 2\nIIT\nA Phase 2 multicentre, randomised, parallel-arm, placebo-controlled, double-blind study to evaluate the safety and target engagement of EXL01 in the prevention of post-operative recurrence of Crohn’s Disease - REMIND-2024-001\nStart Date\n31 Mar 2025\n...\nPhase 2\nFrance\nExeliom Biosciences\n01 Dec 2024\n...\nPhase 2\n...\n16 Apr 2024\n...\nPhase 2\nFrance\nExeliom Biosciences\nCrohn Disease\nPhase 2\nFrance\nExeliom Biosciences", "date": "2025-06-18", "last_updated": "2025-08-14", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.62, "evidence": 0.48}, "jev_score": 0.2976, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Exeliom Biosciences collabore avec REMIND sur l’essai", "url": "https://ala.associates/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/240625-Exeliom-MAINTAIN-POP-FR-1.pdf", "domain": "ala.associates", "snippet": " Cette étude de phase 2 vise à évaluer l'efficacité de EXL01 en \ntraitement préventif des récidives post-opératoires chez les \npatients atteints de la maladie de Crohn \n...\n La partie A de l'étude MAINTAIN a été arrêtée en raison d’un\nrecrutement trop lent \n L'étude MAINTAIN POP commencera début 2025 ; les \npremiers résultats sont attendus au premier trimestre 2027 \nParis, France, le 25 juin 2024 - Exeliom Biosciences, société de biotechnologie au stade clinique développant de nouvelles thérapies en immuno-inflammation et immuno- oncologie, annonce aujourd’hui une collaboration avec REMIND, consortium national de 14 services de gastro-entérologie, autour de MAINTAIN POP, un essai clinique interventionnel post-opératoire de phase 2 dans la maladie de Crohn.\nExeliom continue ainsi ses recherches dans la maladie de Crohn après l’arrêt de l’essai clinique MAINTAIN à la fin de la partie A, du fait d’un recrutement trop lent.\nMAINTAIN POP est un essai de phase 2 randomisé contre placebo auquel participent 13 sites du groupe REMIND.\nIl vise à recruter 80 patients atteints de la maladie de Crohn et étant entrés en rémission suite à une intervention chirurgicale, afin d'évaluer EXL01 comme traitement d'entretien, seul ou en association avec des anti-TNF.\nL'étude évaluera la sécurité et l'efficacité de EXL01 par rapport à des critères d'évaluation endoscopiques\n(par exemple, la récurrence endoscopique six mois après l’intervention).\nCette étude \ninterventionnelle post-opératoire est coordonnée par le groupe REMIND.\n...\nL'autorisation pour l'essai clinique MAINTAIN POP sera soumise en septembre 2024, avec pour objectif de commencer le recrutement début 2025 et de publier les principaux résultats au premier trimestre 2027.\n...\nEXL01 est un produit biothérapeutique vivant (LBP - live biotherapeutic product) contenant une souche unique non modifiée de F. prausnitzii, un acteur important du microbiome intestinal humain, qui est développé en tant que nouveau médicament modulant le système immunitaire.\n...\nEXL01 est évalué en clinique dans plusieurs essais cliniques : un essai de phase 1 dans la maladie de Crohn, trois essais de phase 2 en immuno-oncologie en association avec des inhibiteurs de points de contrôle immunitaire et un essai de phase 1/2 dans la prévention à l’infection récurrente à C. difficile.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-01-19", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.69, "evidence": 0.43}, "jev_score": 0.2967, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "News - exeliom", "url": "https://www.exeliombio.com/news/several-exl01-studies-hit-recruitment-milestones", "domain": "exeliombio.com", "snippet": "Inflammatory Disease - Crohn’s Disease\n- MAINTAIN POP (NCT06925061): Recruitment is ongoing as planned since the launch in April.\nThis is a Phase 2 randomized clinical trial evaluating EXL01 in postoperative Crohn’s disease patients, in collaboration with hashtag#REMIND and 14 French gastroenterology departments.\n...\n- LIVEDIFF (NCT063060): Recruitment has resumed and the first patients have been enrolled in part two of the study.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-23", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.69, "evidence": 0.3967}, "jev_score": 0.2737, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Exeliom Biosciences collaborates with REMIND in MAINTAIN POP ...", "url": "https://www.exeliombio.com/news/exeliom-biosciences-collaborates-with-remind-in-maintain-pop-study", "domain": "exeliombio.com", "snippet": "·      **Phase IItrial will assess efficacy of EXL01 as treatment to prevent post-operative recurrencein patients with Crohn’s disease**\n...\n·     **MAINTAIN POP trial to start early 2025 with toplineresults expected in Q1, 2027**\n**Paris, France, June 25, 2024 –** ExeliomBiosciences, a clinical-stage biotechnology company developing new therapies inimmuno-inflammation and immuno-oncology,today announces its collaboration with REMIND, a nationwide consortium of 14 Frenchgastroenterology departments, in the MAINTAIN POP study, a phase IIpost-operative interventional trial in Crohn’s disease.\nIt will be acontinuation of Exeliom’s efforts in Crohn’s disease after the company decidedto close the MAINTAIN clinical study at the end of Part A due to a slow recruitment.\nMAINTAIN POP is a phase IIrandomized placebo-controlled trial that will involve 13 sites in the REMINDgroup.\nIt aims to enroll 80 patients with Crohn's disease who have successfullybeen brought to remission by surgery, to evaluate EXL01 as a stand-alonemaintenance treatment or in combination with anti-TNFs.\nThe study will assessboth the safety and efficacy of EXL01 against endoscopic endpoints (e.g.endoscopic recurrence at six months post-surgery).\nThis is a post-operativeinterventional study - coordinated by the REMIND group.\nMAINTAIN POP is a continuation of thework undertaken in MAINTAIN (NCT05542355), a first-in-human phase I trialevaluating EXL01 for the maintenance of steroid-induced remission in patientswith mild to moderate Crohn’s disease.\nThe preliminary results support agood safety profile for EXL01 in combination with corticosteroids and as amonotherapy in patients with Crohn’s disease, as recently confirmed by theIDMC.\n...\nThe clinical trial authorization for MAINTAIN POP willbe submitted in September 2024, with the aim of starting enrollment in early2025 and publishing topline results in Q1, 2027.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-10-01", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.62, "evidence": 0.38}, "jev_score": 0.2356, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}], "total_ms": 1281.7, "ts": "2026-10-06T12:18:09+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q021-batch", "row_id": "q021", "mode": "batch", "objective": "Crohn's Disease Ulcerative Colitis competing agents with the same mechanistic class as S1PR S1PR1 Sphingosine-1-phosphate receptor, including Small Molecule", "query": ["Mocravimod IBD S1PR competitors licensing status", "Mocravimod Crohn's disease ulcerative colitis S1PR1 agents", "Mocravimod etrasimod ozanimod head-to-head IBD", "Mocravimod IBD partnering in-licensing", "Mocravimod ClinicalTrials.gov Crohn's ulcerative colitis"], "anchors": ["Mocravimod"], "search_latency_ms": 1139.8, "target": "drugs competing with Mocravimod: other agents in the same mechanistic class or for the same indication, as described in `objective`", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 380.4, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 10378, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000436, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 7, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.864, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Targeting the Sphingosine-1-Phosphate Pathway - PMC - NIH", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12057646/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "In this review, we revisit the role of the S1P/S1PR axis in the pathogenesis of IBD and discuss efficacy and safety data from clinical trials and real-world reports on the two S1PR modulators, ozanimod and etrasimod, that are currently approved for IBD treatment, and comment on their potential positioning in the IBD day-to-day management.\n...\nSphingosine-1-phosphate modulators are an emerging promising therapeutic target in the field of IBD.\nTo date, two such drugs, specifically ozanimod and etrasimod have been approved for use in moderate to severe UC treatment.\n...\n|NCT01375179 [83]|UC|Phase 2|18–65 years old, biologic naïve and exposed|27 participants 14% clinical remission for patients in 1.2 mg mocravimod compared to 0% for placebo at week 8 Terminated for not reaching the minimal clinically relevant threshold of 20%|No difference in the distribution of adverse events between mocravimod and placebo groups 1 case of lymphopenia.\n1 case of infectious diarrhea|\n...\n### 4.1. Approved Agents\n...\nOzanimod (RPC1063) is an orally administered prodrug that is phosphorylated by SphK, and its active metabolite selectively binds to S1PR1 and S1PR5.\nWhile ongoing clinical trials (NCT03915769, NCT05644665, NCT05076175) continue to explore the efficacy of ozanimod in treating UC, it has already garnered approval for the management of moderate to severe cases, largely due to the conclusive findings from the TOUCHSTONE and True North studies [54, 55].\n...\nA Phase 2, randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled dose-ranging trial has been conducted to evaluate the safety and efficacy of icanbelimod (CBP-307) a next-generation selective S1PR1 modulator in moderate to severe UC, where 145 participants were randomized (1:1:1) to receive icanbelimod of 0.1 mg (*n* = 39), 0.2 mg (*n* = 53) or placebo treatment (*n* = 53) for 12 weeks of induction and 36 weeks for maintenance [72].\n...\nAmiselimod (MT-1303) is an orally administered prodrug that functions after its conversion to its active metabolite, amiselimod-P (MT-1303-P), by SphKs and demonstrates increased selectivity for S1PR1, indicating favorable safety features, especially regarding cardiac side effects [76].\n...\nMocravimod (KRP-203) is an orally administered prodrug, the active metabolite of which, mocravimod-P (KRP-203-P) is an agonist of S1PR1, S1PR4 and S1PR5 and only partially antagonizes S1PR3, leading to reduced risk of bradycardia development, in contrast to the first generation of S1P modulators.\n...\nIn a multicenter, double-blind, placebo-controlled, parallel-group, randomized (1:1) trial assessing the safety and efficacy of mocravimod in 27 patients with moderately active 5-ASA-resistant UC, mocravimod did not meet the minimum clinical efficacy criteria, i.e., clinical remission defined as partial Mayo Score of 0 or 1 and a modified Baron Score of 0 or 1 with no macroscopic bleeding (partial Mayo sub-score of 0 for rectal bleeding) at a statistically significant threshold compared to placebo and thus study was terminated [83].\n...\nThe S1P modulators ozanimod and etrasimod act by blocking a previously untapped pathophysiological pathway and induce significantly better outcomes in terms of clinical, endoscopic and histological endpoints, as compared to placebo.\nThus, they are approved as either first- or second-line therapies for UC.", "date": "2024-09-26", "last_updated": "2026-09-05", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.96, "evidence": 0.9}, "jev_score": 0.864, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Targeting Sphingosine-1-Phosphate Signaling in Immune ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8116828/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "| |S1PR1|S1PR2|S1PR3|S1PR4|S1PR5|\n|--|--|--|--|--|--|\n|S1PR agonists|AMG 369, amiselimod, ASP0028, ASP4058, AUY954, cenerimod.\nceralifimod, CS-077, CS-2100, CYM544, etrasimod, fingolimod, GSK1842799, GSK2018682, mocravimod, ozanimod, Ponesimod, RP-001, SEW2871, siponimod, ST-968, ST-1071, Syl948, TC-G 1006, TC-SP14, amgen 8,14| |Fingolimod, ST-968, ST-1071|Amiselimod, etrasimod, fingolimod|A-971432, AMG 369, amiselimod, ASP0028, ASP4058, cerafimod, etrasimod, fingolimod, GSK2018682, ozanimod, siponimod|\n...\nOver the past few years, the S1P/S1PRs axis has been identified as a potential therapeutic target for immune-mediated diseases, and the safety and efficacy of several S1PRs modulators have been explored in MS, RA, SLE, psoriasis, atopic dermatitis (AD), ulcerative colitis (UC), and Crohn’s disease (CD) [23, 36].\n...\nHowever, all available agents in use today require parenteral administration.\nSmall molecules that could be orally administered such as S1PR1 agonist/modulators could be a valuable addition.\n...\nMocravimod/KRP-203 is a prodrug agonist of S1PR1, S1PR4, and S1PR5 and partial agonist of S1PR3.\nDespite attenuating colitis in mouse models [2, 54], it was minimally effective in patients with UC (NCT01375179) [55].\nA multicenter, double-blind, placebo-controlled, parallel-group study evaluated the efficacy, safety, and tolerability of KRP203 in moderately active UC refractory to 5-aminosalicylates over 8 weeks [55].\n...\nClinical remission was achieved in 2/14 patients (14%) receiving KRP203 compared with none in the placebo group.\nThis study did not meet the minimum clinically relevant threshold for efficacy [55] and was terminated (NCT01375179); however, its small sample size was a major limitation.\nOverall, KRP203 was well tolerated and safe, and no adverse cardiac events were reported [55].\n...\nOzanimod/RPC1063/Zeposia is an oral S1PR modulator that selectively binds to S1PR1 and S1PR5 [58, 59], reducing concerns around S1PR3 engagement.\n...\nCurrently, there are three active clinical trials for moderate-severe UC: NCT02531126, NCT01647516, NCT03915769, three of which are in phase 3.\nSTEPSTONE, a phase 2, uncontrolled multicenter trial, recruited 69 patients with moderate to severe Crohn’s disease (CD), to examine endoscopic and clinical outcomes following treatment with ozanimod for 12 weeks [65].\n...\nCurrently four phase 3 ozanimod clinical trials in CD are ongoing: NCT03467958, NCT03440385, NCT03440372, and NCT03464097.\n...\nEtrasimod**/** APD334, an oral selective modulator of S1PR1, S1PR4, and S1PR5, has shown efficacy in the CD4+CD45RBhi T-cell transfer mouse model of colitis [71].\nRecently, the results from a phase 2, double-blind, parallel-group study in patients with moderate to severe UC (*n* = 156) have been published (NCT02447302) [72].\n...\nCurrently, Arena Pharma is evaluating etrasimod’s use in other trials such as a phase 3 extension study for the treatment of moderate to severely active UC (ELEVATE OLE UC; NCT03950232) and a phase 2b trial evaluating etrasimod in moderately to severely active CD (CULTIVATE; NCT04173273).\n...\nApart from the agents described above, there are multiple S1PR agonists/modulators that are being investigated for use in autoimmune/immune-mediated disorders (Table 1).\nThese include ceralifimod (S1PR1,5; RRMS. NCT01081782, NCT01226745), CS-077 (S1PR1; MS) [105], sonepcizumab (S1PR1; macular degeneration, NCT00767949), LC51-0255 (S1PR1; UC.", "date": "2021-05-13", "last_updated": "2026-09-08", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.96, "evidence": 0.8167}, "jev_score": 0.784, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Efficacy and safety of sphingosine-1-phosphate receptor ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12421927/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "This meta-analysis supports S1P receptor modulators, particularly ozanimod and etrasimod, as effective and safe treatments for UC.\n...\nSeveral S1PrMs, including Ozanimod, Etrasimod, Mocravimod, and Amiselimod, have demonstrated efficacy in managing moderate to severe IBD, significantly improving patient health and quality of life.\nNotably, Ozanimod has been approved in both the United States and the European Union for the treatment of UC, underscoring its therapeutic potential.\nIts high-affinity binding to S1P receptor subtypes, particularly S1P1 and S1P5, further strengthens its role as a viable treatment option for UC and Crohn’s disease (14).\n...\nInclusion criteria comprised of randomized controlled trials (RCTs), following the PICO framework: Population—patients diagnosed with ulcerative colitis (UC) or Crohn’s disease (CD); Intervention—treatment with S1P1 receptor modulators, including Ozanimod, Etrasimod, Mocravimod, and Amiselimod; Comparator—placebo or standard treatments for inflammatory bowel disease (IBD); and Outcomes—efficacy (clinical, endoscopic, and histologic remission) and safety (adverse events, serious adverse events, and drug discontinuation rates).\n...\nThis meta-analysis highlights the potential role of S1P receptor modulators as an emerging treatment option for inflammatory bowel disease (IBD), particularly in ulcerative colitis (UC).", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-05-20", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.97, "evidence": 0.8033}, "jev_score": 0.7792, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Safety and efficacy of S1P receptor modulators for the ... - PMC", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11383267/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "S1PrMs show promising potential for establishing histologic remission, endoscopic improvement, clinical remission, and corticosteroid-free clinical remission.\n...\nSphingosine 1-phosphate receptor modulators (S1PrMs) are emerging as a effective class of drugs for IBD.\nThese compounds target the S1P receptor, a key regulator of inflammation and immune responses, offering a new approach to cope with the disease mechanism.\nThe S1PrMs such as Ozanimod, Etrasimod, Mocravimod, and Amiselimod, have been effective in treating IBD, from moderate to severe patients and improving their health and lifestyle.\n^[6]^ Also, Ozanimod use has been approved in the US and European Union for the UC, a type of IBD, treatment.\nIt shows high affinity binding to the subtypes of S1P receptors like S1P and S5P, proving it a potential therapeutic option for the various forms of IBD, including UC and Crohn’s disease.^[7]^\n...\nStudies meeting the following criteria were included in this meta-analysis,\n...\n2. Target population comprising IBD patients, either ulcerative colitis or Crohn’s disease.\n...\n| |Mocravimod (1.2 mg; n = 17)|N/A|N/A|8 (57.1)|2 (14.28)|N/A|N/A|N/A|N/A|N/A|N/A|\n...\nOzanimod and Etrasimod are considered more effective S1P modulators for Ulcerative Colitis than Mocravimod and Amiselimod due to their efficacy, safety profiles, and mechanisms of action.\nThey have demonstrated improved results in clinical trials, including lowering inflammation and improving mucosal repair, with potentially fewer adverse effects.", "date": "2024-09-06", "last_updated": "2026-09-21", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.97, "evidence": 0.7867}, "jev_score": 0.7631, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Mocravimod - Inxight Drugs", "url": "https://drugs.ncats.io/drug/X71GCJ0HLI", "domain": "drugs.ncats.io", "snippet": "KYORIN Pharmaceutical has developed a new sphingosine 1-phosphate (S1P) receptor type 1 agonist, 2-amino-2-propanediol hydrochloride (KRP-203), for immunomodulation in autoimmune diseases and organ transplantation.\nThis drug was in phase II clinical trial for the treatment Ulcerative Colitis, but this study was terminated.\nIn addition, it has been investigated for the treatment of Cutaneous Lupus Erythematosus and Crohn's Disease, these phases II clinical trial studies were successfully completed.\n...\n### Approval Year\nUnknown\n149124\n...\n|Sphingosine 1-phosphate receptor 1 7 Target ID:&nbspP21453 Gene ID:&nbsp1901.0 Gene Symbol:&nbspS1PR1 Target Organism:&nbspHomo sapiens (Human) Sources:&nbsphttps://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/16674913|Agonist 2752| | |\n...\n|Ulcerative colitis 125 Sources:&nbsphttps://clinicaltrials.gov/ct2/show/NCT01375179|Primary| |Phase II 1147|Unknown\n#### Approved Use Unknown|\n|--|--|--|--|--|\n...\n|Crohn's disease 64 Sources:&nbsphttp://www.clinicaltrials.jp/user/showCteDetailE.jsp?japicId=JapicCTI-132108|Primary| |Phase II 1147|Unknown\n#### Approved Use Unknown|", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-04-08", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.81, "evidence": 0.5867}, "jev_score": 0.4752, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "mocravimod | Ligand page", "url": "https://www.guidetopharmacology.org/GRAC/LigandDisplayForward?tab=clinical&ligandId=9727", "domain": "guidetopharmacology.org", "snippet": "|Synonyms: compound 2 [PMID: 23124563] | KRP-203 | KRP203 Compound class: Synthetic organic Comment: Mocravimod (KRP203) is a sphingosine 1-phosphate receptor type 1 (S1P 1R) agonist with immunomodulatory potential [6].\nStructurally mocravimod is a highly modified analogue of the approved multiple sclerosis drug fingolimod.\n...\nMocravimod was also tested in autoimmune indications (ulcerative colitis, subacute cutaneous Lupus erythematosus and Crohn's disease).|\n...\n|NCT01375179|Efficacy & Safety in Moderately Active Refractory Ulcerative Colitis Patients|Phase 2 Interventional|Novartis|", "date": "2023-01-01", "last_updated": "2025-07-06", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.85, "evidence": 0.5567}, "jev_score": 0.4732, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Mocravimod Hydrochloride - Inxight Drugs", "url": "https://drugs.ncats.io/drug/3YH0N35CE3", "domain": "drugs.ncats.io", "snippet": "KYORIN Pharmaceutical has developed a new sphingosine 1-phosphate (S1P) receptor type 1 agonist, 2-amino-2-propanediol hydrochloride (KRP-203), for immunomodulation in autoimmune diseases and organ transplantation.\nThis drug was in phase II clinical trial for the treatment Ulcerative Colitis, but this study was terminated.\nIn addition, it has been investigated for the treatment of Cutaneous Lupus Erythematosus and Crohn's Disease, these phases II clinical trial studies were successfully completed.\n...\n### Approval Year\nUnknown\n149124\n...\n|Sphingosine 1-phosphate receptor 1 7 Target ID:&nbspP21453 Gene ID:&nbsp1901.0 Gene Symbol:&nbspS1PR1 Target Organism:&nbspHomo sapiens (Human) Sources:&nbsphttps://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/16674913|Agonist 2752| | |\n...\n|Ulcerative colitis 125 Sources:&nbsphttps://clinicaltrials.gov/ct2/show/NCT01375179|Primary| |Phase II 1147|Unknown\n#### Approved Use Unknown|\n|--|--|--|--|--|\n...\n|Crohn's disease 64 Sources:&nbsphttp://www.clinicaltrials.jp/user/showCteDetailE.jsp?japicId=JapicCTI-132108|Primary| |Phase II 1147|Unknown\n#### Approved Use Unknown|\n...\n|<table><tr><td> 2-amino-2-[2-(2-chloro-4-{[3-(phenylmethoxy)phenyl]sulfanyl}phenyl)ethyl]propane-1,3-diol hydrochloride</td><td> </td></tr></table>|Systematic Name|English|\n|<table><tr><td> 1,3-Propanediol, 2-amino-2-[2-[2-chloro-4-[[3-(phenylmethoxy)phenyl]thio]phenyl]ethyl]-, hydrochloride (1:1)</td><td> </td></tr></table>|Systematic Name|English|", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-06-23", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.72, "evidence": 0.5433}, "jev_score": 0.3912, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Mocravimod", "url": "https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/mocravimod", "domain": "pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Mocravimod is a sphingosine 1-phosphate (S1P) receptor agonist, with potential immunosuppressive activity.\nUpon administration of mocravimod, this agent binds to S1P receptors on lymphocytes, which prevents binding of serum S1P to S1P receptors and leads to S1P receptor internalization.\n...\nStatus of Orphan Designation\nPositive\nDecision Date", "date": "2025-06-14", "last_updated": "2025-06-15", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.8, "evidence": 0.4833}, "jev_score": 0.3866, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Adjunctive and… · Phase III (2024-512752-38-00) - CTIS.eu", "url": "https://ctis.eu/trial/2024-512752-38-00", "domain": "ctis.eu", "snippet": "2024-512752-38-00\n...\n- **ClinicalTrials.gov**\nNCT05429632\n...\n### Test 1\nMocravimod (KRP203)\nPRD9710650 · Product\n- **Active substance**\nMocravimod\n- **Pharmaceutical form**\nCAPSULES\n- **Route of administration**\nORAL USE\n- **Max daily dose**\n3.00 mg milligram(s)\n- **Max total dose**\n1095.00 mg milligram(s)\n- **Max treatment duration**\n12 Month(s)\n- **Authorisation status**\nNot Authorised\n- **MA holder**\nPRIOTHERA S.A.S.", "date": "2025-09-09", "last_updated": "2026-10-03", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.78, "evidence": 0.4267}, "jev_score": 0.3328, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Mocravimod", "url": "https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB18151", "domain": "go.drugbank.com", "snippet": "## Identification\n- Generic Name\n- Mocravimod\n...\nTarget Actions Organism USphingosine 1-phosphate receptor 1agonist Humans\n...\n**Drug product information from 10+ global regions**Our datasets provide approved product information including: **dosage, form, labeller, route of administration, and marketing period.**Access drug product information from over 10 global regions.\n- Product Ingredients\n...\n**1.\nSphingosine 1-phosphate receptor 1**\n- Kind\n- Protein\n- Organism\n- Humans\n- Pharmacological action\n- Unknown\n- Actions\n- Agonist\n- General Function\n...\nG-protein coupled receptor for the bioactive lysosphingolipid sphingosine 1-phosphate (S1P) that seems to be coupled to the G(i) subclass of heteromeric G proteins.\nSignaling leads to the activation of RAC1, SRC, PTK2/FAK1 and MAP kinases.", "date": "2023-09-12", "last_updated": "2025-08-16", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.75, "evidence": 0.3933}, "jev_score": 0.295, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}], "total_ms": 1520.2, "ts": "2026-10-06T12:18:09+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q025-batch", "row_id": "q025", "mode": "batch", "objective": "Ulcerative Colitis competing agents with the same mechanism of action as HEMAY007, targeting TNF-α Tumor Necrosis Factor Alpha", "query": ["HEMAY007 ulcerative colitis anti-TNF Phase 2 competitors", "HEMAY007 ulcerative colitis anti-TNF licensing availability", "HEMAY007 TNF-alpha ulcerative colitis clinical development status", "HEMAY007 ulcerative colitis anti-TNF biosimilar landscape"], "anchors": ["HEMAY007"], "search_latency_ms": 548.1, "target": "drugs competing with HEMAY007: other agents in the same mechanistic class or for the same indication, as described in `objective`", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 348.1, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 10921, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000459, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 7, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.9474, "results": [{"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Hemay007: TNF-alpha, Phase 2 in Ulcerative Colitis", "url": "https://assetmemo.bio/drug/hemay007", "domain": "assetmemo.bio", "snippet": "Discontinuedsponsor decision\n...\nDiscontinued, for reasons the sponsor has not stated\nHemay007 is Tianjin Hemay's oral candidate.\nIts only ulcerative colitis study, the Phase 2 NCT03977480, is TERMINATED on ClinicalTrials.gov for failure to recruit.\nScoped to the ulcerative colitis market only: the molecule continued elsewhere, with a Phase 2 rheumatoid arthritis study (NCT05247216) completed and last updated 2024-03, and no Crohn's disease study was ever registered.\nThe trials below are preserved as the program's evidence history; no evidence signal is assigned to a program the sponsor has stopped.\n...\n107 competitors in this universe: 22 direct rivals (same mechanism and indication), 5 same-class, 80 same-indication; 76 further along in development.\n|Drug|Stage|Modality|Mechanism|Evidence signal|Reported efficacygraded featured trials|\n|--|--|--|--|--|--|\n|Hemay007 — this drug|Phase 2|Small molecule|TNF-alpha| | |\n|Adalimumab (Humira)|Approved|Monoclonal antibody|**TNF-alpha**| | |\n|Adalimumab-Aacf (Idacio)|Approved|—|**TNF-alpha**| | |\n|Adalimumab-Aaty (Yuflyma)|Approved|—|**TNF-alpha**| | |\n|Adalimumab-Adaz (Hyrimoz)|Approved|—|**TNF-alpha**| | |\n|Adalimumab-Adbm (Cyltezo)|Approved|Monoclonal antibody|**TNF-alpha**| | |\n|Adalimumab-Afzb (Abrilada)|Approved|—|**TNF-alpha**| | |\n|Adalimumab-Aqvh (Yusimry)|Approved|—|**TNF-alpha**| | |\n|Adalimumab-Atto (Amjevita)|Approved|—|**TNF-alpha**| | |\n|Adalimumab-Bwwd (Hadlima)|Approved|—|**TNF-alpha**| | |\n|Adalimumab-Fkjp (Hulio)|Approved|—|**TNF-alpha**| | |\n|Adalimumab-Ryvk (Simlandi)|Approved|—|**TNF-alpha**| | |\n|Certolizumab Pegol (Cimzia)|Approved|Antibody fragment|**TNF-alpha**| | |\n|CT-P13|Approved|Monoclonal antibody|**TNF-alpha**| | |\n|Golimumab (Simponi)|Approved|Monoclonal antibody|**TNF-alpha**| | |\n|Infliximab (Remicade)|Approved|Monoclonal antibody|**TNF-alpha**| | |", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-21", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.98, "evidence": 0.9667}, "jev_score": 0.9474, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Advancing therapeutic frontiers: a pipeline of novel drugs for ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12952356/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "The introduction of biologic therapies targeting tumor necrosis factor-α (TNF-α) (infliximab, adalimumab, golimumab), integrin α4β7 (vedolizumab), and interleukin (IL)-12/23 pathways (ustekinumab, mirikizumab, guselkumab, risankizumab) has transformed treatment paradigms for moderate to severe UC (11, 13–21).\n...\n|JAK Inhibitor|Ivarmacitinib (SHR0302)|Selective JAK1 inhibitor blocking JAK-STAT pathway|NCT03675477, NCT05181137|Phase 2, Phase 3|Phase 2 completed Feb 2021; Phase 3 status unknown|\n...\n|TNF modulator|Hemay007|Small molecule TNF-α regulator|NCT03977480|Phase 2|Terminated early Aug 2022 due to recruitment difficulties|\n...\n|Combination|Guselkumab + Golimumab|IL-23p19 antibody + TNF antagonist|NCT03662542 (VEGA), NCT05242484 (DUET-UC)|Phase 2, Phase 2b|VEGA completed Nov 2021; 83% response with combination.\nDUET-UC primary completion May 2025|\n...\nDespite positive Phase 3 results demonstrating a favorable benefit-risk profile, ontamalimab was not submitted for regulatory approval.\nFollowing Takeda’s acquisition of Shire, development was discontinued due to inability to find a licensee for the compound, rather than efficacy or safety concerns.\n...\nThe following section reviews investigational TNF pathway inhibitors currently in Phase II and III clinical development for moderate to severe UC.\n#### 2.3.1. Hemay007\nA Phase II study (NCT03977480) evaluated Hemay007, a small molecule that acts as a TNF-α regulator, using a multicenter, randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled design with four treatment arms comparing different dosing regimens (400 mg BID, 800 mg QD, 600 mg BID, and placebo) over 12 weeks followed by an open-label extension.\nThe primary outcome measured clinical response defined as MS reduction of ≥3 points and ≥30% from baseline with RBS improvement, while inclusion criteria required patients aged 18–70 years with active UC (MS ≥4, MES ≥2) who had treatment failure or intolerance to standard therapies including 5-ASA, corticosteroids, immunomodulators, or anti-TNF agents.\nThe study was terminated early due to recruitment difficulties, completing in August 2022 with only 70 of the planned 288 patients enrolled, and no efficacy or safety results have been posted on ClinicalTrials.gov.", "date": "2026-01-30", "last_updated": "2026-09-25", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.96, "evidence": 0.9}, "jev_score": 0.864, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "TNF-alpha - ACROBiosystems", "url": "https://www.acrobiosystems.com/A1171-TNF-alpha.html", "domain": "acrobiosystems.com", "snippet": "|Infliximab|TA-650|Approved|Janssen, Merck Sharp & Dohme, Mitsubishi Tanabe Pharma|REMICADE|fda|Ulcerative colitis, Psoriatic arthritis, Rheumatoid arthritis (RA), Crohn's disease|CENTOCOR INC|1998-08-24|Pediatric ulcerative colitis, Psoriasis vulgaris, Crohn's disease, Mucocutaneous lymph node syndrome (Kawasaki syndrome), Psoriatic erythroderma, Ankylosing Spondylitis (AS), Uveitis, Pustular psoriasis, Ulcerative colitis, Psoriatic arthritis, Rheumatoid arthritis (RA), Behcet's disease, Pediatric Crohn's disease|Details|\n...\n|Hemay007|Hemay-007|Phase Ⅱ|Tianjin Hemay Biotech|Ulcerative colitis|Details|", "date": "2018-01-19", "last_updated": "2025-04-14", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.94, "evidence": 0.9067}, "jev_score": 0.8523, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Advancing therapeutic frontiers: a pipeline of novel drugs ...", "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/gastroenterology/articles/10.3389/fgstr.2026.1747118/full", "domain": "frontiersin.org", "snippet": "The introduction of biologic therapies targeting tumor necrosis factor-α (TNF-α) (infliximab, adalimumab, golimumab), integrin α4β7 (vedolizumab), and interleukin (IL)-12/23 pathways (ustekinumab, mirikizumab, guselkumab, risankizumab) has transformed treatment paradigms for moderate to severe UC (11, 13–21).\n...\n|TNF modulator|Hemay007|Small molecule TNF-α regulator|NCT03977480|Phase 2|Terminated early Aug 2022 due to recruitment difficulties|\n|TNF-α/OX40L inhibitor|SAR442970|Bispecific pentavalent Nanobody targeting TNF-α and OX40L|NCT06975722|Phase 2b|Started July 2025; estimated completion Dec 2026|\n...\nA Phase II study (NCT03977480) evaluated Hemay007, a small molecule that acts as a TNF-α regulator, using a multicenter, randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled design with four treatment arms comparing different dosing regimens (400 mg BID, 800 mg QD, 600 mg BID, and placebo) over 12 weeks followed by an open-label extension.\nThe primary outcome measured clinical response defined as MS reduction of ≥3 points and ≥30% from baseline with RBS improvement, while inclusion criteria required patients aged 18–70 years with active UC (MS ≥4, MES ≥2) who had treatment failure or intolerance to standard therapies including 5-ASA, corticosteroids, immunomodulators, or anti-TNF agents.\nThe study was terminated early due to recruitment difficulties, completing in August 2022 with only 70 of the planned 288 patients enrolled, and no efficacy or safety results have been posted on ClinicalTrials.gov.\n...\nMufemilast (Hemay005) is a selective phosphodiesterase 4 inhibitor being tested in a multicenter, double-blind, placebo-controlled Phase II RCT (NCT05486104) with three arms comparing 45mg twice daily, 60mg twice daily, and placebo over 12 weeks of treatment followed by 4 weeks of observation.\n...\nThe study started in November 2022 with an estimated completion date of December 2025, and results are not yet available as the trial is ongoing but no longer recruiting participants.", "date": "2026-01-30", "last_updated": "2026-09-29", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.96, "evidence": 0.88}, "jev_score": 0.8448, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "TNF-Alpha Proteins, Antibodies & ELISA Kits", "url": "https://www.acrobiosystems.com/category/target-protein/tnf-alpha", "domain": "acrobiosystems.com", "snippet": "|Hemay007|Hemay-007|Phase 2 Clinical|Tianjin Hemay Bio-Tech Co Ltd|Arthritis, Rheu\n…\n...\n|Balinatunfib|SAR-441566; SAR441566|Phase 2 Clinical|Sanofi|Arthritis, Rheumatoid; Colitis, Ulcerative; Crohn Disease; Plaque psoriasis; Psoriasis|Details|\n|Brivekimig|SAR-442970; SAR442970|Phase 2 Clinical|Sanofi|Colitis, Ulcerative; Crohn Disease; Diabetes Mellitus, Type 1; Glomerulonephritis; Glomerulosclerosis, Focal Segmental; Hidradenitis Suppurativa; Inflammation; Nephrosis|Details|", "date": "2024-09-23", "last_updated": "2026-09-24", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.93, "evidence": 0.8267}, "jev_score": 0.7688, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Table 1.", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12952356/table/T1/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "|JAK Inhibitor|Ivarmacitinib (SHR0302)|Selective JAK1 inhibitor blocking JAK-STAT pathway|NCT03675477, NCT05181137|Phase 2, Phase 3|Phase 2 completed Feb 2021; Phase 3 status unknown|\n|JAK Inhibitor|Itacitinib (INCB039110)|JAK1 inhibitor|NCT03627052|Phase 2|Withdrawn Nov 2019 due to lack of recruitment|\n...\n|JAK Inhibitor|Brepocitinib (PF-06700841)|Oral dual TYK2/JAK1 inhibitor|NCT02958865 (VIBRATO)|Phase 2b|Completed May 2021; advanced to alopecia areata trials|\n...\n|TYK2 Inhibitor|D-2570|Novel oral TYK2 inhibitor|NCT07035041|Phase 2|Recruiting; began May 2025, estimated completion Dec 2026|\n...\n|TNF modulator|Hemay007|Small molecule TNF-α regulator|NCT03977480|Phase 2|Terminated early Aug 2022 due to recruitment difficulties|\n|TNF-α/OX40L inhibitor|SAR442970|Bispecific pentavalent Nanobody targeting TNF-α and OX40L|NCT06975722|Phase 2b|Started July 2025; estimated completion Dec 2026|\n...\n|PDE4 inhibitor|Mufemilast (Hemay005)|Selective phosphodiesterase 4 inhibitor|NCT05486104|Phase 2|Started Nov 2022; estimated completion Dec 2025|", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-03-14", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.92, "evidence": 0.7867}, "jev_score": 0.7238, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "This summary aims to give you an overview of the information contained in this", "url": "https://www1.hkexnews.hk/app/sehk/2025/107914/a127807/sehk25120100830.pdf", "domain": "www1.hkexnews.hk", "snippet": "Phase II trials have been completed for ankylosing spondylitis (“AS”), with additional trials ongoing or planned for ulcerative colitis (“UC”), atopic dermatitis (“AD”), psoriatic arthritis (“PsA”), Crohn’s disease (“CD”), and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (“COPD”).\n...\nHemay007*(10)(11)\n...\nMufemilast**\n...\nFor Hemay007, both Phase II clinical trials were approved based on results derived from the same Phase I clinical trial, Hemay007AU01.\n...\nHemay007 is screened from a compound library directed by multiple pharmacological functional substructures and is a TNF-�small molecule regulator.\nIn the spontaneous UC monkey model, Hemay007 reduced the number of ulcers in the colons of the testing subjects, and pathological examinations of the intestines after treatment revealed that the colon tissue almost returned to a normal state.\nAs of the Latest Practicable Date, we have completed the\nPhase II clinical trial for monotherapy for UC.\nA Phase II clinical trial for rheumatoid arthritis\n(RA) is ongoing.\nFor phase II clinical trial of Hemay007 for UC at week 12, clinical response\nrates, as evaluated by both central laboratory and principal investigator (PI) assessments, there was no statistically significant differences across all groups in both the FAS and PPS populations (P > 0.05 for all comparisons).\nClinical remission rates at Week 12 were also comparable among the treatment and placebo groups, with no statistically significant differences observed.\nFor the development of Hemay007, we will prioritize the clinical trial for\nRA.\nCurrently, we do not have a definitive timetable for the next steps in UC.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-12-04", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.69, "evidence": 0.5167}, "jev_score": 0.3565, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "[PDF] 概覽", "url": "https://www1.hkexnews.hk/app/sehk/2026/108622/a133802/sehk26060801670_c.pdf", "domain": "www1.hkexnews.hk", "snippet": "強直性脊柱炎（「AS」）的II期試驗，針對潰瘍性結腸炎（「UC」）、特應性皮\n...\nHemay007*(10)(11)\n...\nIII期\n...\n(10) \nHemay007的兩項II期臨床試驗均基於同一項I期臨床試驗(Hemay007AU01)所得的結果而獲得批准。\n...\n我們僅針對Hemay007、Hemay808及Hemay022的開發進行研究。\n...\nHemay007是通過多種藥理功能亞結構組合化合物庫篩選而來，是一種TNF-α小\n分子調節劑。在自發性UC猴模型中，Hemay007減少了受試者結腸中的潰瘍數量，治\n療後對腸道進行病理檢查發現，結腸組織幾乎恢復到正常狀態。截至最後實際可行日\n期，我們已完成單藥治療UC的II期臨床試驗。我們正在進行類風濕關節炎(RA)的II期\n臨床試驗。就Hemay007用於UC的II期臨床試驗第12週而言，無論是透過中央實驗室\n評估還是主要研究者(PI)評估，所有組別在FAS及PPS人群中的臨床反應率均未顯示出\n統計學上的顯著差異（所有比較P>0.05）。第12週的臨床緩解率在治療組與安慰劑組之\n間亦相當，未觀察到統計學上的顯著差異。目前，我們並無就UC的下一步制定正式", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-24", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.72, "evidence": 0.48}, "jev_score": 0.3456, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Hemay007 - Drug Targets, Indications, Patents", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/drug/c6cef31360b74c8b9fd3d4c563740a9e", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "Synonyms\nTarget\nTNF-α\nAction\ninhibitors\nMechanism\nTNF-α inhibitors(Tumor necrosis factor α inhibitors)\n...\nColitis, Ulcerative\nInflammatory Bowel Diseases\n...\nActive Organization\nXiajiang Hemei Pharmaceutical Co., Ltd.\nTianjin Hemay Pharmaceutical Technology Co., Ltd.\nInactive Organization\nHemay Pharmaceutical Pty Ltd\nLicense Organization\nDrug Highest Phase\nPhase 2\n...\nA Multicenter, Randomized, Double-blind Phase 2 Study to Evaluate the Safety, Efficacy, Pharmacokinetics (PK) and Pharmacodynamics (PD) of Hemay007 in Patients With Moderate to Severe Rheumatoid Arthritis\n...\nNCT03977480\nTerminated\nPhase 2\nA Multicenter, Randomized, Double-blind, Placebo Parallel Controlled Clinical Study on the Effecacy and Safety of Different Dosing Regimens of Hemay007 in Patients With Active Ulcerative Colitis\nThis study adopts a multicenter, randomized, double-blind, low-medium-high dose group and placebo parallel controlled clinical study design.\n...\nCTR20182150\nTerminated\nPhase 2\nHemay007不同剂量给药方案用于活动性溃疡性结肠炎患者有效性和安全性多中心、随机、双盲、安慰剂平行对照临床研究\n...\nA multicenter, randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled clinical study on the efficacy and safety of different dosing regimens of Hemay007 in patients with active ulcerative colitis", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-30", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.72, "evidence": 0.4567}, "jev_score": 0.3288, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "OVERVIEW", "url": "https://www1.hkexnews.hk/app/sehk/2025/107914/a127807/sehk25120100842.pdf", "domain": "www1.hkexnews.hk", "snippet": "commence Phase II clinical trials in China, with first patient enrolment expected in\n2026. We have obtained orphan drug designation in the United States from the FDA\n...\nOur key product, Hemay007 is screened from a compound library directed by multiple pharmacological functional substructures and is a TNF-�small molecule regulator.\n...\nHemay007\nHemay007 is screened from a compound library using multiple pharmacological approaches and is a TNF-�small molecule modulator.\nIn the spontaneous UC monkey model,\nHemay007 reduced the number of ulcers in the colons of the diseased monkeys.\n...\nUC ������������\n...\nSupplemental IND approval obtained\n(200mg)\n...\n2021\nPhase II completed\n...\nPhase II:\n– Initiated: April 2021\n– Expected completion: 2026(1)\n...\nFor the development of Hemay007, we will prioritize the clinical trial for RA.\nCurrently, we do not have a definitive timetable for the next steps in UC.\nWe do not currently have plans to initiate any additional clinical trials overseas in respect of Hemay007.\n...\nBy inhibiting this pathway, Hemay007 blocks the release of pro-inflammatory factors, particularly TNF-�, thereby reducing the inflammatory response triggered by immune stimulation.\n...\nUC (Phase II)\nDesign.\nThe clinical trial was a multicenter, randomized, double-blind, placebo- controlled, parallel-group study designed to evaluate the efficacy, safety, and dose-response relationship of various dosing regimens of Hemay007 in patients with UC.\nA total of 70 patients with UC were enrolled and randomized into four parallel groups: 16 patients received\nHemay007 400 mg twice daily (BID), 18 patients received 800 mg once daily (QD), 17 patients received 600 mg BID, and 19 patients received a placebo.\n63 subjects (90.0%) completed the double-blind induction treatment phase, with 13, 18, 15, and 17 subjects in the respective groups.\nFollowing the double-blind period, all patients entered a 12-week open-label extension phase with Hemay007 at 600 mg twice daily.\n...\napplications (20mg tablets\nand 100mg tablets) with", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-12-04", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.57, "evidence": 0.45}, "jev_score": 0.2565, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}], "total_ms": 896.2, "ts": "2026-10-06T12:18:09+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q027-batch", "row_id": "q027", "mode": "batch", "objective": "HQL-79 in vivo single-agent monotherapy tumour growth inhibition TGI tumour regression tumour shrinkage in Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma animal models", "query": ["HQL-79 pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma in vivo", "HQL-79 monotherapy PDAC xenograft", "HQL-79 tumour regression animal model", "HQL-79 tumour growth inhibition pancreatic cancer"], "anchors": ["HQL-79"], "search_latency_ms": 545.7, "target": "HQL-79", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 339.4, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 8635, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000363, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 6, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.7631, "results": [{"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Figure 7.", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12223510/figure/fig7/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "HPGDS inhibition overcomes immunotherapy resistance and is effective in human settings.\nTumor growth (), tumor weight (), and representative images () of ^*V600E*^^*−/−*^ genetically engineered mice (GEM) treated with vehicle, HQL-79 15 mg/kg twice daily, αPD1, or the combination of HQL-79 and αPD1 ( = 6).\n...\nPercentage of CD11c^+^ macrophages in ^*V600E*^^*−/−*^ GEM treated with vehicle, HQL-79, αPD1, or the combination of HQL-79 and αPD1 ( = 6).\nFlow cytometric quantification of PD1 expressing CD8^+^ T cells in ^*V600E*^^*−/−*^ GEM treated with vehicle, HQL-79, αPD1, or HQL-79 in combination and αPD1 ( = 6).\nHistologic quantification of CD8^+^ T cells at the tumor margin vs. core in ^*V600E*^^*−/−*^ GEM treated with vehicle/IgG, HQL-79, αPD1, or HQL-79 in combination with αPD1 ( = 6\n). and Tumor weight () and representative pictures () of KPC FC1245 PDAC-bearing mice treated with vehicle/IgG, HQL-79, αPD1, or HQL-79 in combination with αPD1 ( = 11–16).", "date": "2025-01-01", "last_updated": "2025-07-08", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.97, "evidence": 0.7867}, "jev_score": 0.7631, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "www.creative-biolabs.com › blog › adcHyaluronidase-Conjugated Liposomes May Effectively Target ...", "url": "https://www.creative-biolabs.com/blog/adc/hyaluronidase-conjugated-liposomes-may-effectively-target-pancreatic-cancer/", "domain": "creative-biolabs.com", "snippet": "The research team developed an efficient drug delivery system: quercetin was encapsulated into liposomes, and hyaluronidase (HAase) was conjugated to the surface of the liposomes.\nThis novel liposome was named HQL.\n...\nIn vitro experiments showed that HQL could stably exist in 10% fetal bovine serum and plasma for 15 days.\n...\nIn vitro cell experiments demonstrated that HQL had a significant proliferation-inhibiting effect on pancreatic cancer cells (PANC-1 and Miapaca-2), with an IC50 value lower than that of quercetin and other controls.\nIt had no obvious inhibitory effect on the proliferation of normal pancreatic ductal epithelial cells (HPNE), showing good tumor cell specificity.\nHQL had stronger penetration ability in HA-rich agarose gels.\nIn 2D and 3D cell culture models, it was more effectively taken up by pancreatic cancer cells, promoted cancer cell apoptosis, and induced cell cycle arrest at the G2/M phase.\nIn experiments with 3D-cultured PANC-1 cells and PDAC organoids, HQL also showed superior ability to inhibit cell proliferation and promote apoptosis compared to other control groups.\nIn in vivo experiments, the researchers established a pancreatic cancer mouse model.\nResults showed that the tumor volume and weight in the HQL treatment group were significantly lower than those in the control group and other treatment groups, reducing tumor volume by 73% and decreasing HA levels in tumor tissues by 95%.\nFluorescent labeling experiments revealed that HQL mainly accumulated in tumor tissues, with less distribution in organs such as the liver.\nThe drug concentration of HQL in the blood could still be maintained at an effective level (50 μg/mL) within 48 hours, while the concentration of orally administered quercetin dropped to below 0.2 μg/mL after 48 hours.\nFurthermore, the in vivo enzymatic activity of HQL peaked at 60 U/mL at 2 hours, then gradually decreased and remained at approximately 20 U/mL within 48 hours.\nSafety evaluation indicated that HQL had no obvious impact on mouse body weight or the morphology and function of major organs (heart, liver, spleen, lung, kidney), showing no potential toxic side effects.\nMechanistic studies found that HQL may exert its effects of inhibiting tumor cell growth and promoting apoptosis by downregulating the expression of cell cycle-related proteins (CCNB1, CDK1, and PLK1) as well as apoptosis-related factors PI3K/AKT and Bcl-2.\nIn summary, the HQL liposomal drug delivery system developed in this study can effectively degrade HA in the pancreatic cancer tumor microenvironment, enhance drug penetration and delivery efficiency, significantly inhibit pancreatic cancer growth, and has good safety.\nIt provides a new and promising drug delivery method for the adjuvant treatment of pancreatic cancer, which is expected to bring new hope to pancreatic cancer patients in the future and promote further development in the field of pancreatic cancer treatment.", "date": "2025-08-14", "last_updated": "2026-10-01", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.78, "evidence": 0.88}, "jev_score": 0.6864, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Activated T Cells Break Tumor Immunosuppression by Macrophage ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12223510/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "To translate our genetic results in a clinically relevant setting, we used the HPGDS inhibitor HQL-79 (38) in YUMM 1.7 melanoma-bearing mice.\nSystemic administration of HQL-79 (15 mg/kg twice daily) recapitulated the antitumor phenotype observed in *Hpgds*^ΔMø^ mice (Fig.\n6A–C; Supplementary Fig. S9A and S9B), achieving 78% of tumor growth inhibition with one of six mice undergoing total tumor rejection (Supplementary Fig. S9B).\nThe impairment in tumor growth was accompanied by a shift in TAMs toward antitumoral markers (Fig.\n6D–F) and robust infiltration of CD8^+^ T cells (Supplementary Fig. S9C–S9E).\nLike macrophage-specific *Hpgds* deletion, HQL-79 strongly affected the tumor vasculature by decreasing vessel density and size and by improving pericyte coverage and perfusion (Supplementary Fig.\nS9F–S9L), together with reduced hypoxia (Supplementary Fig. S9M and S9N), cancer cell intravasation into the bloodstream (Fig. 6G), and lung colonization (Fig. 6H).\n...\nLastly, we extended our findings to pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (PDAC).\nThis tumor type was selected due to the higher expression of HPGDS in the malignant tissue compared with the healthy counterpart (Supplementary Fig. S11F) and its colocalization with macrophage markers only in both murine and human tumors (Supplementary Fig.\nS11G).\nIn mice with orthotopic KPC tumors (carrying the two most common mutations found in patients with PDAC, i.e., *KRAS* and *TP53*), αPD1 was ineffective, and HQL-79 displayed a mild inhibitory effect on tumor growth, but their combination was synergic (Fig. 7G and H).", "date": "2025-04-07", "last_updated": "2025-09-23", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.97, "evidence": 0.7}, "jev_score": 0.679, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Figure 6.", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12223510/figure/fig6/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Tumor growth (), tumor weight (), and tumor representative pictures () of YUMM 1.7 CD90.1^+^ melanoma-bearing mice treated by oral gavage with HQL-79, 15 mg/kg BID or with vehicle control ( = 6).", "date": "2025-01-01", "last_updated": "2025-07-08", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.96, "evidence": 0.6033}, "jev_score": 0.5792, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "An efficient drug delivery system to block pancreatic cancer", "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40127723/", "domain": "pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Hence, we developed an efficient drug delivery system by encapsulation of Que. into liposomes and conjugation with hyaluronidase (HAase) at liposome surface, termed as HQL.\nIn the presence of HAase, HQL were predominantly accumulated at tumor with enhanced permeability and retention effect.\nTreatment of xenografted tumor mice with HQL gave rise to suppressed tumor growth, while no toxic effects were observed in mice.\nHQL demonstrated the strong ability to inhibit cell proliferation, promote cell apoptosis, and induce arrest at G2/M cell cycle in pancreatic cancer lines, three-dimensional cultured cell spheroids and pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (PDAC)-derived organoids.\nMechanistically, HQL downregulated expression of cell cycle-associated protein (CCNB1, CDK1 and PLK1) and cell apoptosis-associated factors PI3K/AKT and Bcl-2.\nIn summary, HQL degraded HA in the tumor microenvironment to enhance nano-particle penetration and inhibited tumor cell growth, eliciting efficacy of anti-tumor therapy.\nThereof, HQL may provide a novel efficient drug delivery approach for the adjuvant treatment of pancreatic cancer.", "date": "2025-06-10", "last_updated": "2025-09-17", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.81, "evidence": 0.71}, "jev_score": 0.5751, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "chloroquine and hydroxychloroquine as anti-cancer agents", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5718030/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "* A similar observation can be made when comparing CQ efficacy in pancreatic cancer mouse models and a lung cancer mouse model: CQ significantly slowed down tumour growth and increased survival in the first, but not in the latter [27].\n...\n|Carew *et al* [171]|CQ|Vorinostat|Female nude BALB/c mice|HCT8 colon cancer cells|Significantly enhanced tumour reduction|\n...\nNext, CQ and HCQ were tested via *in vitro* assays, in which they were found to suppress pancreatic cancer cell proliferation [82, 83].", "date": "2017-11-23", "last_updated": "2026-08-15", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.81, "evidence": 0.65}, "jev_score": 0.5265, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Phytochemicals as Multitarget Therapeutics in Pancreatic Cancer: Mechanisms, Clinical Evidence, and Translational Challenges", "url": "https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/ptr.70430", "domain": "onlinelibrary.wiley.com", "snippet": "Quercetin has shown potential to inhibit tumor cell growth, but its effectiveness is limited by low bioavailability and poor solubility.\nHQL's ability to degrade HA in the tumor microenvironment facilitated NP penetration and contributed to its antitumor efficacy (Sun et al. 2025).\nQuercetin-3-*O*-glucoside may enhance gemcitabine's chemotherapeutic effects and inhibit metastasis, determined by various growth factors in PC (Lee et al. 2016).", "date": "2026-08-16", "last_updated": "2026-09-26", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.45, "evidence": 0.6}, "jev_score": 0.27, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "In Vivo Preclinical Models", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8578999/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Zebrafish is a useful and economical *in vivo* animal model for speedy analysis of invasion and metastatic behavior.\n...\n*In vivo* models are useful to model the TME and immune response, key players in PDAC transformation and progression.\nMurine models are ideal platforms for understanding PDAC progression, pathophysiology and testing therapeutic modalities.", "date": "2021-10-27", "last_updated": "2026-02-18", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.05, "evidence": 0.2967}, "jev_score": 0.0148, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Cloudflare", "url": "https://www.cell.com/molecular-therapy-family/oncology/fulltext/S2372-7705(22)00001-8", "domain": "cell.com", "snippet": "If you are using a CNAME origin record, make sure it is valid and resolvable.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-11-03", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.03, "evidence": 0.0067}, "jev_score": 0.0002, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Cloudflare", "url": "https://www.cell.com/molecular-therapy-family/oncology/fulltext/S2372-7705(22)00151-6", "domain": "cell.com", "snippet": "If you are using a CNAME origin record, make sure it is valid and resolvable.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-11-03", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.03, "evidence": 0.0033}, "jev_score": 0.0001, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}], "total_ms": 885.1, "ts": "2026-10-06T12:18:09+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q026-batch", "row_id": "q026", "mode": "batch", "objective": "BM-013 first regulatory approval or market launch year in acute myeloid leukemia", "query": ["BM-013 acute myeloid leukemia regulatory approval launch", "BM-013 BIMINI Biotech approval AML", "BM-013 WASP AML clinical development forecast"], "anchors": ["BM-013"], "search_latency_ms": 831.6, "target": "BM-013", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 321.5, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 7196, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000302, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 1, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.5161, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Portfolio - BIMINI Biotech", "url": "https://biminibiotech.nl/technology-2/", "domain": "biminibiotech.nl", "snippet": "Our WASp inhibitor (BM-013) downregulates the WASp concentration and function by enhancing degradation of the protein, thereby inhibiting the proliferation specifically of leukemic cells.\nTarget indications include Acute myeloid leukemia (AML) and T-Cell Lymphoblastic Leukemia (T-ALL).", "date": "2025-01-01", "last_updated": "2025-09-15", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.95, "evidence": 0.5433}, "jev_score": 0.5161, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Bimini Biotech company information, funding & investors", "url": "https://app.dealroom.co/companies/bimini_biotech", "domain": "app.dealroom.co", "snippet": "BIMINI Biotech's business model revolves around the development and commercialization of its proprietary portfolio of products, including BM-011, a WASp activator, and BM-013, a WASp inhibitor.\nThese products have shown promising results in treating difficult-to-treat non-Hodgkin lymphomas and leukemias, respectively.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-01-18", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.93, "evidence": 0.4867}, "jev_score": 0.4526, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Home | AML Hub", "url": "https://aml-hub.com/drug-updates/1996251866145952164", "domain": "aml-hub.com", "snippet": "China’s NMPA has approved quizartinib in combination with standard induction and consolidation chemotherapy and as maintenance monotherapy for the treatment of ND FLT3-ITD AML, based on results from the phase III QuANTUM-First trial.", "date": "2025-12-03", "last_updated": "2026-09-23", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.42, "evidence": 0.8033}, "jev_score": 0.3374, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Oncology (Cancer)/Hematologic Malignancies Approval ...", "url": "https://www.fda.gov/drugs/resources-information-approved-drugs/oncology-cancerhematologic-malignancies-approval-notifications", "domain": "fda.gov", "snippet": "venetoclax for newly diagnosed acute myeloid leukemia|On May 13, 2026, the Food and Drug Administration approved an oral combination of decitabine and cedazuridine tablets (Inqovi, Taiho Oncology, Inc.) with venetoclax for the treatment of newly diagnosed acute myeloid leukemia (AML) in adults 75 years or older, or who have comorbidities that preclude the use of intensive induction chemotherapy.|5/13/2026|", "date": "2026-09-24", "last_updated": "2026-10-02", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.23, "evidence": 0.56}, "jev_score": 0.1288, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "BIMINI Biotech", "url": "https://biobase.whitefordresearch.com/companies/bimini-biotech", "domain": "biobase.whitefordresearch.com", "snippet": "Its public materials describe the company as advancing lead programs toward first-in-human studies, with current work centered on translational preclinical development and patient-stratification biomarkers.\n...\nThe company's lead emphasis is oncology, particularly aggressive hematological cancers including lymphomas, leukemias, mantle cell lymphoma, and acute myeloid leukemia (AML).\nIn 2026, BIMINI announced the MYELO-WASP Eurostars project, a EUR 2.2 million international consortium intended to complete translational preclinical development of proprietary WASp modulators for myeloid and lymphoid leukemias and generate a preclinical package for CTA-enabling studies and first-in-human trials.\nIt also announced a Health~Holland-backed AML-WASP collaboration with a EUR 440,000 budget to optimize small-molecule WASp activators, validate activity in relapse/refractory AML models, and identify predictive biomarkers for patient stratification.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-15", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.13, "evidence": 0.5567}, "jev_score": 0.0724, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "News – BIMINI Biotech", "url": "https://biminibiotech.nl/category/news/", "domain": "biminibiotech.nl", "snippet": "maart 18, 2026\nLeiden, 18 March 2026 – BIMINI Biotech is pleased to announce that it has been awarded funding through the Health~Holland International Kickstarter Call for Public-Private Partnerships 2025 for a collaborative project focused on advancing first-in-class therapeutics for acute myeloid leukemia (AML).", "date": "2026-09-18", "last_updated": "2026-09-21", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.12, "evidence": 0.3}, "jev_score": 0.036, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Bimini Biotech B.V. - Drug pipelines, Patents, Clinical trials - Synapse", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/organization/b09b26122a623602b4fd5d207414aef3", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "Pipeline Snapshot as of 16 Apr 2026\nNo data posted", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-04-16", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.36, "evidence": 0.0033}, "jev_score": 0.0012, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title