{"case_id": "q030-batch", "row_id": "q030", "mode": "batch", "objective": "SGR-4174 in vivo single-agent monotherapy tumour growth inhibition TGI tumour regression or shrinkage in Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma animal models", "query": ["SGR-4174 Mia PaCa2 monotherapy tumour regression xenograft", "SGR-4174 PDAC xenograft tumour shrinkage single agent"], "anchors": ["SGR-4174"], "search_latency_ms": 473.0, "target": "SGR-4174", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 355.7, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 9237, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000388, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 7, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.7742, "results": [{"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "[PDF] Schrödinger Presents New Preclinical Data at AACR Annual Meeting", "url": "https://s203.q4cdn.com/609444515/files/doc_news/Schrdinger-Presents-New-Preclinical-Data-at-AACR-Annual-Meeting-2025.pdf", "domain": "s203.q4cdn.com", "snippet": "The data also demonstrated that\nSGR-4174 has strong tumor growth inhibition as a monotherapy as well as in combination with MEK or KRAS inhibitors, while maintaining a favorable safety pro�le.\n...\nThe poster (Abstract #4376), “Preclinical characterization of SGR-4174, a potent and selective SOS1 inhibitor for the treatment of pan KRAS mutant cancers in combination with KRAS pathway inhibitors,” includes preclinical data demonstrating the superior binding, selectivity, and drug-like properties of SGR-4147 compared to MRTX0902 as assessed via comprehensive in vitro potency, ADME, and safety assays.\nThe preclinical data for SGR-4174 also show robust suppression of the RAS signaling pathway and potent cell killing activity across multiple cancer types harboring diverse KRAS mutations as well as EGFR and SOS1 mutations.\nSGR-4174 monotherapy achieved dose- dependent target engagement and tumor growth inhibition and induced tumor shrinkage when used in combination with MEK or KRAS inhibitors in preclinical models of pancreatic and non-small cell lung cancer.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-08", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.98, "evidence": 0.79}, "jev_score": 0.7742, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "SGR-4174 / Schrodinger - Larvol Delta", "url": "https://delta.larvol.com/Products/?ProductId=1b3e9eec-7ffe-4dfd-8404-27376217e477", "domain": "delta.larvol.com", "snippet": "## Preclinical characterization of SGR-4174, a potent and selective SOS1 inhibitor for the treatment of pan KRAS mutant cancers in combination with KRAS pathway inhibitors (AACR 2025) - \"Compared to other published SOS1 inhibitors (BI-3406, MRTX0902), SGR-4174 is more potent, and demonstrated similar anti-tumor activity and target engagement at a lower dose both in monotherapy and combination therapy studies in xenograft tumor models. SGR-4174 was well tolerated as monotherapy and in combination with sotorasib or trametinib.SGR-4174 demonstrated favorable PK properties across preclinical species and toxicity profile was well characterized. Our comprehensive preclinical efficacy and safety data support progressing SGR-4174 to clinical development to treat KRAS mutant cancers.\"\n...\n## Schrödinger Presents New Preclinical Data at AACR Annual Meeting (Businesswire) - \"SGR-4174 Data at AACR: The poster (Abstract #4376)...includes preclinical data demonstrating the superior binding, selectivity, and drug-like properties of SGR-4147 compared to MRTX0902 as assessed via comprehensive in vitro potency, ADME, and safety assays. The preclinical data for SGR-4174 also show robust suppression of the RAS signaling pathway and potent cell killing activity across multiple cancer types harboring diverse KRAS mutations as well as EGFR and SOS1 mutations. SGR-4174 monotherapy achieved dose-dependent target engagement and tumor growth inhibition and induced tumor shrinkage when used in combination with MEK or KRAS inhibitors in preclinical models of pancreatic and non-small cell lung cancer.\"", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-30", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.98, "evidence": 0.7233}, "jev_score": 0.7088, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "SOS1: tracking the evolving path from promising to actionable ...", "url": "https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1186/s12943-026-02632-7.pdf?error=cookies_not_supported&code=aa72aed6-b31a-4006-88df-722541dec95c", "domain": "link.springer.com", "snippet": "In vivo studies showed that single-agent administra­\ntion (100 mg/kg; q.d; 21 days) inhibited tumor growth in a PDAC KRASG12C mutated model (Mia PaCa-2) but not in the KRASG12C CRC (SW837) or the KRASG12D PDAC\n(AsPC-1) models.\n...\nOral administration of \nHW071021 as a single-agent significantly caused TGI \n(78%) in a murine xenograft model of KRASG12C-driven \noncogenicity (Mia PaCa-2 cells) [79].\n...\nSGR-4174 appears as a novel, potent, and highly selec­\n…\n...\nMechanistically, SGR-\n4174 binds to the catalytic domain of SOS1, disrupting \nSOS1::KRASG12C PPI (IC50 of 13 nM) [80].\n...\ngraft models, including NSCLC and PDAC, single-agent administration of SGR-4174 elicited dose-dependent\nTGI (please note that we were unable to find the pre­ cise dosing regimens employed in this study) [80].\nIn addition, concomitant administration of SGR-4174 and", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-03", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.96, "evidence": 0.7367}, "jev_score": 0.7072, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "SOS1: tracking the evolving path from promising to actionable ...", "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12943-026-02632-7?error=cookies_not_supported&code=89f85997-69de-41bd-b40a-f6985ed2b656", "domain": "link.springer.com", "snippet": "In vivo assays demonstrated that in monotherapy, BI-3406-mediated SOS1 inhibition (50 mg/kg; bid; 21 days) resulted in potent tumor volume reduction in certain KRAS oncogenic xenograft models including G12C (PDAC: Mia PaCa-2), G12S (NSCLC: A549), G12V (PDAC: PATX53; CRC: SW620), and G13D (CRC: LoVo) [39].\n...\nIn vivo studies showed that single-agent administration (100 mg/kg; q.d; 21 days) inhibited tumor growth in a PDAC KRAS^G12C^ mutated model (Mia PaCa-2) but not in the KRAS^G12C^ CRC (SW837) or the KRAS^G12D^ PDAC (AsPC-1) models.\n...\nOral administration of HW071021 as a single-agent significantly caused TGI (78%) in a murine xenograft model of KRAS^G12C^-driven oncogenicity (Mia PaCa-2 cells) [79].\n...\nMechanistically, SGR-4174 binds to the catalytic domain of SOS1, disrupting SOS1::KRAS^G12C^ PPI (IC~50~ of 13 nM) [80].\nPreclinical in vitro studies demonstrated that SGR-4174 administration (IC~50~ values ranging from 42 to107 nM) resulted in a robust impairment of RAS-mediated signal transduction and reduced cell viability (3D cell cultures) across a wide spectrum of human cancer cell lines harboring KRAS^G12C/D/V^ or EGFR mutations (since we had no access to the panel of the specific cells employed in this study, this information is not included in Supplementary Table 2) [80].\nIn KRAS^G12C^-mutated tumor xenograft models, including NSCLC and PDAC, single-agent administration of SGR-4174 elicited dose-dependent TGI (please note that we were unable to find the precise dosing regimens employed in this study) [80].\nIn addition, concomitant administration of SGR-4174 and Sotorasib resulted in a deeper and more sustained inhibition of KRAS signal transmission, pronounced inhibition of cell proliferation in 3D cell culture models, and tumor regression in xenograft models when compared to the respective monotherapies [80].\nFurthermore, along with Trametinib, SGR-4174 prevented the reactivation of the KRAS pathway, which is typically observed upon MEK inhibition, leading to increased tumor cell death and stasis [80].", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-19", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.95, "evidence": 0.71}, "jev_score": 0.6745, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Schrödinger, Inc. - Drug pipelines, Patents, Clinical trials", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/organization/ee6d0cc13d500f7740bc81b30e6d5978", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "In KRAS G12C tumor xenograft mouse models, SGR-4174 monotherapy demonstrated dose-dependent tumor growth inhibition and a decrease in the tumor PD biomarker pERK.SGR-4174 was examined in combination with KRAS G12C inhibitor sotorasib and MEK inhibitor trametinib in vitro and in vivo.\nWhen combined with sotorasib, SGR-4174 demonstrated deeper and more sustained inhibition of the KRAS signaling pathway, stronger suppression of cell proliferation in 3D cell culture, and tumor regression in xenograft models compared to single agent activity.\n...\nCompared to other published SOS1 inhibitors (BI-3406, MRTX0902), SGR-4174 is more potent, and demonstrated similar anti-tumor activity and target engagement at a lower dose both in monotherapy and combination therapy studies in xenograft tumor models.\nSGR-4174 was well tolerated as monotherapy and in combination with sotorasib or trametinib.SGR-4174 demonstrated favorable PK properties across preclinical species and toxicity profile was well characterized.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-10-02", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.98, "evidence": 0.6767}, "jev_score": 0.6632, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Tumor growth inhib News", "url": "https://sigma.larvol.com/moa.php?MoaId=705294&tab=newstrac&page=21", "domain": "sigma.larvol.com", "snippet": "SGR-4174\nSchrodinger\nPreclinical characterization of SGR-4174, a potent and selective SOS1 inhibitor for the treatment of pan KRAS mutant cancers in combination with KRAS pathway inhibitors\n(Section 21; Poster Board No: 11) - Mar 25, 2025 - Abstract #AACR2025AACR_6921;\nSGR-4174 was well tolerated as monotherapy and in combination with sotorasib or trametinib.SGR-4174 demonstrated favorable PK properties across preclinical species and toxicity profile was well characterized.\nOur comprehensive preclinical efficacy and safety data support progressing SGR-4174 to clinical development to treat KRAS mutant cancers.\n...\nSimilarly, treatment of multiple BRAFV600E patient-derived xenograft (PDX, N=3) models of CRC resulted in synergistic triplet TGI (80%, 102%, 103%) and some tumor regression compared against monotherapy azenosertib (43%, 19%, 51%) or the E + C doublet (49%, 65%, 87%).", "date": "2025-03-25", "last_updated": "2025-07-26", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.97, "evidence": 0.6567}, "jev_score": 0.637, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Preclinical assessment with clinical validation of Selinexor ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7073299/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Compared to single or dual combination, selinexor-GEM-nab-paclitaxel resulted in almost complete loss of the PDAC spheroids (Figure 2A and 2B).\n...\nSelinexor given at 15 mg/kg every other day x 3weeks caused statistically significant reduction in the growth of PDAC CSC xenograft (Figure 6A and Supplementary Figure 7A, p<0.01).\n...\nContinued shrinkage was seen in each of the subsequent restaging CT scans for at least eight months and was followed by disease stability (Figure 6H).", "date": "2019-12-12", "last_updated": "2025-10-08", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.64, "evidence": 0.7833}, "jev_score": 0.5013, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Schrödinger to Present Preclinical Data at AACR Annual ...", "url": "https://s203.q4cdn.com/609444515/files/doc_news/Schrdinger-to-Present-Preclinical-Data-at-AACR-Annual-Meeting-2025.pdf", "domain": "s203.q4cdn.com", "snippet": "The SGR-4174 presentation will include data characterizing SGR-4174 in preclinical oncology models.\n...\nPreclinical data suggest SGR-\n4174 has a di�erentiated pro�le and support its continued development as a potential therapy for patients with\nKRAS mutant cancers and in rare diseases arising from KRAS pathway mutations.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-12-17", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.97, "evidence": 0.4767}, "jev_score": 0.4624, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Delving into the Latest Updates on SGR-4174 with Synapse", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/drug/b8e102fe163a4c6eabf652c6ce503310", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "Clinical Result\n...\nPhase\n...\nAnalyzed Enrollment\n...\nEvaluation\nPublication Date\nNo Data", "date": "2025-04-05", "last_updated": "2025-04-19", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.92, "evidence": 0.05}, "jev_score": 0.046, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "MIA PaCa-2 Xenograft Model", "url": "https://altogenlabs.com/xenograft-models/pancreatic-xenograft-models/miapaca-2-xenograft-model/", "domain": "altogenlabs.com", "snippet": "The MIA PaCa-2 cell line (human pancreas) is used to create the CDX (Cell Line Derived Xenograft) MIA PaCa-2 xenograft mouse model.\nThe MIA PaCa-2 xenograft model exhibits tumor growth inhibition and chemosensitization utilizing monotherapies or in combination (e.g. sulindac, LC-1, parthenolide, gemcitabine).", "date": "2023-03-07", "last_updated": "2026-10-03", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.05, "evidence": 0.2767}, "jev_score": 0.0138, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}], "total_ms": 828.7, "ts": "2026-10-06T12:18:10+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q029-batch", "row_id": "q029", "mode": "batch", "objective": "TP-317 in vivo monotherapy tumour growth inhibition TGI tumour regression tumour shrinkage in Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma animal models", "query": ["TP-317 KPC pancreatic cancer tumour regression monotherapy", "TP-317 KPC PDAC dose schedule monotherapy"], "anchors": ["TP-317 KPC", "TP-317"], "search_latency_ms": 567.6, "target": "TP-317 KPC / TP-317", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 409.5, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 8724, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000366, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 3, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.8709, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Abstract 1135: TP317, a first-in-class resolvin E1 small molecule, potentiates the efficacy of immune checkpoint (ICI) inhibitors in ICI-resistant and ICI-sensitive tumors", "url": "https://aacrjournals.org/cancerres/article/83/7_Supplement/1135/722183/Abstract-1135-TP317-a-first-in-class-resolvin-E1", "domain": "aacrjournals.org", "snippet": "Methods: Subcutaneous murine models of lung (LLC), melanoma (B16F10), and pancreatic (Panc02; KPC) tumors were used to investigate TP-317 monotherapy and combinations with ICI.\n...\nIn KRAS-mutant KPC tumors (N=10/group), TP317 (7.5 µg Q6D) demonstrated significant anti-tumor activity compared to placebo (500 ± 110 vs 1314 ± 106 mm 3; p<0.001) and was comparable to anti-PD1, while TP-317 + anti-PD1 was superior to anti-PD1 alone (353 ± 82 vs 710 ± 106 mm 3; p<0.01).\nIn Panc02 and KPC models, TP317’s anti-tumor efficacy was attenuated by CD8+ T cell or NK cell depletion.\n...\nConclusions: TP317 monotherapy and ICI combinations significantly inhibited tumor growth in various murine models of cancer.\n...\nIn summary, TP317, an RvE1 drug with a high therapeutic index, has potent single agent efficacy and offers a novel approach in combination with ICI to treat ICI-resistant and ICI-sensitive tumors.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-01-17", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.95, "evidence": 0.9167}, "jev_score": 0.8709, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Award | SBIR", "url": "https://www.sbir.gov/awards/203006", "domain": "sbir.gov", "snippet": "To address this opportunity, Thetis is developing TP-317, a Resolvin E1 (RvE1) therapy for PC.\n...\nPreliminary research conducted in collaboration with Dr. Dipak\nPanigrahy’s Lab (Harvard Medical School) has shown that TP-317 can suppress tumor growth in multiple animal\nmodels as a single agent and has an additive effect with chemotherapy.\n...\nThe overall goal of this Fast Track grant is\nto complete key preclinical studies to support an investigational new drug (IND) application and advance TP-317\ninto clinical trials for the treatment of PC.\n...\nPhase I, Specific Aim 1: To investigate TP-317 in Murine Pancreatic Cancer Models.\nThe efficacy of TP-\n317 will be assessed in two established mouse models of PC, the KPC model and a patient derived xenograft\nmodel.\n...\nPhase I Milestones: Improvement in survival and tumor volume greater than 25% with TP-317 monotherapy or\ncombination arm compared to placebo or active comparator and identification of biomarkers that correlate with\nresponse/non-response preclinically, are clinically significant in PC and are easily assayed in clinical trials.\nPhase II, Specific Aim 2.\n...\nPhase II, Specific Aim 3.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-06-09", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.96, "evidence": 0.7233}, "jev_score": 0.6944, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Firm | SBIR", "url": "https://www.sbir.gov/portfolio/406944", "domain": "sbir.gov", "snippet": "The efficacy of TP- 317 will be assessed in two established mouse models of PC, the KPC model and a patient derived xenograft model.\n...\nPhase I Milestones: Improvement in survival and tumor volume greater than 25% with TP-317 monotherapy or combination arm compared to placebo or active comparator and identification of biomarkers that correlate with response/non-response preclinically, are clinically significant in PC and are easily assayed in clinical trials.\nPhase II, Specific Aim 2.\n...\nPhase II, Specific Aim 3.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-06-09", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.95, "evidence": 0.6833}, "jev_score": 0.6491, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Unbiased in vivo preclinical evaluation of anticancer drugs identifies ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7720119/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Among 57 drug conditions screened, combined inhibition of heat shock protein (Hsp)-90 and MEK was found to produce robust suppression of tumor growth, leading to an 80% increase in the survival of PDAC-bearing mice with no significant toxicity.\n...\n() A waterfall representation of the average response of PDAC orthotopic transplants following a 2-wk treatment with the indicated monotherapies.\nNote that none of the drugs caused tumor regression (mean ± SEM; = 4 per group; * < 0.05, ** < 0.01; one-way ANOVA).\n...\nNone of the 16 monotherapies elicited a notable tumor regression following 2 wk of drug administration using concentrations identified in previously published literature (Fig. 1*C*).\nHowever, several drugs, including gemcitabine (GEM), trametinib, a MAPK/ERK kinase (MEK)-1/2 inhibitor, and PU-H71, a heat-shock protein (HSP)-90 inhibitor with specificity for tumor HSP90 variants (11–14), significantly reduced tumor growth when compared to control.\n...\n|CDK4/6|Palbociclib|50|QD|p.o.|Sodium lactate buffer (50 mM, pH 4.0)|\n|Microtubule inhibitor|Abraxane|30|1x/week|i.p.|Saline|\n|PI3K|Alpelisib|50|QD|i.p.|1% (wt/vol) Carboxymethylcellulose (CMC)+0.5% (wt/vol) Tween 80|\n|RTK|Sunitinib|40|QD|p.o.|0.5% Carboxy-methylcellulose, ph3.5|\n|JAK1/2|Ruxolitinib|60|QD|p.o.|0.1% Tween 80|\n|HSP90|PU-H71|75|3x/week|p.o.|Phosphate buffer|\n|EGFR|Erlotinib|25|QD|p.o.|0.5% methylcellulose|\n...\n|DNA|Gemcitabine|400|1x/week|i.p.|Saline|\n|MEK1/2|Trametinib|2|QD|p.o.|0.5% HPMC+0.2%Tween 80|\n|Src|Dasatinib|30|BID|p.o.|80 mM sodium citrate pH 3.1|\n...\nIn contrast, the T/P combination was highly effective at impairing tumor growth and produced a significant survival benefit, extending the median survival of mice by 1.8-fold over control (83 d vs. 46 d, *P* < 0.001, Fig. 2*C*).", "date": "2020-11-16", "last_updated": "2025-10-17", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.16, "evidence": 0.3}, "jev_score": 0.048, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Graphical Abstract", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/view-large/figure/573834660/oyag337f5.tif?itm_medium=graphical+abstract+image&itm_content=open+image&itm_source=http://academic.oup.com/oncolo/advance-article/doi/10.1093/oncolo/oyag337/8768668&itm_campaign=graphical+abstract", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "snippet": "Graphical Abstract | Oxford Academic \nGraphical Abstract\n...\nEach link will stop working after 30 days or 10 uses.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-08", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.56, "evidence": 0.04}, "jev_score": 0.0224, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "�", "url": "https://cdn.clinicaltrials.gov/large-docs/99/NCT03814499/Prot_SAP_000.pdf", "domain": "cdn.clinicaltrials.gov", "snippet": "� ������������������������������\n...\n���������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-08-30", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.46, "evidence": 0.0467}, "jev_score": 0.0215, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "���������", "url": "https://cdn.clini