{"case_id": "q039-batch", "row_id": "q039", "mode": "batch", "objective": "Inflammatory Bowel Disease competing agents in the same mechanistic class as KPG-818, including Small Molecule and Aiolos CRBN Ikaros therapies", "query": ["KPG-818 Aiolos CRBN Ikaros inflammatory bowel disease competitors", "KPG-818 CELMoD IBD clinical pipeline", "KPG-818 CRBN degrader inflammatory bowel disease"], "anchors": ["KPG-818"], "search_latency_ms": 464.8, "target": "drugs competing with KPG-818: other agents in the same mechanistic class or for the same indication, as described in `objective`", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 734.5, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 10874, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000457, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 3, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.744, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Epaldeudomide - Drug Targets, Indications, Patents - Synapse", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/drug/d9e6cffea08e4b19a31bb148c2ddeb00", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "# Epaldeudomide\n...\n|Target CRBN x IKZF1 x IKZF3 x IL17RB|\n|Action modulators, degraders|\n|Mechanism CRBN modulators(Cereblon modulators), IKZF1 degraders(DNA-binding protein Ikaros degraders), IKZF3 degraders(Zinc finger protein Aiolos degraders)|\n...\n|Active Indication Systemic Lupus ErythematosusInflammatory Bowel DiseasesAdult T-Cell Leukemia-Lymphoma+ [9]|\n...\n|Drug Highest PhasePhase 2|\n...\n|Inflammatory Bowel Diseases|Phase 2|China|Kangpu Biopharmaceuticals Ltd.|-|", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-04-15", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.9, "evidence": 0.8267}, "jev_score": 0.744, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "A Novel Paradigm for Targeting Challenging Targets: Advancing Technologies and Future Directions of Molecular Glue Degraders", "url": "https://www.mdpi.com/1420-3049/31/3/459", "domain": "mdpi.com", "snippet": "Molecular glue degraders (MGDs) constitute a class of innovative therapeutic agents within the field of targeted protein degradation (TPD).\nIn contrast to proteolysis-targeting chimeras (PROTACs), MGDs induce protein degradation by stabilizing the interaction between an E3 ubiquitin ligase and a target protein.\n...\nTo date, three molecular glue drugs have been approved worldwide: thalidomide and its derivatives (lenalidomide and pomalidomide) [7].\n...\nIt was not until 2010 that Japanese scientists identified cereblon (CRBN) as the key target protein of thalidomide [53,54].\nSubsequent research gradually elucidated its mechanism of action: by binding to CRBN, thalidomide redirects the CRL4CRBN E3 ubiquitin ligase complex, inducing proteasomal degradation of novel substrates, including the transcription factors Ikaros and Aiolos encoded by the genes IKZF1 and IKZF3.\n...\nMezigdomide (**CC-99282**) [41], which promotes degradation of IKZF1 and IKZF3 via CRBN E3 ligase engagement, is currently under clinical evaluation both as monotherapy and in combination regimens.\n**KPG-818** (The chemical structure has not been disclosed yet) [61], a novel orally available CRBN modulator designed by Kling Biopharma with global intellectual property rights, is being investigated in a Phase 1, multicenter, open-label, multiple ascending dose study to evaluate its safety, pharmacokinetics, and preliminary efficacy in combination with dexamethasone in adults with multiple myeloma, as well as a monotherapy in selected hematologic malignancies including mantle cell lymphoma.\n...\nWang et al. established a FRET-based screening platform to monitor IKZF2–CRBN dimerization and, through structure-guided optimization supported by protein crystallography, successfully developed the selective IKZF2 molecular glues ALV1 and ALV2 (Figure 5) [90].\nCompared to lenalidomide and CC-885 (Figure 5), both compounds induce significantly stronger CRBN–Helios interactions.", "date": "2026-01-28", "last_updated": "2026-08-11", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.83, "evidence": 0.55}, "jev_score": 0.4565, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "epaldeudomide (KPG-818) / Kangpu Biopharma - Larvol Delta", "url": "https://delta.larvol.com/Products/?ProductId=06e8ceca-7e58-4124-a47c-d90d42c4951a", "domain": "delta.larvol.com", "snippet": "## Kangpu Biopharmaceuticals Received CDE Approval for Phase IIb Clinical Trial of KPG-818 in Moderate to Severe Cutaneous Manifestations of SLE (PRNewswire) - \"Kangpu Biopharmaceuticals, Ltd. announced today that the Center for Drug Evaluation (CDE) of China's National Medical Products Administration (NMPA) has approved Phase IIb clinical trial of KPG-818 capsule for the treatment of moderate to severe cutaneous manifestations of systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE).\"\n...\n## Therapeutic effects of KPG-818 in a trinitrobenzene sulfonic acid - induced mouse Crohn's/Ulcerative Colitis model (ECCO-IBD 2024) - \"KPG-818 is a novel generation of lenalidomide derivative and small molecule immunomodulatory drug...KPG-818 showed similar therapeutic effects to Upadacitinib. Conclusion In this mouse model of chronic Crohn’s/ulcerative colitis, KPG-818 showed remarkable therapeutic effects in a dose-dependent manner. These data encourage the clinical evaluation of KPG-818 in inflammatory bowel diseases.\"\n...\n## KPG-818, a Novel Cereblon (CRBN) Modulator, in Patients with SLE: Results of a Phase Ib Multiple Ascending Dose Study (ACR Convergence 2022) - P1b/2a | \"KPG 818 was generally well-tolerated in this SLE population over a 14-day of treatment. The drug-related AEs, mainly skin and hematologic disorders, were clinically manageable. Treatment with KPG-818 resulted in robust and dose-dependent modulatory effects including degradation of Aiolos, B cell depletion, down-regulation of pDC, and up-regulation of Treg.\"", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-28", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.79, "evidence": 0.5333}, "jev_score": 0.4213, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Multiple Investigational Approaches Show Promise for Lupus", "url": "https://www.medscape.com/viewarticle/multiple-investigational-approaches-show-promise-treatment-2024a1000e5y", "domain": "medscape.com", "snippet": "In a systematic review published last year, Arnaud and coauthors found that there were 92 investigational biologic or novel targeted agents in various phases of clinical testing.\nThis included B-cell–targeting agents such as ianalumab, plasma cell-targeting agents such as daratumumab, and drugs with novel mechanisms of action such as KPG-818, which targets the CRL4-Cereblon (CRBN) E3 ubiquitin ligase complex.\nPhase 2 data on all three of these investigational agents were presented during various sessions at EULAR 2024, all with positive results, suggesting that their further development in SLE is worth pursuing.\n...\nElsewhere at EULAR 2024, positive results of another phase 2 study involving a drug with an entirely different mechanism of action, KPG-818, were reported in a poster presentation.\nKPG-818 modulates CRBN, which results in the degradation of two transcription factors (Aiolos and Ikaros) that are involved in the development, maturation, and proliferation of innate and adaptive immune cells and have been linked to genetic risk in SLE, according to the poster's authors.\nIt is currently in development for the treatment of SLE, Behçet disease, inflammatory bowel disease, multiple myeloma, and non-Hodgkin lymphoma.\nYao Wang, MD, chief medical officer of KPG-818's developer Kangpu Biopharmaceuticals, Hefei, China, and associates found that oral doses of 0.15 or 0.6 mg KPG-818 were \"generally well-tolerated\" and produced immunomodulatory changes that could be beneficial in people with SLE over a 12-week treatment period.\n...\nCompared with placebo, they observed reduced numbers of total B cells, Aiolos+ T and B cells, and increased T reg cells.\n...\n\"The proof-of-concept findings suggest a favorable benefit/risk ratio in SLE for KPG-818,\" Wang and coauthors said, supporting its further development in SLE.", "date": "2024-08-01", "last_updated": "2025-11-03", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.62, "evidence": 0.4733}, "jev_score": 0.2934, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Chongqing Kangpu Chemical Industry Co Ltd.", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/organization/566a91a30b9da1a0f8337a5e2aa3cf14", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "Kangpu Completed Bridging Clinical Study of KPG-818 in China\nHEFEI, China, Sept. 20, 2024 /PRNewswire/ -- Kangpu Biopharmaceuticals, a clinical-stage company based in Hefei, China, announced today that the Company has successfully completed a bridging clinical study of KPG-818 in healthy subjects in China.\nThe randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled, and food effects (FE) study aimed to assess the safety and tolerability of multiple oral doses of KPG-818 in male and female healthy participants over a treatment period of 14 days and a follow-up period of 4 weeks, to characterize the single and multiple oral dose pharmacokinetics (PK) of KPG-818, and to select dosing regimens of KPG-818 for future clinical trials.\nA total of 30 participants were randomized to three escalating cohorts (n=10 each; 8 KPG-818, 2 placebo) receiving 0.15 mg, 0.6 mg, 2 mg or placebo once daily.\n...\nKPG-818 is well tolerated in healthy subjects.\nNo serious adverse events (SAEs) were reported.\nThe most common AEs were grades 1-2 of pruritus or rash.\n...\nKPG-818 is a small molecule modulator of CRBN E3 ubiquitin ligase complex CRL4-CRBN.\nIn the Phase Ib/IIa clinical study in SLE patients (trial ID: NCT04643067) completed in the US, KPG-818 demonstrated promising efficacy and a favorable safety and tolerability profile.\n...\nPoster tour title: Clinical Poster Tours: Systemic lupus - Of old and new therapies Poster tour date and time: Wednesday, June 12, 2024, 15:30 - 16:30 CEST Poster tour location: Hall C The Phase 2a trial is a multicenter, randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled study, to assess the safety and tolerability, pharmacokinetics, and preliminary efficacy of KPG-818 in patients with SLE, and to select dosing regimens for Phase 2b study.\nPatients were randomized in a 1:1:1:1 ratio to receive KPG-818 capsules at doses of 0.15 mg, 0.6 mg, 2 mg, or placebo orally once daily for 12 weeks.\n...\nThe results showed that KPG-818 0.15 mg and 0.6 mg were generally well tolerated after 12 weeks of drug administration.\nCompared with placebo arm, 0.15 mg arm exhibited obvious improvements in SLEDAI-2K and CLASI scores at week 12.\nKPG-818 demonstrated a favorable pharmacokinetic profile, reduced the total B cell count and increased Treg in SLE patients.\n...\nDiscovered by Kangpu Biopharmaceuticals and acting as molecular glue, KPG-818 is a modulator of Cereblon (CRBN) E3 ubiquitin ligase complex CRL4CRBN.\nThe compound demonstrated potent degradation of Aiolos (IKZF3) and Ikaros (IKZF1).\nKPG-818 showed outstanding in vitro anti-inflammatory properties, broad spectrum of anti-proliferative activities, and remarkable in vivo efficacy in multiple blood cancer animal models.\nA bridge study (CTR20241116) for the treatment of SLE has been initiated in China.\nMeanwhile, a Phase 1 study of KPG-818 for the treatment of patients with hematological malignancies is being completed in the United States (NCT04283097).\n...\nDiscovered by Kangpu Biopharmaceuticals, KPG-121 is a modulator of the Cereblon (CRBN) E3 ubiquitin ligase complex CRL4CRBN targeting rapid ubiquitination and degradation of casein kinase 1α (CK1α) and transcription factors Aiolos (IKZF3) and Ikaros (IKZF1).", "date": "2025-10-02", "last_updated": "2025-10-02", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.65, "evidence": 0.3933}, "jev_score": 0.2556, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "KPG-818, a Novel Cereblon (CRBN) Modulator, in Patients with SLE", "url": "https://acrabstracts.org/abstract/kpg-818-a-novel-cereblon-crbn-modulator-in-patients-with-sle-results-of-a-phase-ib-multiple-ascending-dose-study/", "domain": "acrabstracts.org", "snippet": "**Background/Purpose:** KPG-818 is a novel CRL4-CRBN E3 ubiquitin ligase modulator that binds to CRBN with high affinity and leads to rapid and effective degradation of Aiolos and Ikaros, the transcription factors that play critical roles in B cell development and proliferation.\nKPG-818 is currently in development for the treatment of SLE as well as hematological malignancies.\nThis Phase 1b study (NCT04643067) evaluated the safety, tolerability, pharmacokinetics (PK), and potential biomarkers of KPG-818 in patients with mild to moderate SLE.\n...\nIn comparison to baseline, KPG-818 decreased the percentage of B cells in leukocytes, downregulated Aiolos, increased the percentage of Treg, and decreased the percentage of pDC in a dose-dependent manner.\n...\nTreatment with KPG-818 resulted in robust and dose-dependent modulatory effects including degradation of Aiolos, B cell depletion, down-regulation of pDC, and up-regulation of Treg.", "date": "2022-09-01", "last_updated": "2026-10-02", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.73, "evidence": 0.3033}, "jev_score": 0.2214, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "[PDF] Protocol - ClinicalTrials.gov", "url": "https://cdn.clinicaltrials.gov/large-docs/67/NCT04643067/Prot_000.pdf", "domain": "cdn.clinicaltrials.gov", "snippet": "This is a Phase 1b/2a multicenter study to evaluate the safety, PK, PD, and clinical efficacy of KPG-818 in patients with SLE.\nThe trial will consist of 2 parts: Phase 1b, a multiple-ascending dose (MAD) study; and Phase 2a, a proof of concept (POC) study.\n...\nKPG-818 is a novel CRBN (Cereblon) modulator, part of the cullin 4-RING E3 ubiquitin ligase complex CRL4-CRBN.1 KPG-818 was discovered by Kangpu Biopharmaceuticals,\nLtd.’s drug discovery program aiming at the design of novel and next-generation immunomodulatory agents.\nKPG-818 is structurally similar to Celgene’s new investigational drug CC-220, but with greater CRBN binding affinity.\nResults from in vitro studies in human peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMC) demonstrated that KPG-818 significantly inhibits levels of the inflammatory cytokines interleukin (IL)-6 and tumor necrosis factor\n(TNF)-α, and CRBN substrate proteins Aiolos and Ikaros.\nBased on these available data, \nKangpu Biopharmaceuticals, Ltd. is developing KPG-818 for the treatment of patients with\nSLE as the intended indication.\n...\nThe data indicated that CRBN is the binding target of \nKPG-818.\n...\no  PD markers (Aiolos [intracellular protein], Ikaros [intracellular protein], CRBN ", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-25", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.62, "evidence": 0.3567}, "jev_score": 0.2212, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Kangpu Biopharmaceuticals Ltd. - Drug pipelines, Patents, ...", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/organization/a3ed44b7c744ea291a03d6118706097c", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "Kangpu specializes in creating small molecule drugs that target immuno-modulating sites for the treatment of cancer, autoimmune diseases, and inflammation.\n...\nCTR20241116\n/ CompletedPhase 1\n随机、双盲、安慰剂对照I期临床研究评价KPG-818胶囊多次口服给药后在中国健康受试者中的安全性和耐受性以及药代动力学特征和食物影响试验\n[Translation] A randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled Phase I clinical study to evaluate the safety and tolerability of KPG-818 capsules after multiple oral administration in healthy Chinese subjects, as well as the pharmacokinetic characteristics and food effect test\nNCT04283097\n/ CompletedPhase 1\nA Phase 1, Multicenter, Open-label, Multiple-ascending Dose Study to Evaluate the Safety, Pharmacokinetics and Clinical Activity of KPG-818 in Subjects With Hematological Malignancies\nNCT04643067\n/ CompletedPhase 1/2\nA Phase 1b/2a Multicenter Study to Assess the Safety and Tolerability, Pharmacokinetics, and Preliminary Efficacy of KPG-818 in Patients With Systemic Lupus Erythematosus\n...\nKangpu to Present Latest Study Results of epaldeudomide (KPG-818) at the 67th ASH Annual Meeting\n...\n### Pipeline Snapshot as of 04 Sep 2026\nThe statistics for drugs in the Pipeline is the current organization and its subsidiaries are counted as organizations,Early Phase 1 is incorporated into Phase 1, Phase 1/2 is incorporated into phase 2, and phase 2/3 is incorporated into phase 3\nPreclinical\n4\n2\nPhase 2\nOther\n7", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-04", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.63, "evidence": 0.35}, "jev_score": 0.2205, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Kangpu Biopharmaceuticals | Drug Developments | Pipeline Prospector", "url": "https://www.pharmacompass.com/pipeline-prospector-drugs-in-development/kangpu-biopharmaceuticals", "domain": "pharmacompass.com", "snippet": "Kangpu gets CDE Nod for KPG-818 Phase IIb in Severe SLE Skin Symptoms **\n...\nKPG-818 is a novel oral molecular glue modulator of the E3 ubiquitin ligase complex CRL4-CRBN.\nIt is being evaluated for the treatment of systemic lupus erythematosus.\n...\nKangpu Completed Bridging Clinical Study of KPG-818 in China **\n...\nKPG-818 is a small molecule modulator of CRBN E3 ubiquitin ligase complex CRL4-CRBN.\nIt is being evaluated for the treatment of Systemic Lupus Erythematosus.\n...\nKPG-121 is a modulator of the CRL4CRBN complex targeting rapid ubiquitination and degradation of CK1 alpha, IKZF3 and IKZF1.\nIt is being investigated in combination with Abiraterone for mCRPC.\n...\nKangpu Completes Patient Enrollment for Phase IIa Clinical Trial of KPG-818 in SLE in the US **\n...\nKPG-818 is a novel generation of small molecule modulators of CRBN E3 ubiquitin ligase complex CRL4-CRBN.\nIt provides a potent induction of the ubiquitination and degradation of Aiolos (IKZF3) and Ikaros (IKZF1), factors critical in B-cell development.\n...\nKangpu Announces First Patient Dosed in the US Phase IIa Clinical Trial of NeoMIDES™ Molecular Glue KPG-818 ...KPG-818 is novel cereblon E3 ubiquitin ligase complex modulator for treatment of autoimmune diseases and hematologic malignancies, demonstrated good tolerability, a favorable pharmacokinetic profile, and substantial potency for the degradation of Ikaros ...Kangpu Biopharmaceuticals Initiates Phase Ib/IIa Clinical Trial of KPG-818 in Systemic Lupus Erythematosus **\n...\nThe Phase Ib/IIa multicenter, randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled clinical study will evaluate the safety and tolerability, pharmacokinetics and preliminary efficacy of KPG-818 in patients with mild to moderate SLE.", "date": "2025-04-28", "last_updated": "2025-05-02", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.6, "evidence": 0.35}, "jev_score": 0.21, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Future of Lupus Treatments Looks Brighter With Multiple Promising Therapeutic Approaches | MDedge", "url": "https://www.mdedge.com/content/future-lupus-treatments-looks-brighter-multiple-promising-therapeutic-approaches/page/0/2", "domain": "mdedge.com", "snippet": "Elsewhere at EULAR 2024, positive results of another phase 2 study involving a drug with an entirely different mechanism of action, KPG-818, were reported in a poster presentation.\nKPG-818 modulates CRBN, which results in the degradation of two transcription factors (Aiolos and Ikaros) that are involved in the development, maturation, and proliferation of innate and adaptive immune cells and have been linked to genetic risk in SLE, according to the poster’s authors.\nIt is currently in development for the treatment of SLE, Behçet disease, inflammatory bowel disease, multiple myeloma, and non-Hodgkin lymphoma.\nYao Wang, MD, chief medical officer of KPG-818’s developer Kangpu Biopharmaceuticals, Hefei, China, and associates found that oral doses of 0.15 or 0.6 mg KPG-818 were “generally well-tolerated” and produced immunomodulatory changes that could be beneficial in people with SLE over a 12-week treatment period.\n...\nCompared with placebo, they observed reduced numbers of total B cells, Aiolos+ T and B cells, and increased Treg cells.\n...\n“The proof-of-concept findings suggest a favorable benefit/risk ratio in SLE for KPG-818,” Dr. Wang and coauthors said, supporting its further development in SLE.\n...\nDr. Isenberg told this news organization that both daratumumab and KPG-818 would be welcome additions as treatment options if further trials proved their worth.\n...\nDaratumumab and KPG-818 are “different types of molecules, and if they work that will be great.\nIt would be nice to have the choice,” Dr. Isenberg said.", "date": "2024-08-02", "last_updated": "2026-09-20", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.58, "evidence": 0.3467}, "jev_score": 0.2011, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}], "total_ms": 1199.3, "ts": "2026-10-06T12:18:13+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q040-batch", "row_id": "q040", "mode": "batch", "objective": "Active Ulcerative Colitis Moderate to Severe Ulcerative Colitis competing agents in the same mechanistic class as CHST15 Oligonucleotide Small Interfering RNA therapies", "query": ["GUT-1 CHST15 ulcerative colitis competitors", "GUT-1 siRNA CHST15 ulcerative colitis", "GUT-1 CHST15 moderate to severe ulcerative colitis pipeline"], "anchors": ["GUT-1"], "search_latency_ms": 474.5, "target": "drugs competing with GUT-1: other agents in the same mechanistic class or for the same indication, as described in `objective`", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 530.5, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 10500, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000441, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 4, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.6946, "results": [{"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Research progress in molecular targeted therapy for inflammatory ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12797379/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Tofacitinib (JAK1/JAK3 inhibitor) demonstrated efficacy comparable to that of biologics in TNF-α inhibitor–naive UC and maintains superiority over placebo; it is approved for moderate to severe UC.\nTrials in CD did not meet primary endpoints and development was halted [42, 43].\n...\nTocilizumab and PF-04236921 have completed clinical trials, while other programs have been terminated due to insufficient efficacy or safety concerns [45].\n...\nPF-00547659, a fully human monoclonal antibody targeting MAdCAM-1, selectively reduces lymphocyte gut homing for moderate to severe UC.\n...\nOzanimod is approved by the FDA for moderate to severe UC.\n...\nCarbohydrate sulfotransferase 15(CHST15) is involved in biosynthesis of sulfated glycosaminoglycans and is associated with intestinal inflammation and fibrosis.\nGUT-1, a double-stranded RNA oligonucleotide targeting CHST15, was evaluated in a Phase IIa study of 28 UC patients unresponsive to conventional treatments.\nSingle-dose administration (25 nM, 250 nM) was compared with placebo; the primary endpoint was endoscopic improvement at weeks 2 and 4.\nIn the 250 nM group, endoscopic improvement was 71.4%, clinical remission 57.1%, and histological improvement 42.9%, significantly higher than the 25 nM group (0%) and placebo (28.6%, 14.3%, 0%).\nGUT-1 reduces CHST15 expression, inhibiting mucosal inflammation and fibrosis, and may serve as an inducible therapy to regulate tissue remodeling in UC [55].\n...\nSystematic reviews and network meta-analyses indicate that infliximab has the highest clinical response and remission rates for induction in moderate to severe UC, while ozanimod is most effective for inducing mucosal healing.\n...\nAfter anti TNF failure or intolerance, vedolizumab and ustekinumab are favored for their safety and gut selectivity, whereas JAK and S1P modulators provide oral options for patients preferring non injectable therapy or requiring systemic anti-inflammatory potency.\n...\nCurrent therapeutic classes—including anti‑TNF agents, integrin antagonists, IL‑12/23 and IL‑23 inhibitors, Janus kinase inhibitors, and S1P modulators—have substantially improved remission rates and durability in moderate to severe disease, particularly for patients unresponsive to conventional treatments.", "date": "2025-12-05", "last_updated": "2026-08-20", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.91, "evidence": 0.7633}, "jev_score": 0.6946, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Research progress in molecular targeted therapy for inflammatory ...", "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12967-025-07385-3?error=cookies_not_supported&code=1e53ce24-191f-48ed-a22e-45f1dec47600", "domain": "link.springer.com", "snippet": "Carbohydrate sulfotransferase 15(CHST15) is involved in biosynthesis of sulfated glycosaminoglycans and is associated with intestinal inflammation and fibrosis.\nGUT-1, a double-stranded RNA oligonucleotide targeting CHST15, was evaluated in a Phase IIa study of 28 UC patients unresponsive to conventional treatments.\nSingle-dose administration (25 nM, 250 nM) was compared with placebo; the primary endpoint was endoscopic improvement at weeks 2 and 4.\nIn the 250 nM group, endoscopic improvement was 71.4%, clinical remission 57.1%, and histological improvement 42.9%, significantly higher than the 25 nM group (0%) and placebo (28.6%, 14.3%, 0%).\nGUT-1 reduces CHST15 expression, inhibiting mucosal inflammation and fibrosis, and may serve as an inducible therapy to regulate tissue remodeling in UC [55].\n...\nThe VARSITY study demonstrated that VDZ showed higher clinical remission, endoscopic improvement, and histological remission than adalimumab in UC.", "date": "2025-12-05", "last_updated": "2026-09-11", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.76, "evidence": 0.6467}, "jev_score": 0.4915, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "[PDF] Current and emerging therapeutic targets for IBD - SciSpace", "url": "https://scispace.com/pdf/current-and-emerging-therapeutic-targets-for-ibd-191591vd96.pdf", "domain": "scispace.com", "snippet": "Specifically, a study has examined carbohydrate sulfotransferase 15 (CHST15) in gut inflammation78.\n...\nIn experimental acute dextran sulfate sodium colitis, small-interfering RNA (siRNA)-based silencing of\nCHST15 reduced colitis activity and intestinal accumu­ lation of F4/80+ macrophages and ER‑TR7+ fibroblasts.\n...\nFurthermore, a phase I study in 18 patients with Crohn’s disease published in 2016 determined the safety of STNM01, a synthetic double-stranded RNA oligonucleotide directed against CHST15, in Crohn’s disease79.\nThe majority of individuals who received \nSTNM01 showed a reduction of endoscopic inflam­ mation as compared with placebo therapy.\nMoreover, histo­logical analyses revealed that STNM01 reduces\n­tissue fibrosis in Crohn’s disease.\n...\n79. Suzuki, K. et al.\nPhase 1 clinical study of siRNA\ntargeting carbohydrate sulphotransferase 15 in \nCrohn’s disease patients with active mucosal lesions.\nJ. Crohns Colitis http://dx.doi.org/10.1093/ecco-jcc/\njjw143 (2016).", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-06-26", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.66, "evidence": 0.61}, "jev_score": 0.4026, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Y-ECCO Literature Review: Lushen Pillay - ECCO - European Crohn’s and Colitis Organisation", "url": "https://ecco-ibd.eu/publications/news/issues/2023/volume-18-issue-4/y-ecco-literature-review-lushen-pillay", "domain": "ecco-ibd.eu", "snippet": "One such target is carbohydrate sulphotransferase 15 (CHST15).\nThis is a type II transmembrane Golgi protein that biosynthesises highly sulphated disaccharide units (E-units) of chondroitin sulphate, which binds to various functional proteins and pathogenic microorganisms.\n...\nBased on this promising pre-clinical data, blockade of CHST15 has emerged as a potentially promising therapeutic target, and such blockade can be achieved by a silencing RNA oligonucleotide molecule called GUT-1 (previously called STNM01).\nA prior phase I clinical trial demonstrated the safety of GUT-1 in patients with Crohn’s Disease [6].\nAccordingly, Atreya and colleagues now sought to evaluate the safety, as well as the efficacy and mode of action, of GUT-1 in patients with UC as part of a phase IIa placebo-controlled, clinical trial.\n...\nAdult patients aged 18–65 years with moderate-to-severe left-sided UC were recruited from five centres around Germany.\nTwenty-eight patients were screened and of these 24 were randomised in a 1:1:1 fashion to receive a single dose of 250 nM GUT-1, 25 nM GUT-1 or placebo.\n...\nEndoscopic improvement was achieved in 71.4% of patients in the 250 nM GUT-1 arm, 0% of patients in the 25 nM GUT-1 arm and 28.6% of patients in the placebo arm.\n...\nThe mean percentage positive areas of CHST15 in the 250 nM GUT-1 arm were 19.7% (95% CI 17.0%–22.5%) at baseline, 11.5% (95% CI 6.5%–16.5%) at week 2 and 8.4% (95% CI 3.1%–13.7%) at week 4, suggesting good target engagement with a >50% drop in expression from baseline to week 4.\nThe values in the 25 nM GUT-1 arm were 19.6% (95% CI 15.2%–24.1%) at baseline, 14.7% (95% CI 10.6%–18.7%) at week 2 and 14.0% (95% CI 7.7%–20.2%) at week 4, suggesting an element of target engagement with the lower dose of GUT-1 although this was seemingly insufficient to enact an effect on the outcome measures described above.\n...\nAt two hours after injection, the plasma concentration of GUT-1 was below the lower limit of quantification (LLOQ 0.5 ng/ml) in all treatment groups, and both lower and higher doses of GUT-1 were generally well tolerated.\n...\nAtreya and colleagues should be congratulated for conducting this trial, which provides promising evidence of safety, efficacy and target engagement for GUT-1, a selective RNA oligonucleotide molecule in patients with left-sided UC.", "date": "2023-12-18", "last_updated": "2025-08-12", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.61, "evidence": 0.6267}, "jev_score": 0.3823, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Y-ECCO Literature Review: Lushen Pillay", "url": "https://www.ecco-ibd.eu/publications/ecco-news/item/2023-4-y-ecco-literature-review-lushen-pillay.html", "domain": "ecco-ibd.eu", "snippet": "### Submucosal injection of the RNA nucleotide GUT-1 in active ulcerative colitis patients: A randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled phase 2a induction trial.\n...\nOne such target is carbohydrate sulphotransferase 15 (CHST15).\nThis is a type II transmembrane Golgi protein that biosynthesises highly sulphated disaccharide units (E-units) of chondroitin sulphate, which binds to various functional proteins and pathogenic microorganisms.\nTargeting this molecule in mouse models has previously been shown to offer promising signals for ameliorating colitis [5].\nBased on this promising pre-clinical data, blockade of CHST15 has emerged as a potentially promising therapeutic target, and such blockade can be achieved by a silencing RNA oligonucleotide molecule called GUT-1 (previously called STNM01).\nA prior phase I clinical trial demonstrated the safety of GUT-1 in patients with Crohn’s Disease [6].\nAccordingly, Atreya and colleagues now sought to evaluate the safety, as well as the efficacy and mode of action, of GUT-1 in patients with UC as part of a phase IIa placebo-controlled, clinical trial.\n...\nAdult patients aged 18–65 years with moderate-to-severe left-sided UC were recruited from five centres around Germany.\nTwenty-eight patients were screened and of these 24 were randomised in a 1:1:1 fashion to receive a single dose of 250 nM GUT-1, 25 nM GUT-1 or placebo.\n...\nEndoscopic improvement was achieved in 71.4% of patients in the 250 nM GUT-1 arm, 0% of patients in the 25 nM GUT-1 arm and 28.6% of patients in the placebo arm.\n...\nThe mean percentage positive areas of CHST15 in the 250 nM GUT-1 arm were 19.7% (95% CI 17.0%–22.5%) at baseline, 11.5% (95% CI 6.5%–16.5%) at week 2 and 8.4% (95% CI 3.1%–13.7%) at week 4, suggesting good target engagement with a >50% drop in expression from baseline to week 4.\nThe values in the 25 nM GUT-1 arm were 19.6% (95% CI 15.2%–24.1%) at baseline, 14.7% (95% CI 10.6%–18.7%) at week 2 and 14.0% (95% CI 7.7%–20.2%) at week 4, suggesting an element of target engagement with the lower dose of GUT-1 although this was seemingly insufficient to enact an effect on the outcome measures described above.\n...\nAt two hours after injection, the plasma concentration of GUT-1 was below the lower limit of quantification (LLOQ 0.5 ng/ml) in all treatment groups, and both lower and higher doses of GUT-1 were generally well tolerated.\n...\nAtreya and colleagues should be congratulated for conducting this trial, which provides promising evidence of safety, efficacy and target engagement for GUT-1, a selective RNA oligonucleotide molecule in patients with left-sided UC.\nImportant steps going forward will be to determine whether this treatment can also be used in pancolitis and proctitis and to further elucidate the biological basis as part of mechanistic work in future clinical trial phases.", "date": "2024-09-27", "last_updated": "2024-11-29", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.59, "evidence": 0.5933}, "jev_score": 0.35, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Add-on multiple submucosal injections of the RNA oligonucleotide GUT-1 to anti-TNF antibody treatment in patients with moderate-to-severe ulcerative colitis: an open-label, proof-of concept study", "url": "https://d-nb.info/1335375937/34", "domain": "d-nb.info", "snippet": "Background Carbohydrate sulfotransferase 15 (CHST15) is an enzyme biosynthesizing matrix glycosaminoglycan that modulates tissue remodeling.\nWe evaluated the efficacy of add-on submucosal injections of GUT-1, the RNA oligonucleotide inhibitor of CHST15, to ongoing anti-tumor necrosis factor (TNF) antibody treatment in patients with moderate-to-severe ulcerative colitis (UC).\nMethods This was an open-label study of 250 nM of GUT-1 by endoscopic submucosal injections at weeks 0, 2, 4 in five UC patients who lost response during maintenance treatment to anti-TNF antibodies.\n...\nResults At week 6, rates of endoscopic improvement and clinical remission were 80% (n = 4/5) and 60% (n = 3/5),\nrespectively.\n...\nGUT-1\nwas well tolerated.\nThree patients did not show clinical recurrence for 52 weeks.\nAll three corticosteroid-dependent patients showed no corticosteroid exposure for at least 24 weeks after achieving clinical remission.\nMultiple dosing was also well tolerated.\nConclusions Add-on multiple injections of GUT-1 to ongoing anti-TNF antibody was able to induce rapid and dura- ble clinical responses in UC patients who lost response to anti-TNF therapy.\n...\nGUT-1 is a synthesized double stranded RNA oli- gonucleotide that selectively inhibits the expression of the CHST15 gene through siRNA mechanisms [9, 14,\n15].\nCHST15 siRNA suppressed CHST15 mRNA in a \ndose dependent manner and demonstrated repression of fibrosis-related genes in human fibroblast cell lines\n[11, 13].\n...\nIn a phase IIa induction trial, one-time submu-\ncosal injection of 250 nM of GUT-1 demonstrated 71.4% of endoscopic improvement (vs. 28.6% for placebo) and\n57.1% of clinical remission (vs. 14.3% for placebo) at week\n4 in patients with ulcerative colitis (UC) refractory to \nconventional treatment [16].\nIn histological evaluation, reduction of mucosal target CHST15 protein expression and reversal of tissue remodeling was shown [16].\n...\nAdd-on multiple injections of 250 nM of\nGUT-1 to ongoing anti-TNF antibody treatment reduced \nSFS at week 2, reduced RBS and Endoscopy Subscore till week 4 and induced 80% of endoscopic improvement,\n100% of clinical response and 60% of clinical remission \nat week 6.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-03-09", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.64, "evidence": 0.4633}, "jev_score": 0.2965, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Submucosal Injection of the RNA Oligonucleotide GUT-1 in Active ...", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/ecco-jcc/article/18/3/406/7287358?guestAccessKey=", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "snippet": "Carbohydrate sulfotransferase 15 [CHST15] biosynthesizes sulphated matrix glycosaminoglycans and is implicated in intestinal inflammation and fibrosis.\nHere, we evaluate the efficacy and safety of the double-stranded RNA oligonucleotide GUT-1, a specific blocker of CHST15, as induction therapy in patients with ulcerative colitis [UC].\n...\nIn this randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled, phase 2a study, we enrolled endoscopically active UC patients, refractory to conventional therapy, in five hospital centres across Germany.\nPatients were randomized 1:1:1 using a block randomized technique to receive a single dosing of 25 nM GUT-1, 250 nM GUT-1, or placebo by endoscopic submucosal injections.\n...\nGUT-1 250 nM reduced CHST15 expression significantly and suppressed mucosal inflammation and fibrosis.\nGUT-1 application was well tolerated.\n...\nSingle dosing by submucosal injection of GUT-1 repressed CHST15 mucosal expression and may represent a novel induction therapy by modulating tissue remodelling in UC.\n...\nGUT-1 is a synthetic double-stranded RNA oligonucleotide that selectively inhibits the expression of CHST15.^15–17^ Previous *in vitro* studies demonstrated that GUT-1 suppresses CHST15 mRNA in a dose-dependent manner as well as the mRNA expression of vimentin, α-SMA and Wnt3 in a TGF-β-stimulated human fibroblast cell line.\n...\nIn this Phase 2a induction trial, we found that CHST15 was highly expressed in the inflamed colon at baseline in UC patients who did not respond sufficiently to prior conventional treatment including biologics and had active endoscopic lesions with respect to an MES of ≥2.\nSingle dosing of 250 nM GUT-1, an oligonucleotide that specifically suppresses CHST15, revealed 71.4% achieved endoscopic improvement at the end of this induction trial [at week 2 or week 4].\n...\nIn conclusion, the present study shows that local application of GUT-1 could be a novel option for induction therapy in active UC.\nThe effects may be due to a reduced sulphated matrix and CS-E generation, by targeting CHST15, which simultaneously acts to repress neutrophil-based mucosal inflammation and accelerate degradation of established fibrosis, leading to tissue remodelling in UC.", "date": "2024-03-01", "last_updated": "2026-03-17", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.65, "evidence": 0.4267}, "jev_score": 0.2774, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Submucosal Injection of the RNA Oligonucleotide GUT-1 in Active Ulcerative Colitis Patients: A Randomized, Double-Blind, Placebo-Controlled Phase 2a Induction Trial", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/ecco-jcc/article-abstract/18/3/406/7287358?redirectedFrom=fulltext", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "snippet": "Carbohydrate sulfotransferase 15 [CHST15] biosynthesizes sulphated matrix glycosaminoglycans and is implicated in intestinal inflammation and fibrosis.\nHere, we evaluate the efficacy and safety of the double-stranded RNA oligonucleotide GUT-1, a specific blocker of CHST15, as induction therapy in patients with ulcerative colitis [UC].\nIn this randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled, phase 2a study, we enrolled endoscopically active UC patients, refractory to conventional therapy, in five hospital centres across Germany.\nPatients were randomized 1:1:1 using a block randomized technique to receive a single dosing of 25 nM GUT-1, 250 nM GUT-1, or placebo by endoscopic submucosal injections.\n...\nEndoscopic improvement at weeks 2 or 4 was achieved by 71.4% in the GUT-1 250 nM, 0% in the GUT-1 25 nM, and 28.6% in the placebo group.\nClinical remission was shown by 57.1% in the GUT-1 250 nM, 0% in the GUT-1 25 nM, and 14.3% in the placebo groups.\nHistological improvement was shown by 42.9% in the GUT-1 250 nM, 0% in the GUT-1 25 nM, and 0% in the placebo groups.\nGUT-1 250 nM reduced CHST15 expression significantly and suppressed mucosal inflammation and fibrosis.\nGUT-1 application was well tolerated.\nSingle dosing by submucosal injection of GUT-1 repressed CHST15 mucosal expression and may represent a novel induction therapy by modulating tissue remodelling in UC.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-01-20", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.56, "evidence": 0.43}, "jev_score": 0.2408, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Unveiling the key genes, environmental toxins, and drug exposures in modulating the severity of ulcerative colitis: a comprehensive analysis", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10394652/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Via WGCNA, a total 77 high Mayo score-associated genes specific in UC were identified.\nSubsequently, the 9 gene signatures of the exacerbation of UC was screened out by random forest algorithm and Limma analysis, including BGN,CHST15,CYYR1,GPR137B,GPR4,ITGA5,LILRB1,SLFN11 and ST3GAL2.", "date": "2023-07-19", "last_updated": "2025-07-21", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.1, "evidence": 0.2367}, "jev_score": 0.0237, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Identification of Ferro‐Aging‐Related Biomarkers for Ulcerative ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13525582/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Collectively, these findings identify CHST15 and MSN as ferro‐aging‐related biomarkers linking mucosal inflammation, immune‐stromal remodeling, and disease‐associated tissue organization in UC.", "date": "2026-08-29", "last_updated": "2026-09-19", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.08, "evidence": 0.19}, "jev_score": 0.0152, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}], "total_ms": 1005.0, "ts": "2026-10-06T12:18:13+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q042-batch", "row_id": "q042", "mode": "batch", "objective": "Moderately to Severely Active Ulcerative Colitis, Duration \u00143 Months, Modified Mayo Score 5 to 9, With Inadequate Response, Loss of Response, or Intolerance to Prior Standard-of-Care Medications competing agents in the same mechanistic class as TYK2 Small Molecule", "query": ["D-2570 TYK2 ulcerative colitis competitor Phase 2 status"], "anchors": ["D-2570"], "search_latency_ms": 483.3, "target": "drugs competing with D-2570: other agents in the same mechanistic class or for the same indication, as described in `objective`", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 417.4, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 9890, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000415, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 3, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.7695, "results": [{"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Emerging Therapies for Ulcerative Colitis: Updates from Recent ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10441644/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "OST-122 is a gut selective oral JAK inhibitor with selectivity for JAK3, TYK2 and AMPK-related protein kinase 5 (ARK5).\nThe phase Ib/IIa trial which enrolled 32 patients with moderate-to-severe UC has been recently completed and results are yet to be published (NCT04353791).84\n...\nDeucravacitinib is an oral TYK2 inhibitor in development for the treatment of moderate-to-severe UC.\nIn a phase II, double-blind trial (LATTICE-UC), 131 patients were randomized to deucravacitinib 6 mg twice daily vs placebo.89 The trial failed to meet its primary endpoint of clinical remission at 12 weeks defined by the modified Mayo score (stool frequency sub-score ≤1 with a decrease from baseline of at least 1 point, a rectal bleeding sub-score of 0, and an endoscopy sub-score of ≤1).", "date": "2023-08-17", "last_updated": "2026-08-12", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.95, "evidence": 0.81}, "jev_score": 0.7695, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Advancing therapeutic frontiers: a pipeline of novel drugs for ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12952356/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "|TYK2 Inhibitor|Zasocitinib (TAK-279)|AI-designed highly selective allosteric TYK2 inhibitor|NCT06254950, NCT06764615|Phase 2|Recruiting; estimated completion Aug 2027|\n...\n|TYK2 Inhibitor|D-2570|Novel oral TYK2 inhibitor|NCT07035041|Phase 2|Recruiting; began May 2025, estimated completion Dec 2026|\n...\nIt is being tested in a Phase 2, multicenter, placebo-controlled, double-blind RCT for moderately to severely active UC in 207 participants aged 18–75 who have inadequate response to conventional, biologic, or advanced therapies, with the primary outcome measuring clinical remission at week 12 using mMS, currently recruiting with estimated completion in August 2027 (NCT06254950).\n...\n#### 2.1.9. D-2570\nD-2570 is a novel oral TYK2 inhibitor being developed for autoimmune diseases including psoriasis and UC evaluated in a Phase 2 multicenter double-blind, placebo-controlled RCT (NCT07035041) with 120 participants randomized 1:1:1 to two different D-2570 dosing arms or placebo for 12 weeks.", "date": "2026-01-30", "last_updated": "2026-09-25", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.93, "evidence": 0.8}, "jev_score": 0.744, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Interleukin-23p19 Inhibitors in Inflammatory Bowel Disease", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12942054/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "In the ANTHEM-UC trial, a phase 2b study involving 252 adults with moderate-to-severe UC, preliminary results reported dose-dependent improvements in clinical response rates.\n...\nIn addition, small-molecule inhibitors targeting JAK1 and TYK2, which act downstream of the IL-23 receptor, are currently under clinical development.\nDeucr\navacitinib is a selective, allosteric inhibitor of TYK2, an intracellular signaling mediator of IL-23.\nHowever, in three phase 2 clinical trials in patients with moderately to severely active CD and UC (LATTICE-CD, LATTICE-UC, and IM011-127), deucravacitinib demonstrated modest signals of efficacy, but failed to achieve the primary or secondary efficacy endpoints after 12 weeks of treatment across multiple dosing regimens [48].\n...\nOf interest, a next-generation allosteric TYK2 inhibitor, TAK-279 (zasocitinib), is currently in phase 2 evaluation for both UC and CD [51,52].\nTAK-279 is designed for sustained 24 h pathway suppression and these trials will help understand if deeper TYK2 target engagement can translate to mucosal efficacy in IBD.", "date": "2026-02-14", "last_updated": "2026-10-02", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.92, "evidence": 0.7333}, "jev_score": 0.6746, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Advancing therapeutic frontiers: a pipeline of novel drugs ...", "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/gastroenterology/articles/10.3389/fgstr.2026.1747118/full", "domain": "frontiersin.org", "snippet": "|TYK2 Inhibitor|D-2570|Novel oral TYK2 inhibitor|NCT07035041|Phase 2|Recruiting; began May 2025, estimated completion Dec 2026|\n...\nIt is being tested in a Phase 2, multicenter, placebo-controlled, double-blind RCT for moderately to severely active UC in 207 participants aged 18–75 who have inadequate response to conventional, biologic, or advanced therapies, with the primary outcome measuring clinical remission at week 12 using mMS, currently recruiting with estimated completion in August 2027 (NCT06254950).\n...\n#### 2.1.9 D-2570\nD-2570 is a novel oral TYK2 inhibitor being developed for autoimmune diseases including psoriasis and UC evaluated in a Phase 2 multicenter double-blind, placebo-controlled RCT (NCT07035041) with 120 participants randomized 1:1:1 to two different D-2570 dosing arms or placebo for 12 weeks.\n...\nThe study began May 2025 with estimated primary completion July 2026 and final completion December 2026, currently in the recruiting Phase with no results yet available as the trial is ongoing.", "date": "2026-01-30", "last_updated": "2026-09-29", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.63, "evidence": 0.4967}, "jev_score": 0.3129, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Yifang Bio-U (688382) 2026 H1 Report Commentary: Multiple Core Pipeline Programs Show Strong Progress; Optimistic About the Realization of Innovation", "url": "https://news.futunn.com/en/post/78219536/yifang-biopharma-u-688382-2026-interim-report-review-core-pipeline", "domain": "news.futunn.com", "snippet": "D-2570 is demonstrating broad therapeutic potential, with the value of its TYK2 inhibitor pipeline continuing to stand out.\nIn the first half of 2026, D-2570 has made significant progress: (1) enrollment and dosing have been initiated for the first patient in a head-to-head Phase III trial in psoriasis; (2) the first patient has been enrolled in a Phase II trial for psoriatic arthritis; (3) the first patient has been enrolled in a Phase II trial for systemic lupus erythematosus;\n(4) all participants have been enrolled in a pivotal Phase III trial for moderate-to-severe plaque psoriasis; (5) all participants have been enrolled in a Phase II trial for moderate-to-severe active ulcerative colitis; and (6) the U.S. FDA has formally approved D-2570 for a Phase II monotherapy study in psoriasis.\nIn the Phase II psoriasis trial, D-2570 demonstrated superior efficacy compared to both placebo and currently marketed TYK2 inhibitors, highlighting its \"best-in-class\" potential.\nIts indications are expanding into multiple autoimmune diseases, including psoriatic arthritis, systemic lupus erythematosus, and ulcerative colitis, with substantial market opportunities.", "date": "2026-08-25", "last_updated": "2026-08-26", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.59, "evidence": 0.39}, "jev_score": 0.2301, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "A Study on the Safety of TAK-279 and Whether it Can Reduce Inflammation in the Bowel of Participants With Moderately to Severely Active Ulcerative Colitis", "url": "https://med.agiltrace.com/clinical-trials/NCT06254950", "domain": "med.agiltrace.com", "snippet": "NCT03943446 ·Status: TERMINATED ·Phase: PHASE2\n- A Phase 2 Study of D-2570 in Subjects With Moderately to Severely Active Ulcerative Colitis\nNCT07035041 ·Status: ACTIVE_NOT_RECRUITING ·Phase: PHASE2", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-05", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.62, "evidence": 0.3267}, "jev_score": 0.2026, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "SUMMARY", "url": "https://www1.hkexnews.hk/app/sehk/2026/108029/a128849/sehk26010100538.pdf", "domain": "www1.hkexnews.hk", "snippet": "Within our clinical-stage pipeline, our Core Products tyrosine kinase 2 (“TYK2”) inhibitor D-2570 and oral estrogen receptor degrader (“SERD”) taragarestrant (D-0502) are both advancing through Phase III registration trials, while our urate transporter 1 (“URAT1”) inhibitor darbinurad (D-0120) has completed a Phase IIb clinical trial.\n...\nD-2570\nTYK2\nAutoimmune \ndiseases\nSubmit NDA in H2 2027\nGlobal\nInitiate Phase III trial in H1 2026\nComplete Phase II trial in H2 2026\nComplete Phase I trial in H1 2026\nInitiate Phase II trial in H1 2026\nInitiate Phase II trial in H1 2026\nSubmit IND in H1 2026\n...\nOur Core Product D-2570 is a self-developed, orally administered, highly selective TYK2 allosteric inhibitor for the treatment of psoriasis and other autoimmune diseases.\nOur Phase II clinical trial in moderate-to-severe psoriasis delivered outstanding results, with efficacy significantly superior to both placebo and approved products of the same class (based on published clinical data), and comparable to that of biologics, while maintaining strong safety and tolerability.\nWe have agreed with the NMPA on the Phase III clinical trial protocol and have initiated the registrational Phase III clinical trial in September 2025.\nIn parallel, we have also commenced a Phase II clinical study for ulcerative colitis (“UC”) in China.\n...\nD-2570 is a TYK2 allosteric inhibitor under development primarily for the treatment of plaque psoriasis and UC.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-06-06", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.58, "evidence": 0.3267}, "jev_score": 0.1895, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "The Efficacy of Hyperbaric Oxygen-assisted Treatment for ASUC and Refractory IBD", "url": "https://med.agiltrace.com/clinical-trials/NCT07257588", "domain": "med.agiltrace.com", "snippet": "- A Phase 2 Study of D-2570 in Subjects With Moderately to Severely Active Ulcerative Colitis\nNCT07035041 ·Status: ACTIVE_NOT_RECRUITING ·Phase: PHASE2\n- A Study to Evaluate IBI112 in the Treatment of Moderate to Severe Active Ulcerative Colitis\n...\n- Clinical Response and Safety Following FMT for UC", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-05", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.53, "evidence": 0.3133}, "jev_score": 0.166, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Study Evaluating ISM5411 Administered Orally to Subjects With Active Ulcerative Colitis (BETHESDA)", "url": "https://med.agiltrace.com/clinical-trials/NCT07265570", "domain": "med.agiltrace.com", "snippet": "NCT02368717 ·Status: COMPLETED ·Phase: PHASE3\n...\n- A Phase 2 Study of D-2570 in Subjects With Moderately to Severely Active Ulcerative Colitis\nNCT07035041 ·Status: ACTIVE_NOT_RECRUITING ·Phase: PHASE2", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-05", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.54, "evidence": 0.3033}, "jev_score": 0.1638, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "BUSINESS", "url": "https://www1.hkexnews.hk/app/sehk/2026/108029/a128849/sehk26010100550.pdf", "domain": "www1.hkexnews.hk", "snippet": "inhibitor D-2570 and oral estrogen receptor degrader (“SERD”) taragarestrant (D-0502) are both advancing through Phase III registration trials, while our urate transporter 1 (“URAT1”) inhibitor darbinurad (D-0120) has completed a Phase IIb clinical trial.\n...\nOur Core Product D-2570 is a self-developed, orally administered, highly selective TYK2 allosteric inhibitor for the treatment of psoriasis and other autoimmune diseases.\n...\nWe have agreed with the NMPA on the Phase III clinical trial protocol and have initiated the registrational Phase III clinical trial in September 2025.\nIn parallel, we have also commenced a Phase II clinical study for ulcerative colitis (“UC”) in China.\n...\nWe have initiated the registrational Phase III clinical trial of D-2570 for moderate-to- severe plaque psoriasis in China and are actively preparing for expansion into additional\n...\nMeanwhile, we will continue with the China Phase II clinical trial targeting UC and the U.S.\n...\nD-2570 (TYK2 inhibitor): The U.S. Phase I clinical trial has been initiated.\nThe clinical data are expected in the first half of 2026.\n...\nD-2570 is an oral selective allosteric inhibitor of TYK2 under clinical development for the treatment of autoimmune diseases, including psoriasis and UC.\n...\nCurrently, a registrational Phase III clinical trial of D-2570 targeting moderate-to-severe plaque psoriasis is ongoing.\nWe expect to complete the trial in the second half of 2027 and thereafter submit to the NMPA an NDA for D-2570.\nMeanwhile, we are exploring the potential of D-2570 as an induction therapy for moderately to severely active UC.\nA Phase II clinical trial is ongoing for that purpose in China, and we expect to complete the trial in the second half of 2026.\nWe expect to conduct other\nPhase II clinical trials targeting PsA and SLE in China in the first half of 2026.\nWe have also initiated Phase I clinical trial in U.S., with completion expected in the first half of 2026.\n...\nWe initiated the Phase II clinical trial in January 2024 and completed the trial in April 2025.\n...\nWe initiated the trial in September 2025 and expect to complete the trial in the second half of 2027.\nA Phase II clinical trial to evaluate the safety and efficacy of D-2570 as induction therapy in subjects with moderately to severely active UC in China sponsored by us\nOverview.\nThis is a multicenter, randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled Phase II clinical trial.\nThe target population is the patients with moderately to severely active UC.\nThe primary objective is to conduct a preliminary evaluation of the efficacy of different doses of\nD-2570 in the treatment of moderately to severely active UC.\n...\nTrial design.\nA total of 120 subjects are planned to be included.\nThe trial is divided into two phases: the induction treatment phase and the extended treatment phase.\n...\nTrial status.\nWe initiated the Phase II clinical trial in May 2025 and expect to complete the trial in the second half of 2026.\n...\nIn March 2025, the CDE granted the IND approval to conduct the Phase II clinical trial of D-2570 as induction therapy in subjects with moderately to severely active UC.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-06-06", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.55, "evidence": 0.2767}, "jev_score": 0.1522, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}], "total_ms": 900.7, "ts": "2026-10-06T12:18:13+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q043-batch", "row_id": "q043", "mode": "batch", "objective": "Inflammatory Bowel Disease competing agents in the same mechanistic class as VNN1, including Small Molecule therapies", "query": ["MY009 VNN1 inflammatory bowel disease competing agents"], "anchors": ["MY009"], "search_latency_ms": 514.4, "target": "drugs competing with MY009: other agents in the same mechanistic class or for the same indication, as described in `objective`", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 440.0, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 8766, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000368, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 2, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.4133, "results": [{"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "MY-009212A - Drug Targets, Indications, Patents", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/drug/c49892e432464b30b9412d5cb70cce59", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "Small molecule drug\n...\nMY-009,\nMY009,\nMY009212A\n...\nTarget\npantetheinase\nAction\ninhibitors\nMechanism\npantetheinase inhibitors(vanin 1 inhibitors)\nTherapeutic Areas\nImmune System Diseases\nDigestive System Disorders\nActive Indication\nUlcerative colitis, active moderate\nUlcerative colitis, active severe\n...\nPhase 2\n...\nInflammatory Bowel Diseases\nPhase 1\nChina\nShanghai Meiyue Biotech Development Co.\nLtd.\n29 May 2022", "date": "2025-08-16", "last_updated": "2025-11-19", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.77, "evidence": 0.5367}, "jev_score": 0.4133, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "The Present and Future of Inflammatory Bowel Disease ... - PMC", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4969780/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Anti-tumor necrosis factor (TNF) drugs currently include infliximab (Remicade, Janssen), adalimumab (Humira, AbbVie), and certolizumab pegol (Cimzia, UCB).\n...\nApproximately 2 years ago, the anti— alpha-4 beta-7 integrin antibody vedolizumab (Entyvio, Takeda) was approved for induction and maintenance of Crohn’s disease.\nThis drug is fairly slow-acting, so it is somewhat better for maintenance than for induction.\nIt is selective in its targeting, even though it is administered intravenously, so it does not have black box warnings for serious infection and malignancy as anti-TNF agents do.\nThere is emerging use of vedolizumab as first-line biologic therapy because it tends to show better results in patients who have not previously had anti-TNF drugs and because it is likely safer than anti-TNF drugs.\n...\n**WS** Etrolizumab (Genentech) is an interesting drug in the same general class as vedolizumab that is currently being tested in phase 3 trials in patients with ulcerative colitis as well as in patients with Crohn’s disease.\nLike vedolizumab, etrolizumab blocks alpha-4 beta-7, but it also blocks alpha-E beta-7, which affects lymphocyte trafficking to the skin and to the gut.\n...\n**WS** Anti-P40 and anti-P19 antibodies block signaling through the Th1 and Th17 pathways.\nUstekinumab (Stelara, Janssen) is an anti-P40 antibody that blocks the P40 subunit of interleukin (IL)-12 and -23.\nThis agent is currently approved by the FDA for psoriasis and psoriatic arthritis and has finished phase 3 testing in Crohn’s disease.\nClinical trial data have recently been presented in abstract form showing that ustekinumab is effective for induction of remission in patients with inflammatory bowel disease who are failing conventional therapy (not anti-TNF drugs) and separately for induction of remission in patients who have failed anti-TNF drugs.\nA maintenance trial mixed these 2 populations and showed that the drug is effective for maintaining remission for over a year.\n...\n**WS** Janus kinase (JAK) inhibitors block a variety of proinflammatory cytokines by blocking the JAK/Signal Transducer and Activator of Transcription signaling pathway.\nTofacitinib (Xeljanz, Pfizer), which is currently approved by the FDA for rheumatoid arthritis, is a small molecule that blocks predominantly JAK1 and JAK3 receptors but also has some JAK2 effects at higher doses.\nA phase 2 study showed that the drug was highly effective in ulcerative colitis, and two phase 3 studies recently showed that the drug was effective for inducing response, remission, and mucosal healing, both in patients with moderate to severe ulcerative colitis who are failing anti-TNF drugs as well as in patients who are naive to anti-TNF therapy.\nA phase 3 maintenance trial will be completed in the third quarter of this year, which means that tofacitinib may be sent for FDA review sometime next year.\n...\n**WS** A metalloproteinase-9 antibody (GS-5745, Gilead) showed some evidence of efficacy in a phase 1A study in ulcerative colitis and will be undergoing phase 2/3 trials in ulcerative colitis and Crohn’s disease.\n...\n**WS** Most of these agents are being studied in patients who have failed mesalamine, corticosteroids, and perhaps immunosuppressants.\nSome, but not all, of the protocols require failure of a biologic agent as well.\nThese agents are not being examined for first-line treatment in Crohn’s disease or ulcerative colitis.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-23", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.67, "evidence": 0.5033}, "jev_score": 0.3372, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Inflammatory Bowel Disease: Understanding Therapeutic Effects of ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12649937/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Five major classes of advanced therapies, comprising biologics and oral small molecules, are currently approved, each targeting different points of the inflammatory cascade: tumor necrosis factor (TNF)-α antagonists, adhesion and trafficking inhibitors (including anti-integrin agents and sphingosine1-phosphate receptor modulators), interleukin Inhibitors, and Janus kinase inhibitors.\n...\nGuselkumab, the newest IL-23 inhibitor available for IBD treatment, was first approved for UC in 2024 in the United States, based on results from the QUASAR phase 3 induction and maintenance [15] trials.\n...\nThe two JAK 1 selective inhibitors approved for IBD are upadacitinib and filgotinib.", "date": "2025-10-30", "last_updated": "2026-08-20", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.59, "evidence": 0.4267}, "jev_score": 0.2518, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "What drugs are in development for Inflammatory Bowel Diseases?", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/article/what-drugs-are-in-development-for-inflammatory-bowel-diseases", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "Even though substantial progress has been made in the past decades with the introduction of biologics such as anti-tumor necrosis factor (TNF) agents (e.g., infliximab, adalimumab), anti-integrin antibodies (e.g., vedolizumab) and anti-IL-12/23 therapies (e.g., ustekinumab), many patients remain non-responders or lose response over time.\nIn addition, traditional therapies like 5-aminosalicylic acid (5-ASA) preparations, corticosteroids, and immunomodulators (azathioprine, methotrexate) are still used, though their long-term effectiveness is limited by side effects and suboptimal efficacy.\n...\nA broad spectrum of drug candidates is now emerging from the bench into clinical development for IBD, representing multiple drug classes and mechanisms of action.\nAnti-Integrin Agents:\nNew anti-integrin antibodies are under investigation to improve upon the gut-selectivity seen with vedolizumab while providing more convenient dosing schedules.\nFor example, SPY001 is a highly potent anti-α4β7 monoclonal antibody featuring half-life extension technology, which is currently advancing through IND-enabling studies and is expected to enter first-in-human trials soon.\nIn parallel, other gut-selective therapies such as etrolizumab and abrilumab, which also target integrin complexes on lymphocytes, are being evaluated in Phase II/III studies.\nOntamalimab, an antibody targeting the mucosal vascular addressin cell adhesion molecule (MAdCAM-1), is another candidate that aims to block the recruitment of activated T cells to inflamed intestinal tissues.\n...\nTL1A is a member of the tumor necrosis factor superfamily that is implicated in driving T cell activation and gut inflammation.\nSPY002 is a promising anti-TL1A monoclonal antibody engineered to bind both monomeric and trimeric forms of TL1A.\n...\nIL-23 Axis Inhibitors:\nWhile ustekinumab, an anti-IL-12/23p40 antibody, is already approved, there is growing interest in selectively inhibiting the p19 subunit of IL-23.\nSeveral agents, including risankizumab, brazikumab, guselkumab, and mirikizumab, are in various stages of development for IBD.\n...\nJanus Kinase (JAK) Inhibitors:\nTofacitinib, a JAK inhibitor, is currently in use for ulcerative colitis; however, next-generation JAK inhibitors with enhanced selectivity and safety profiles, such as upadacitinib and filgotinib, are also being studied in IBD.\n...\nS1P receptor modulators such as ozanimod have already been approved in some regulatory territories, and newer molecules in this class may be under investigation for additional IBD indications.\nThese agents work by sequestering lymphocytes in lymphoid tissues, thereby preventing their migration to sites of intestinal inflammation.\n...\nDP Technology’s DPT0218 is a promising example of a small molecule inhibitor that shows sub-nanomolar potency against the Kv1.3 ion channel, which plays a role in T cell activation.", "date": "2025-03-12", "last_updated": "2026-09-23", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.55, "evidence": 0.4033}, "jev_score": 0.2218, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Methods and pharmaceutical compositions for repairing intestinal ...", "url": "https://patents.google.com/patent/US20250009694A1/en", "domain": "patents.google.com", "snippet": "Based on the VIVA transgenic model of Vnn1 overexpression on intestinal cells, the inventors show that the epithelial pantetheinase Vnn1 has a dual effect on colon: (1) its enzymatic products, cysteamine and pantothenic acid (vitamin B5) enhance coenzyme A regeneration and colon fitness through metabolic rewiring: (2) they favor microbiota-dependent accumulation of butyrate, previously shown to regulate mucosal energetics and to be reduced in IBD patients.\n...\nOur pioneering studies in mouse showed that the Vnn1 pantetheinase isoform regulates metabolic, inflammatory and cytoprotective programs in diverse tissues [28-32].\n...\nLikewise, the colonic epithelium of IBD patients displays high VNN1 levels [34].\nIn addition, SNPs in the regulatory regions of the VNN1 gene are associated with disease risk and some VNN1 high patients harbouring specific VNN1 SNPs develop a severe disease [34].\n...\nIn the management of patients with inflammatory bowel diseases (IBD), there is a need to identify prognostic markers and druggable biological pathways to improve mucosal repair and efficacy of TNF alpha biologics.\n...\nHerein the inventors show that enhancement of vitamin B5-driven metabolism should improve mucosal healing and maintain colon fitness and might enhance the efficacy of anti-inflammatory anti-TNF alpha therapy.\n...\nIn some embodiments, the substrate of vanin-1 pantetheinase and/or product(s) of vanin-1 pantetheinase enzymatic activity (i.e the agent(s) of Vnn1 metabolism of the invention) are administered in combination with a therapeutic compound used to treat IBD or IBS.\n...\nThus, the invention refers to a method for treating inflammatory bowel disease in a subject in need thereof, comprising the step of i) determining if the subject have inflammatory bowel disease according to the method of the invention and ii) administering a substrate of vanin-1 pantetheinase and/or product(s) of vanin-1 pantetheinase enzymatic activity when the subject is determined having inflammatory bowel disease.\n...\n- Generation of the VIVA Mouse.\n...\n- High VNN1 Expression Correlates with IBD Severity.\n...\nWe previously shown that VNN1 is highly expressed in colon biopsies from IBD patients [34].\n...\nHere we show that VNN1 expression levels increase with disease severity, in both CD and UC (data not shown).\nIndeed, whereas VNN1 was barely detectable during the quiescent phases of the diseases, its expression augmented during the flares and reached the highest levels in patients resistant to anti-TNFα biologics.\n...\nAltogether, colonic VNN1 overexpression is a feature of IBD patients and reflects the degree of tissue inflammation and damage.\n...\nThus, increased levels of Vnn1 on intestinal epithelial cells in mice limit the development of DSS-induced epithelial lesions, immune-mediated damage and disease severity.\n...\nOur current work clearly established that high VNN1 expression is associated with the degree of inflammation, disease severity and response to therapy in IBD patients.\n...\nOf major interest, we could demonstrated that co-administration of the products of Vnn1 activity (ie. Pan and CEA) to normal mice recapitulates most of the protective phenotypes conferred by the constitutive overexpression of Vnn1 in the VIVA model.\n...\nA pharmaceutical composition comprising at least one agent of Vnn1 metabolism selected from the group consisting of i) pantethine, ii) pantothenate and/or a pantothenate analog, iii) cysteamine, iv) pantothenate and cysteamine, and v) a pantothenate analog and cysteamine for use for repairing intestinal mucosal barrier in subject in need thereof.", "date": "2022-10-31", "last_updated": "2026-02-17", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.44, "evidence": 0.4467}, "jev_score": 0.1965, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Medications for Inflammatory Bowel Disease", "url": "https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/gastrointestinal-disorders/inflammatory-bowel-disease-ibd/medications-for-inflammatory-bowel-disease", "domain": "merckmanuals.com", "snippet": "Several classes of medications are used to treat inflammatory bowel disease (IBD).\nDetails of their selection and use are discussed under each disorder (see Crohn disease treatment, ulcerative colitis treatment).\n...\n|**Glucocorticoids**|Regulation of gene expression|Hydrocortisone (IV) Methylprednisolone (IV) Prednisone (oral) Prednisolone (oral) Budesonide (oral) Budesonide MMX (enteric coated slow release) Hydrocortisone (enema or foam)|\n...\nThe antimetabolites azathioprine and 6-mercaptopurine (thiopurines), and methotrexate, are used in combination therapy with biologic agents, and for maintenance therapy in patients with Crohn disease in whom remission was induced with glucocorticoids (1, 2).", "date": "2026-03-06", "last_updated": "2026-09-23", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.48, "evidence": 0.36}, "jev_score": 0.1728, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Landscape of new drugs and targets in inflammatory bowel disease", "url": "https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/ueg2.12305", "domain": "onlinelibrary.wiley.com", "snippet": "Among the most promising molecules are anti-IL-23, JAK-inhibitors, anti-integrins and S1P modulators.\n...\nlthough UST (an inhibitor of p40 subunit) has been shown to be effective in IBD,^8^ the p19 antagonists (selective IL-23 inhibitors) have proven to be more effective than UST in other immune-mediated conditions, leading us to evaluate them in IBD.^15, 16^ Four IL-23 p19 inhibitors are currently being evaluated in IBD: risankizumab, guselkumab, brazikumab and mirikizumab.", "date": "2022-12-01", "last_updated": "2026-09-28", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.42, "evidence": 0.3667}, "jev_score": 0.154, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Inflammatory Bowel Disease Biomarkers - PMC - NIH", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10321231/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Tumor necrosis factor-alpha (TNF-α) inhibitors, also called biologics, are available as injectables to treat IBD.", "date": "2022-05-23", "last_updated": "2026-07-25", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.22, "evidence": 0.3167}, "jev_score": 0.0697, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Biological agents as attractive targets for inflammatory bowel ...", "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0925443924006422", "domain": "sciencedirect.com", "snippet": "You can set your browser to block or alert you about these cookies, but some parts of the site will not then work.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-09-30", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.11, "evidence": 0.05}, "jev_score": 0.0055, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Targeting the Unmet Needs in IBD: Emerging Therapies Beyond ...", "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1471489225000736", "domain": "sciencedirect.com", "snippet": "You can set your browser to block or alert you about these cookies, but some parts of the site will not then work.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-09-30", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.14, "evidence": 0.02}, "jev_score": 0.0028, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}], "total_ms": 954.4, "ts": "2026-10-06T12:18:13+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q041-batch", "row_id": "q041", "mode": "batch", "objective": "competing agents in the same mechanistic class for this indication DB-3Q, Direct Biologics LLC, Medically Refractory Crohn's Disease", "query": ["DB-3Q MSC-derived extracellular vesicle Crohn's competitors", "DB-3Q extracellular vesicle Crohn's disease clinical trials", "DB-3Q MSC-derived EV medically refractory Crohn's"], "anchors": ["DB-3Q"], "search_latency_ms": 785.7, "target": "drugs competing with DB-3Q: other agents in the same mechanistic class or for the same indication, as described in `objective`", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 360.6, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 10035, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000421, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 1, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.3802, "results": [{"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Filgotinib in the Induction and Maintenance of Remission in Adults ...", "url": "https://www.clareohealth.com/clinical-trials/NCT02914561", "domain": "clareohealth.com", "snippet": "RECRUITINGPhase 2\n### DB-3Q bmMSC-EVs in Patients With Perianal Fistulizing Crohn's Disease\nNCT06918808 · Perianal Fistula Due to Crohn's Disease\n...\n### Trial snapshot\nStatusCOMPLETED\nPhasePHASE3\nEnrollment1,372\nStart dateOct 2016\nLast updatedDec 18, 2023\n### Condition\nCrohn's Disease\n### Locations shown\nDigestive Health Specialists of the Southeast\nDothan, Alabama\nArizona Digestive Health - Sun City\nSun City, Arizona\nSouthern California Research Center, Inc.\nCoronado, California", "date": "2016-10-31", "last_updated": "2026-05-25", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.61, "evidence": 0.6233}, "jev_score": 0.3802, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "DB-3Q bmMSC-EVs in Patients With Perianal Fistulizing Crohn's Disease", "url": "https://ichgcp.net/clinical-trials-registry/NCT06918808", "domain": "ichgcp.net", "snippet": "# DB-3Q bmMSC-EVs in Patients With Perianal Fistulizing Crohn's Disease\n...\n## A Phase 2a Study of Allogeneic Bone Marrow Mesenchymal Stem Cell Derived Extracellular Vesicle Isolate Product (DB-3Q) in Patients With Perianal Fistulizing Crohn's Disease\nThis is a double-blind, randomized, placebo-controlled, sequential cohort, ascending dose clinical trial to evaluate the safety and determine the efficacy of ascending doses of DB-3Q for the treatment of Perianal Fistulizing Crohn's Disease.\n...\n#### Status\nRecruiting\n...\n#### Study Type\nInterventional\n#### Enrollment (Estimated)\n36\n#### Phase\n- Phase 2\n...\nNew York\nNew York, New York, United States, 10032\n- Recruiting\n- Columbia University Irving Medical Center/NYPH\nContact:\n- Claudia Musat\n...\n#### Ages Eligible for Study\n...\nInclusion Criteria:\n- Written informed consent from participant\n- Men and women 18-75 years of age with a diagnosis of Crohn's Disease for at least six months duration prior to Day 1\n- Single and/or multi-tract perianal fistula(s) with 2 or fewer openings, that are actively draining\n- Failed at least one medical therapy within the last year including, but not limited to, antibiotics, immunomodulators (6-MP, methotrexate, azathioprine), monoclonal antibodies (adalimumab, certolizumab, golimumab, guselkumab, infliximab, risankizumab, ustekinumab, vedolizumab), or small molecule inhibitors (tofacitinib, upadacitinib)\n- Previous failed surgical intervention, including seton placement at least two weeks prior to screening, or are not candidates for surgical intervention or are not willing to undergo surgical intervention for the management of their fistula\n- Medical therapy for CD stable for at least 2 months prior to Day 1 (Changes in dosing or dosing intervals related to serum drug levels are not permitted)\n...\n**Primary Purpose**: Treatment **Allocation**: Randomized **Interventional Model**: Sequential Assignment **Masking**: Quadruple\n...\n|--|--|\n|Experimental: Single dose DB-3Q 15 mL direct injection|DB-3Q (IMP) is the frozen liquid formulation of acellular secretome proteins and extracellular vesicles isolated from human bmMSC and processed under current Good Manufacturing Process standards.|\n|Placebo Comparator: Single dose Placebo 15 mL direct injection|0.9% NaCl|\n|Experimental: Single dose DB-3Q 30 mL direct injection|DB-3Q (IMP) is the frozen liquid formulation of acellular secretome proteins and extracellular vesicles isolated from human bmMSC and processed under current Good Manufacturing Process standards.|\n|Placebo Comparator: Single dose Placebo 30 mL direct injection|0.9% NaCl|\n|Experimental: Single dose DB-3Q 15 ml IV and DB-3Q 30 mL direct injection|DB-3Q (IMP) is the frozen liquid formulation of acellular secretome proteins and extracellular vesicles isolated from human bmMSC and processed under current Good Manufacturing Process standards.|\n|Placebo Comparator: Single dose Placebo 15 ml IV and Placebo 30 mL direct injection|0.9% NaCl|\n...\n|## Outcome Measure|## Measure Description|## Time Frame|\n|--|--|--|\n|Combined Remission Time Frame: 24 Weeks|Clinically confirmed 100% closure of all treated external openings, without development of new fistulas or abscesses and drainage > 1 cm by the external openings, occurring spontaneously or after gentle finger compression; and on blinded central review of the MRI results, combined imaging endpoint of >50% reduction in MAGNIFI-CD score and >20% increase in perianal fistula fibrosis-to-fluid ratio.|24 Weeks|\n...\n|Combined Remission Time Frame: 12 Weeks|Clinically confirmed 100% closure of all treated external openings, without development of new fistulas or abscesses and drainage > 1 cm by the external openings, occurring spontaneously or after gentle finger compression; and on blinded central review of the MRI results, combined imaging endpoint of >50% reduction in MAGNIFI-CD score and >20% increase in perianal fistula fibrosis-to-fluid ratio.|12 Weeks|\n...\n#### Study Start (Actual)\nMay 19, 2025\n#### Primary Completion (Estimated)\nApril 30, 2026\n#### Study Completion (Estimated)\nOctober 31, 2026", "date": "2025-06-13", "last_updated": "2025-07-24", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.59, "evidence": 0.4833}, "jev_score": 0.2851, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "[PDF] Claudia Musat, MD - Columbia Surgery", "url": "https://columbiasurgery.org/sites/default/files/2025-03/colorectal-ibd-trials-2025.pdf", "domain": "columbiasurgery.org", "snippet": "DB-3Q: Bone marrow mesenchymal stem cell (bmMSC) derived extracellular \nvesicles product in Patients with Perianal Fistulizing Crohn's Disease (CD) \nPI: Marco Zoccali, MD \nIRB: AAAV6645 \nDisease: Perianal Fistulas due to Crohn's Disease (CD)\nTreatment: DB-3Q (a bone-marrow mesenchymal stem cell product) or Placebo \nThe purpose of this study is to evaluate the efficacy and safety of ascending doses of DB-3Q, a bone-marrow mesenchymal stem cell (bmMSC) derived secretome with extracellular vesicles\n(EVs) isolate product, via direct injection and intravenous (IV) administration, as treatment for \nparticipants with perianal fistulizing Crohn’s disease (PFCD).\nThe investigational product is experimental and has not been approved by the US Food and Drug Administration (FDA).\nThe experimental study drug is a biologic product which is purified from the bone marrow of a healthy well-screened individual.\n36 participants with perianal fistulizing Crohn’s disease, randomized 3:1 to IMP or placebo in \nblocks of 4 participants (3 randomized to IMP, and 1 randomized to placebo) such that each cohort will have 12 participants (9 randomized to IMP and 3 randomized to placebo).\nYour participation in this study will be approximately 24 weeks long.\nEligibility: \n1. 18-75 years of age with a diagnosis of Crohn’s Disease for at least six months duration \nprior to Day 1 \n2. Single and/or multi-tract perianal fistula(s) with 2 or fewer openings, that are actively \ndraining \n3. Failed at least one medical therapy within the last year \n4. Previous failed surgical intervention or not interested in surgical intervention \n5. NO ileal anal pouch and/or history of proctectomy and severe proctitis", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-05-25", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.69, "evidence": 0.3733}, "jev_score": 0.2576, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Columbia Enrolls Nation's First Patient in Direct Biologics' Phase 2 ...", "url": "https://columbiasurgery.org/news/columbia-enrolls-nations-first-patient-direct-biologics-phase-2-clinical-trial-fistulizing", "domain": "columbiasurgery.org", "snippet": "Columbia Enrolls Nation's First Patient in Direct Biologics' Phase 2 Clinical Trial for Fistulizing Crohn's Disease | Columbia Surgery\n...\nColumbia University Irving Medical Center has enrolled the first patient in a multicenter Phase 2 clinical trial investigating the effectiveness of Direct Biologics' proprietary extracellular vesicle technology (DB-3Q, extracellular vesicles (EVs) secreted from bone marrow-derived mesenchymal stem cells) for the treatment of perianal fistulizing Crohn's Disease.\n...\n\"This study aims to determine if this approach is a viable option to treat anal fistula in patients with Crohn's disease specifically.\"\n...\nEVs may offer a game-changing avenue for treating autoimmune diseases, with their ability to repair damaged tissues thanks to their anti-inflammatory and immunomodulatory properties.\n\"The advantages of EVs over traditional stem cell therapies are multiple”, notes Dr. Zoccali.\n\"While derived from stem cells, it is a much more stable product, hence easier to store and administer.\nIt's also less immunogenic, which can mitigate some of the problems associated with stem cell treatment due to the body's response to foreign DNA.\"\nThis phase 2 multicenter randomized study will have multiple cohorts with three different doses and administration routes.\nThe first uses an initial dose administered in the operating room via direct injection near the fistula.\nThe second phase will double that dose.\nThe third phase will include local injections and add an IV infusion of the medication.\n...\nWe are very optimistic that this trial will determine the appropriate dosage to help patients with this novel modality.\"", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-25", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.68, "evidence": 0.36}, "jev_score": 0.2448, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Direct Biologics | MTEC", "url": "https://mtec-sc.org/life-sciences/direct-biologics", "domain": "mtec-sc.org", "snippet": "Developer of investigational secretome-based biologics derived from adult bone marrow mesenchymal stem cells (MSC).\nThe company focuses on cGMP manufacturing of extracellular vesicle (EV)-enriched secretome products, clinical trial execution across phases, and multi-omics characterization using AI-enabled analytics to support mechanism-of-action and potency assay development.\nPrograms target conditions with unmet needs including ARDS and inflammatory bowel disease.\n...\n### DB-3Q — Perianal Fistulizing Crohn's Disease\nInvestigational secretome-based product candidate being evaluated in clinical trials for perianal fistula due to Crohn's disease (Phase II noted).\n### DB-3Q — Medically Refractory Crohn's Disease\nInvestigational secretome-based product candidate under clinical evaluation for medically refractory Crohn's disease.\n...\n### DB-3Q\nAn investigational secretome-based product being evaluated in clinical trials for Perianal Fistulizing Crohn’s Disease and Medically Refractory Crohn’s Disease.", "date": "2025-02-13", "last_updated": "2026-09-26", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.58, "evidence": 0.3967}, "jev_score": 0.2301, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Direct Biologics Clinical Research Programs", "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/posts/direct-biologics_our-clinical-development-programs-span-multiple-activity-7490811433820266496-1073", "domain": "linkedin.com", "snippet": "Our clinical development programs span multiple investigational products, indications, and stages of research.\nAs shown in our pipeline, Direct Biologics currently has Phase 2 through Phase 3 clinical research programs underway.\n...\nHere are our current trials:\nExtracellular Vesicle Treatment for Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome (ARDS) (EXTINGUISH ARDS) https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT05354141?intr=exoflo&rank=7 DB-3Q bmMSC-EVs in Patients With Perianal Fistulizing Crohn's Disease https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT06918808?lead=Direct%20Biologics DB-3Q for the Treatment of Medically Refractory Crohn's Disease\nLike", "date": "2026-08-05", "last_updated": "2026-08-18", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.73, "evidence": 0.31}, "jev_score": 0.2263, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Study Details | NCT07625293 | ClinicalTrials.gov - ClinicalTrials.gov", "url": "https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT07625293", "domain": "clinicaltrials.gov", "snippet": "## DB-3Q for the Treatment of Medically Refractory Crohn's Disease\nClinicalTrials.gov ID ** NCT07625293\nSponsor ** Direct Biologics, LLC\n...\nLast Update Posted ** 2026-06-12\n**Not yet recruiting** **\n...\nThis research study is studying DB-3Q as a possible treatment for medically refractory Crohn's disease.\nThe purpose of this study is to research and evaluate safety and effectiveness of the administration of bone marrow mesenchymal stem cell (bmMSC) derived extracellular vesicles product, DB-3Q, the study drug for Crohn's disease.\nOfficial Title\nA Phase 2a Study of DB-3Q for the Treatment of Medically Refractory Crohn's Disease\nConditions **\nCrohn Disease (CD)\nCrohn Disease (CD)\nCrohn Disease (CD)\nCrohn Disease (CD)\nIntervention/Treatment **\n- Biological: DB-3Q\n- Other: Placebo\nOther Study ID Numbers **\n- 3Q-CD-201\nStudy Start (Estimated) **\n2026-06\nPrimary Completion (Estimated) **\n2027-06\nStudy Completion (Estimated) **\n2027-12\nEnrollment (Estimated) **\n36\nStudy Type **\nInterventional\nPhase **\nPhase 2\n...\nStudy Contact **\n**Name: ** Bill Arana\n**Phone Number: ** 15123547124\n**Email: ** barana@directbiologics.com\nThis study has 1 location\nUnited States\nMissouri Locations\n**St Louis, Missouri, United States, 63110**\nWashington University, St. Louis\nContact :\nStudy Coordinator\n314-273-0301 luluhuang@wustl.edu\n...\nInclusion Criteria:\n1. Written informed consent from participant\n2. Males and females 18-75 years of age\n3. Diagnosed with Crohn's disease of at least 6 months duration with medically refractory symptoms that failed to respond or responded but recurred after one advanced immunologic therapy (must have been receiving at least one advanced immunological therapy for 14 weeks duration prior to screening, including, but not limited to, adalimumab, certolizumab, , infliximab, risankizumab, upadacitinib, ustekinumab and vedolizumab), or is intolerant, or has a contraindication to advanced immunological therapy with a next step of subtotal colectomy or escalation in medical management\n4. Active CD as defined by a CDAI score ≥ 220 and/or SES-CD score ≥ 4\n5. Exposure to corticosteroids, 5-aminosalicylic acid (5-ASA) drugs, thiopurines, methotrexate, anti-TNF therapy, anti-integrin and anti-interleukin in the past are permitted but a washout period of 8 weeks for any monoclonal antibody is necessary\n...\nExclusion Criteria:\n1. Lack of informed consent\n2. Pregnant woman, woman of childbearing potential without a documented negative urine or serum pregnancy test, or woman who is breast feeding\n3. Clinically significant medical conditions within the six months before initial administration of IMP: e.g., myocardial infarction, active angina, congestive heart failure or other conditions that would, in the opinion of the investigators, compromise the safety of the participant\n4. Confirmed Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV), Hepatitis B, or Hepatitis C infections\n...\n12. Microscopic, ischemic or infectious colitis\n...\n|Participant Group/Arm **|Intervention/Treatment **|\n|--|--|\n|Participant Group/Arm **|Experimental: DB-3Q 15 mL IV|\n|Participant Group/Arm **|Experimental: DB-3Q 30 mL IV|\n|Participant Group/Arm **|Experimental: DB-3Q 45 mL IV|\n|Participant Group/Arm **|Placebo Comparator: Placebo IV|\n...\n|--|--|--|\n|Change from baseline in SES-CD Score|Simple Endoscopic Score for Crohn's Disease (SES-CD) is a standardized tool used by gastroenterologists to assess and quantify the severity of Crohn's disease during an endoscopy or colonoscopy.\nThe scale goes from 0 to greater than 15, with a lower score being better.|12 Weeks|", "date": "2026-06-12", "last_updated": "2026-06-30", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.62, "evidence": 0.3167}, "jev_score": 0.1964, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Direct Biologics LLC - Drug pipelines, Patents, Clinical trials", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/organization/7ab73070909872e45913d775d2ff7416", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "|Active Indication Respiratory Distress Syndrome, Acute [+1]|\n|Inactive Indication Colitis, Ulcerative [+7]|\n|Drug Highest PhasePhase 3|\n...\n|Active Indication Perianal fistula due to Crohn's disease|\n|Inactive Indication-|\n|Drug Highest PhasePhase 2|\n...\n12\nClinical Trials associated with Direct Biologics LLC\nNCT06918808\n/ Not yet recruitingPhase 2\nA Phase 2a Study of Allogeneic Bone Marrow Mesenchymal Stem Cell Derived Extracellular Vesicle Isolate Product (DB-3Q) in Patients With Perianal Fistulizing Crohn's Disease\nThis is a double-blind, randomized, placebo-controlled, sequential cohort, ascending dose clinical trial to evaluate the safety and determine the efficacy of ascending doses of DB-3Q for the treatment of Perianal Fistulizing Crohn's Disease.\n|Start Date15 Apr 2025|\n|--|\n|Sponsor / Collaborator Direct Biologics LLC|\nNCT05127122\n/ WithdrawnPhase 1/2\nBone Marrow Mesenchymal Stem Cell Derived Extracellular Vesicles Infusion Treatment for Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome: A Phase I/II Clinical Trial\n...\n|Bone Marrow Mesenchymal Stem Cell Derived Extracellular Vesicles (Direct Biologics)|Respiratory Distress Syndrome, Adult More|Phase 3|\n|Allogeneic Bone Marrow Mesenchymal Stem Cell Derived Extracellular Vesicle Isolate Product(Direct Biologics)|Perianal fistula due to Crohn's disease More|Phase 2|", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-29", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.6, "evidence": 0.3067}, "jev_score": 0.184, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Allogeneic Bone Marrow Mesenchymal Stem Cell Derived ...", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/drug/76f5bfcac1da44b8ac9629daf1034a48", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "Allogeneic Bone Marrow Mesenchymal Stem Cell Derived Extracellular Vesicle Isolate Product(Direct Biologics)\n...\nDrug Type\nExosomes, Mesenchymal stem cell therapy\nSynonyms\nDB-3Q\n...\nActive Indication\nPerianal fistula due to Crohn's disease\nInactive Indication\nOriginator Organization\nDirect Biologics LLC\n...\nClinical Trials associated with Allogeneic Bone Marrow Mesenchymal Stem Cell Derived Extracellular Vesicle Isolate Product(Direct Biologics)\nNCT06918808\nRecruiting\nPhase 2\nA Phase 2a Study of Allogeneic Bone Marrow Mesenchymal Stem Cell Derived Extracellular Vesicle Isolate Product (DB-3Q) in Patients With Perianal Fistulizing Crohn's Disease\nThis is a double-blind, randomized, placebo-controlled, sequential cohort, ascending dose clinical trial to evaluate the safety and determine the efficacy of ascending doses of DB-3Q for the treatment of Perianal Fistulizing Crohn's Disease.\nStart Date\n19 May 2025\nSponsor / Collaborator\nDirect Biologics LLC\n100\n...\nIndication\nHighest Phase\nCountry/Location\nOrganization\nDate\nPerianal fistula due to Crohn's disease\nPhase 2\nUnited States\nDirect Biologics LLC\n19 May 2025\n...\nNo Data", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-05-25", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.55, "evidence": 0.3233}, "jev_score": 0.1778, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "NCT06918808: An ongoing trial by Direct Biologics, LLC", "url": "https://fdaaa.trialstracker.net/trial/NCT06918808/", "domain": "fdaaa.trialstracker.net", "snippet": "## NCT06918808: An ongoing trial by Direct Biologics, LLC\nThis trial is ongoing.\nIt must report results 1 year, 10 months from now.\n...\n|Full entry on ClinicalTrials.gov|NCT06918808|\n...\n|Title|**A Phase 2a Study of Allogeneic Bone Marrow Mesenchymal Stem Cell Derived Extracellular Vesicle Isolate Product (DB-3Q) in Patients With Perianal Fistulizing Crohn's Disease**|\n|Results Status|Ongoing|\n|ACT or pACT?|This is what FDAAA officially calls an \"Applicable Clinical Trial\"|\n|Start date|May 19, 2025|\n|Completion date|July 30, 2027|\n|Required reporting date|July 29, 2028, midnight|\n|Actual reporting date|None|", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-11", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.56, "evidence": 0.27}, "jev_score": 0.1512, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}], "total_ms": 1146.3, "ts": "2026-10-06T12:18:13+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q044-batch", "row_id": "q044", "mode": "batch", "objective": "Inflammatory Bowel Disease competing agents in the same mechanistic class as GPR52 Prostaglandin E2 Receptor EP4, including Small Molecule therapies", "query": ["NXE0033744 Phase 2 inflammatory bowel disease", "NXE0033744 EP4 agonist clinical trial", "NXE0033744 development stage IBD", "NXE744 EP4 agonist competitors IBD"], "anchors": ["NXE0033744", "NXE744"], "search_latency_ms": 572.2, "target": "drugs competing with NXE0033744 / NXE744: other agents in the same mechanistic class or for the same indication, as described in `objective`", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 353.8, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 8821, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.00037, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 4, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.5242, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Presentation title - investors.nxera.life", "url": "https://investors.nxera.life/common/pdf/Corporate_Presentation_2026Apr_EN_vF.pdf", "domain": "investors.nxera.life", "snippet": "NXE’744\nIBD\nPhase1\nUnder development in-house\nLicensing activities in progress\nIn discussions with multiple \npartners for a 2026 license deal\nPhase 1 completed successfully\nPhase 2 ready to initiate\n...\nNXE’744 showed early efficacy signals \nin an indomethacin challenge study\n...\nPhase1 completed\n✓\nPhase 2 ready\n...\nIBD\nAdvanced solid tumors\nDiscovery\nPhase 2\nPhase 2 ready\n...\nA first-in-class GI-targeted agent to promote mucosal healing in IBD\n•\nAll elements of the first-in-human study have now completed dosing in the clinic\n➢\nSAD/MAD studies are complete with no concerning adverse events and no systemic exposure observed\n➢\nGut restricted profile confirmed by high gut tissue concentrations measured following oral dosing\n➢\nUC patient cohort has completed dosing (n=6) with \ninterim analysis confirming high gut tissue concentration.\n➢\nIndomethacin challenge cohort complete, interim analysis complete with no need to increase subjects and final data read-out by March 2026\n➢\nPreliminary data analysis demonstrates a highly significant ~50% reduction in indomethacin induced permeability in the NXE’744 treatment group; these data confirm target engagement in the small intestine\n27\nNXE’744: EP4 agonist for IBD* – target engagement & Phase 2 ready\n...\nModulation of barrier homeostasis and inflammatory axis positions EP4 as an attractive MOA for IBD therapy\n...\nNXE0033744\nEP4 agonist\nSME\nInflammatory bowel disease\n...\nNXE0033744\nEP4 agonist\nInflammatory bowel diseases\nPh1\nUp to  220\n-\n2023-11-24\n2026-06-30\n2024-05-02\nISRCTN70080074", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-11", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.85, "evidence": 0.6167}, "jev_score": 0.5242, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Nxera Pharma : Annual Report Year ended 31 December 2024", "url": "https://www.marketscreener.com/news/nxera-pharma-annual-report-year-ended-31-december-2024-ce7c50dcd18dff20", "domain": "marketscreener.com", "snippet": "EP4 ag.\nNXE'744\nInflammatory Bowel Disease\n...\nOur three wholly owned clinical programs are also advancing as planned in Phase 1 development, with several important milestones expected over the next 12-18 months.\nThese programs are: NXE-149, which is targeting unmet needs in schizophrenia and is the subject of an option-to-license agreement with Boehringer Ingelheim; NXE-732, which is being investigated in solid tumors in collaboration with Cancer Research UK; and NXE-744, which offers a possible novel mechanism to treat inflammatory bowel diseases.\n...\nEP4 agonist\nNXE0033744\nThe first subject was dosed in a Phase 1 trial of NXE'744, an oral, gut-restricted EP4 agonist with potential to treat Inflammatory Bowel Disease (IBD).\nNXE'744 has been designed to bring clinical benefit by accelerating the healing of damaged epithelial mucosa and suppressing exaggerated gut inflammation, with minimal systemic exposure thereby providing significantly enhanced safety and efficacy.", "date": "2025-08-29", "last_updated": "2025-08-29", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.84, "evidence": 0.5633}, "jev_score": 0.4732, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "アニュアルレポート", "url": "https://investors.nxera.life/jp/common/pdf/20250829_Annual_Report_2024_JP.pdf", "domain": "investors.nxera.life", "snippet": "められています。NXE-744は、炎症性腸疾患の治療における新たなメカニズムを\n提供する可能性があります。\n...\n階、3品目がフェーズ2試験段階、1品目がフェーズ3段階にあります。\n...\n消化管で局所作用する炎症性腸疾患（以下「IBD」）の治療薬候補であるEP4受容体作動薬\nNXE0033744のフェーズ1試験で、最初の被験者への投与を実施。NXE0033744は、全身へ\nの影響を最小限に抑えながら、損傷した粘膜上皮の治癒を促進し、腸菅の炎症亢進を抑制\nすることで安全性および有効性を大幅に高め、治療効果を発揮するように設計されている", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-08", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.78, "evidence": 0.58}, "jev_score": 0.4524, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "[PDF] Corporate Presentation - Investors - Nxera Pharma", "url": "https://investors.nxera.life/common/pdf/Corporate_Presentation_2025Feb_EN_vF.pdf", "domain": "investors.nxera.life", "snippet": "NXE’744 (EP4 agonist)\n...\nPhase 2 study start in IBD\n...\nImmuno-oncology\nNXE0033744\nEP4 agonist\nSME\nInflammatory bowel disease\n...\n-\nNXE0033744\nEP4 agonist\nInflammatory bowel diseases\nPh1\nUp to  220\n-\n2023-11-24\n2026-06-30\n2024-02-15\nISRCTN70080074\n-", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-07-17", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.79, "evidence": 0.52}, "jev_score": 0.4108, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "[PDF] Presentation title - Nxera Pharma", "url": "https://investors.nxera.life/common/pdf/Corporate_Presentation_2025Jan_EN_v2.pdf", "domain": "investors.nxera.life", "snippet": "EP4 ag.\nNXE’744\nIBD\n...\nNXE’744 (EP4 agonist)\n...\nImmuno-oncology\nNXE0033744\nEP4 agonist\nSME\nInflammatory bowel disease\n...\nNXE0033744\nEP4 agonist\nInflammatory bowel diseases\nPh1\n-", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-10", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.85, "evidence": 0.47}, "jev_score": 0.3995, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Nxera Pharma Operational Highlights and Consolidated Results for ...", "url": "https://www.biospace.com/nxera-pharma-operational-highlights-and-consolidated-results-for-the-first-quarter-2024", "domain": "biospace.com", "snippet": "**Progress with in-house programs**\n- First subject dosed in a Phase 1 trial with NXE0033744, a novel, potent, selective and gut-restricted EP4 receptor agonist discovered by Nxera in development for the treatment of Inflammatory Bowel Disease (IBD)", "date": "2024-05-09", "last_updated": "2026-08-15", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.79, "evidence": 0.46}, "jev_score": 0.3634, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Nxera Pharma Operational Highlights and Consolidated Results ...", "url": "https://www.daiwair.co.jp/td_download.cgi?c=4565&i=2938504", "domain": "daiwair.co.jp", "snippet": "Progress with in-house programs \n...\n• First subject dosed in a Phase 1 trial with NXE0033744, a novel, potent, selective and gut-restricted \nEP4 receptor agonist in development for the treatment of Inflammatory Bowel Disease (IBD)", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-12-01", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.81, "evidence": 0.43}, "jev_score": 0.3483, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Consolidated Financial Results for the Nine months Ended September 30, 2024", "url": "https://finance.stockweather.co.jp/contents/dispPDF.aspx?disclosure=20241101509584", "domain": "finance.stockweather.co.jp", "snippet": "Biosciences (“Neurocrine”) that NBI-1117568, an oral selective muscarinic M4 receptor agonist then being advanced in Phase 2 clinical trials by Neurocrine for the treatment of schizophrenia and other neuropsychiatric disorders, had successfully completed a long-term preclinical toxicity program that meets US Food & Drug Administration (“FDA\") requirements to allow for safe, chronic\n...\nOn August 28, 2024, the Group noted the announcement by Neurocrine that NBI-1117568 had delivered positive topline results from its Phase 2 clinical study in adults with schizophrenia.\nThe\nNBI-1117568-SCZ2028 dose-finding study met its primary endpoint for the once-daily 20 mg dose.\n...\nOn March 21, 2024, the Group announced it had dosed the first subject in a Phase 1 trial evaluating its novel EP4 receptor agonist, NXE0033744 for the treatment of Inflammatory Bowel Disease\n(“IBD”), a therapeutic area where there remains significant unmet need for millions of people \nworldwide.\nNXE0033744 is a potent, selective and gut-restricted prostaglandin EP4 receptor agonist that has been uniquely designed to bring clinical benefit by accelerating the healing of damaged epithelial mucosa and suppressing exaggerated gut inflammation, with minimal systemic exposure to avoid adverse events.\nThis approach is widely recognized to promote deeper remission and offer better long-term clinical outcomes.\nNXE0033744 aims to address the significant unmet need of people with IBD that do not achieve satisfactory disease control.\nCurrent treatments for IBD typically achieve remission rates of less than 25%, and the safety profiles of these drugs mean that they require careful monitoring.\nThe Phase 1 trial is a first-in-human randomized, double-blind study to assess the safety, pharmacokinetics and effects on pharmacodynamic biomarkers of single and multiple ascending doses of NXE0033744 in adult healthy volunteers and people with Crohn’s disease to generate proof of mechanism.\nThe trial will be conducted in the UK and initial data read-outs are anticipated from 2025.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-01-07", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.75, "evidence": 0.4167}, "jev_score": 0.3125, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Pipeline - Nxera Pharma", "url": "https://www.nxera.life/pipeline", "domain": "nxera.life", "snippet": "### EP4 agonist\nPhase 1\nRead More\nIndication\nInflammatory Bowel Disease\nModality\nSmall molecule\nCandidate\nNXE0033744", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-24", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.74, "evidence": 0.3633}, "jev_score": 0.2688, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "[PDF] Environment, Social & Governance Report - Investors", "url": "https://investors.nxera.life/common/pdf/20250829_ESG_Report_2024_EN.pdf", "domain": "investors.nxera.life", "snippet": "During 2024, we have progressed our EP4 agonist,\nNXE0033744, into a Phase 1 clinical trial for the treatment of patients with Inflammatory Bowel Disease\n(IBD).\nAlthough we didn’t progress any new assets into \npreclinical development during the year, our discovery pipeline continues to grow and we are constantly evaluating exciting targets where we believe we can", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-18", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.64, "evidence": 0.37}, "jev_score": 0.2368, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}], "total_ms": 926.0, "ts": "2026-10-06T12:18:13+00:00"}
